1	NVDA	yearly	2013	For NVIDIA, 2013 was a year defined by record-breaking financial performance and strategic pivots into mobile and cloud computing. The company reported record annual revenue of $4.28 billion for fiscal year 2013, representing a 7.1% increase over the previous year. Despite a slight 3.2% decrease in net income to $562.5 million, NVIDIA achieved a record cash position of $3.73 billion by year-end. A major financial milestone was the initiation of NVIDIA's first-ever quarterly cash dividend of $0.075 per share, signaling a new phase of returning value to shareholders. Operationally, the company restructured its financial reporting into two primary segments: GPU and Tegra Processor, reflecting its focus on both core graphics and the burgeoning mobile market. \n\nOn the development front, 2013 saw the launch of the Tegra 4, marketed as the world's fastest mobile system-on-a-chip (SoC), and the Tegra 4i, NVIDIA's first fully integrated 4G LTE mobile processor. The company also made significant moves in the gaming and data center sectors, announcing Project SHIELD—a unique handheld Android gaming device—and NVIDIA GRID, a cloud-based gaming technology designed to stream high-end graphics to any screen. Additionally, NVIDIA launched the Tesla K20 family of GPU accelerators, which powered 'Titan,' then the world's fastest open-science supercomputer.	In 2013, NVIDIA reached record annual revenue of $4.28 billion and initiated its first-ever quarterly dividend of $0.075 per share. Key developments included the launch of the Tegra 4 mobile processor line, the announcement of the Project SHIELD gaming device, and the introduction of NVIDIA GRID cloud technology. The company also restructured its financial reporting into two main segments: GPU and Tegra Processor.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Annual and Fourth Quarter Fiscal 2013 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Third Quarter Fiscal Year 2013 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'What Will 2013 Hold for NVIDIA? (The Motley Fool)', 'NVIDIA 2013 Annual Review (SEC Form 10-K)', "NVIDIA's revenue hits a record $1.20 billion for Q3 powered by Tegra 3 tablets (Engadget)"]	2026-01-29 15:53:09
2	NVDA	yearly	2022	In 2022, NVIDIA navigated a transformative year characterized by significant strategic pivots, record-breaking financial performance, and the introduction of foundational technologies for the AI era. A major corporate event occurred in February 2022 when NVIDIA and SoftBank Group officially announced the termination of the proposed $40 billion acquisition of Arm Limited due to insurmountable regulatory challenges. Consequently, NVIDIA recorded a $1.35 billion acquisition termination charge in its first quarter of fiscal year 2023. Financially, the company reached a major milestone in February by reporting record full-year fiscal 2022 revenue of $26.91 billion, a 61% increase from the previous year. This growth was fueled by record-high revenues across its Gaming, Data Center, and Professional Visualization platforms. In March 2022, during its GTC event, NVIDIA unveiled the Hopper architecture and the H100 GPU, which would later become the industry standard for training large language models. Throughout the year, NVIDIA's Data Center business continued to see explosive growth, with quarterly revenues reaching a record $3.75 billion in the first quarter (announced May 2022) and $3.81 billion in the second quarter (announced August 2022). However, the Gaming segment faced headwinds later in the year, with second-quarter revenue dropping 33% year-over-year to $2.04 billion as demand for consumer GPUs softened. To support its stock value during this volatility, the board of directors increased its share repurchase program to a total of $15 billion in May 2022.	The year 2022 was a pivotal period for NVIDIA, marked by the termination of its high-profile $40 billion acquisition of Arm Limited in February due to regulatory hurdles. Despite this, the company achieved record fiscal 2022 revenue of $26.91 billion and announced its next-generation Hopper architecture and H100 GPU in March, which became the benchmark for generative AI development. While the Data Center business reached new heights, with quarterly revenues surpassing $3.8 billion, the Gaming segment saw a significant decline by mid-year. In response to market shifts, NVIDIA also expanded its share repurchase program to $15 billion in May 2022.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-fourth-quarter-and-fiscal-2022', 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-first-quarter-fiscal-2023', 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2023', 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-and-softbank-group-announce-termination-of-nvidias-acquisition-of-arm-limited', 'https://www.hpcwire.com/2022/03/22/nvidia-launches-hopper-h100-gpu-new-dgxs-and-grace-megachips/', 'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1045810/000104581022000166/R9.htm']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
3	NVDA	yearly	2024	2024 was a year of unprecedented financial milestones and technological breakthroughs for NVIDIA, solidifying its position as the primary engine of the global AI revolution. On February 21, 2024, the company reported record-breaking results for its 2024 fiscal year, with total revenue reaching $60.9 billion, a 126% increase from the previous year. This growth was primarily fueled by the 'AI Data Center boom,' as quarterly Data Center revenue alone soared 409% year-over-year to $18.4 billion. In March 2024, at the GTC conference, NVIDIA announced its next-generation Blackwell Architecture, designed to be the successor to Hopper and capable of delivering up to 30x performance increases for AI inference. Financially, the company reached a historic 10-for-1 stock split in June 2024 to make share ownership more accessible to retail investors and employees. During this same month, NVIDIA's market capitalization surpassed $3 trillion, briefly making it the world's most valuable company as it overtook tech giants Apple and Microsoft. The company's exceptional year was further recognized in late 2024 when it was invited to join the Dow Jones Industrial Average. Throughout the year, NVIDIA maintained high profitability with gross margins exceeding 70%, driven by 'insane' demand for its AI chips like the H100 and the initial ramp-up of Blackwell.	In 2024, NVIDIA transformed from a graphics leader into the world's most valuable company, briefly surpassing a $3.3 trillion market cap. Key highlights included reporting record fiscal year revenue of $60.9 billion, announcing the high-performance Blackwell AI architecture, and executing a major 10-for-1 stock split in June. The year was defined by a massive 'AI Data Center boom,' which saw data center revenue triple as the company became a member of the Dow Jones Industrial Average.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf (Sources 87, 91, 92, 93, 94, 99)", 'NVIDIA Investor Relations (Q4 and Fiscal 2024 Results)', 'Nasdaq: NVIDIA path in AI and Blackwell Launch', 'The Motley Fool: NVIDIA $30 Billion Milestone and Dow Jones', 'Verified Investing: NVDA Stock Analysis and Market Cap Milestones', 'NordFX: NVIDIA 2024 Stock Split Breakdown']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
4	NVDA	yearly	1998	The year 1998 was a pivotal turning point for NVIDIA, transforming it from a struggling startup into a formidable market leader. Most notably, the company released the RIVA TNT (codenamed NV4) in August 1998, which was the industry's first multi-texturing 3D processor. This chip established NVIDIA as a serious competitor to then-leader 3dfx and cemented its reputation for high-performance graphics. Earlier that year, in January, NVIDIA shipped its one millionth RIVA 128 chip, and by March 1998, it formed a critical strategic partnership with Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company (TSMC) for chip production. Financially, NVIDIA filed for its initial public offering (IPO) in early 1998, though it ultimately delayed the listing until January 1999. The company also underwent a significant legal and corporate restructuring, reincorporating in Delaware in April 1998 and moving its headquarters to Santa Clara, California in July. Despite its growth, the year was marked by legal challenges as NVIDIA was hit with several patent infringement lawsuits from rivals like Silicon Graphics, S3, and 3dfx Interactive. By the end of 1998, NVIDIA was recognized by PC Magazine as the most influential 3D processor company and was elected to the OpenGL Architecture Review Board.	In 1998, NVIDIA transitioned from a niche player to a major industry force with the release of the RIVA TNT, the first multi-texturing 3D processor. The company reached key operational milestones, including shipping its millionth RIVA 128 chip and forming a long-term manufacturing partnership with TSMC. Corporately, NVIDIA filed for its IPO, reincorporated in Delaware, and moved its headquarters to Santa Clara. While facing multiple patent lawsuits from competitors, the company ended the year recognized as the most influential 3D processor firm in the market.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", '1998 - Nvidia Corporate Timeline (nvidia.com)', 'NVIDIA Corporation | Encyclopedia.com', 'NVIDIA Corporation | History, GPUs, & Artificial Intelligence | Britannica Money', 'RIVA TNT - Wikipedia', 'Annual Report on Form 10-K - SEC.gov']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
5	NVDA	yearly	1996	The year 1996 was a pivotal 'make-or-break' period for NVIDIA, characterized by a transition from a failed initial product to a desperate fight for survival. Following the commercial failure of its first chip, the NV1, which utilized non-standard quadratic texture mapping, the company found itself on the brink of bankruptcy. In a strategic shift in March 1996, NVIDIA began supporting Microsoft’s Direct3D API to align itself with the broader game developer community and industry standards. Financially, the company secured its second round of venture capital funding in June 1996 from Sequoia Capital and Sierra Ventures, providing a temporary buffer. However, the most critical development was the termination of a contract with Sega for the Dreamcast's graphics chip; because NVIDIA's technology was lagging behind the industry's shift toward triangle-based rendering, Jensen Huang convinced Sega’s CEO, Shoichiro Irimajiri, to invest $5 million in the company despite the project's cancellation. This 'lifeline' provided the company with roughly six months of operating capital. To survive, Huang executed a drastic restructuring in 1996, laying off more than half of the workforce—reducing headcount from 100 to 40 employees—to focus all remaining resources on developing the RIVA 128.	In 1996, NVIDIA faced a near-collapse following the failure of its NV1 chip and the shift toward industry-standard triangle rendering. To survive, CEO Jensen Huang laid off over 50% of the staff, reducing the team to 40 employees. The company secured a critical $5 million investment from Sega’s CEO, which served as a six-month lifeline to develop new technology. Strategically, NVIDIA also pivoted to support Microsoft’s Direct3D and closed a second round of venture capital funding to focus on the desktop PC graphics market.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'https://www.reddit.com/r/hardware/comments/1cwomld/nvidia_nearly_went_out_of_business_in_1996_trying/', 'https://www.nvidia.com/content/timeline/time_96.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nvidia', 'https://www.fundinguniverse.com/company-histories/nvidia-corporation-history/', 'https://quartr.com/insights/edge/the-story-of-jensen-huang-and-nvidia']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
6	NVDA	five_year	2013-2017	NVIDIA: 2013-2017 Timeline Summary\nBetween 2013 and 2017, NVIDIA underwent a massive transformation, evolving from a PC graphics specialist into a dominant force in mobile, automotive, and artificial intelligence.\n\n2013: Record Revenue and Strategic Pivots\nNVIDIA achieved record annual revenue of $4.28 billion and initiated its first-ever quarterly cash dividend. The company restructured into two main segments: GPU and Tegra Processor, highlighting a new focus on mobile computing. This year saw the launch of the Tegra 4 mobile line and the announcement of Project SHIELD, a handheld Android gaming device. Additionally, the company introduced NVIDIA GRID for cloud-based gaming.\n\n2014: Mobile Innovation and Maxwell Architecture\nThe company reported $4.13 billion in revenue and returned over $1 billion to shareholders through buybacks and dividends. At CES, NVIDIA unveiled the Tegra K1, a "super chip" featuring 192 cores, and introduced the Tegra K1 VCM for the automotive industry. This year also marked the debut of the power-efficient Maxwell GPU architecture with the GeForce GTX 750 series.\n\n2015: The Shift to AI and Automotive\nNVIDIA hit a new record revenue of $4.68 billion, driven by a strategic pivot toward deep learning and autonomous driving. Key developments included the launch of the DRIVE PX autonomous platform and the GeForce GTX TITAN X. The company also decided to exit the cellular modem market (Icera) to focus on its core growth areas in gaming, data centers, and automotive.\n\n2016: The Generative AI Era Begins\nFiscal revenue surpassed $5 billion as NVIDIA's stock price nearly tripled due to its growing dominance in AI. A major milestone occurred when CEO Jensen Huang delivered the first DGX-1 AI supercomputer to OpenAI. The company launched the Pascal architecture, powering the Tesla P100 and GTX 10-series GPUs, and secured a partnership with Volvo for self-driving technology.\n\n2017: Record Growth and Volta Architecture\n2017 was a landmark year with revenue jumping 38% to $6.91 billion. NVIDIA unveiled the Volta architecture and the Tesla V100, the first GPU to feature Tensor Cores specifically for AI acceleration. The company also released the GeForce GTX 1080 Ti and the Titan V, while expanding its automotive reach through a major partnership with Toyota.			2026-01-29 15:53:09
7	NVDA	yearly	2004	In 2004, NVIDIA navigated a year of significant technological leaps and strategic partnerships, tempered by intense competition in the desktop graphics market. Financially, the company reported revenue of $1.82 billion for its fiscal year 2004 (ending January 25, 2004), with a net income of $74.4 million. However, the middle of the year proved challenging; in August 2004, NVIDIA's stock price plummeted after the company reported a nearly 80% drop in quarterly profit, attributed to market share losses to rivals ATI and Intel. Despite these financial headwinds, 2004 was a landmark year for innovation. On April 14, NVIDIA launched the GeForce 6 series (NV40), which introduced industry-defining technologies such as SLI (Scalable Link Interface), PureVideo, and Shader Model 3.0 support. In October, the company expanded its chipset lineup with the nForce4 MCP for AMD64 environments. Strategically, NVIDIA secured its future in the gaming and computing ecosystems by announcing a joint GPU development agreement with Sony for the next-generation PlayStation 3 on December 7 and signing a major multi-year cross-licensing and chipset agreement with Intel on November 19.	2004 was a pivotal year of high-stakes competition and major technical milestones for NVIDIA. The company launched the groundbreaking GeForce 6 series in April, introducing SLI and Shader Model 3.0, and announced a massive partnership with Sony in December to develop the graphics processor for the PlayStation 3. While fiscal year revenue reached $1.82 billion, the company faced a difficult summer with a sharp profit decline in August due to intense competition from ATI and Intel. The year concluded with a strategic multi-year licensing deal with Intel, strengthening NVIDIA's position in the chipset market.	['CNET: Nvidia profit plummets (Aug 2004)', 'SEC Form 10-K: NVIDIA Annual Report (Fiscal Year 2005)', 'Wikipedia: GeForce 6 series', 'Sony Interactive: Press Release - Sony and NVIDIA Joint GPU Development (Dec 2004)', 'NVIDIA Press Release: Quadro NVS 280 PCI (Jan 2004)', 'GameSpot: Nvidia reports mixed results for 2004 (Feb 2004)']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
8	NVDA	yearly	2018	The year 2018 was a pivotal and volatile period for NVIDIA, characterized by groundbreaking technological leaps and significant financial turbulence. In February 2018, the company reported record-breaking results for its fiscal year 2018 (ending January 28), with annual revenue reaching $9.71 billion, a 41% increase year-over-year, and GAAP earnings per share jumping 88% to $4.82. During this period, NVIDIA's data center business saw explosive growth of 133%, reaching $1.93 billion as AI adoption accelerated. \n\nTechnologically, 2018 marked the introduction of the Turing architecture, which NVIDIA hailed as the most significant innovation since the invention of the CUDA GPU in 2006. In August 2018, the company launched the GeForce RTX series, bringing real-time ray tracing and AI-enhanced graphics via Tensor Cores to the consumer market for the first time. NVIDIA also made strides in autonomous driving by announcing NVIDIA DRIVE, a functionally safe AI self-driving platform.\n\nDespite these milestones, the latter half of the year was marred by the "cryptocurrency hangover". As the crypto market crashed, demand for GPUs used in mining plummeted, leading to an oversupply of mid-range Pascal-based cards in the sales channel. This inventory glut caused NVIDIA to miss revenue expectations in its third-quarter report on November 15, 2018, and issue weak fourth-quarter guidance. Consequently, NVIDIA's stock price, which had reached record highs earlier in the year, crashed more than 30% in the final months of 2018, erasing billions in market value.	In 2018, NVIDIA experienced both record growth and a sharp market correction. The company achieved record fiscal year revenue of $9.71 billion, driven by a 133% surge in its data center business. The year was highlighted by the launch of the Turing architecture and the GeForce RTX series, which introduced real-time ray tracing to the public. However, the late 2018 "cryptocurrency hangover" led to a massive oversupply of GPUs, causing the stock to plunge over 30% in the fourth quarter after the company missed financial projections.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal 2018 (Feb 2018)', '2018 NVIDIA CORPORATION ANNUAL REVIEW', 'History of Nvidia: Company timeline and facts - TheStreet', 'Why NVIDIA Stock Plunged 31% in 2018 - The Motley Fool', "Why NVIDIA's Stock Crashed - The Motley Fool (Nov 2018)", 'Why NVIDIA Stock Dropped 22% in November - Nasdaq']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
9	NVDA	yearly	2011	In 2011, NVIDIA experienced a pivotal year defined by strategic shifts toward mobile computing and the early foundations of the AI revolution. Financially, the company reported a record fiscal year revenue of $3.54 billion for the period ending January 30, 2011. A landmark financial event was the $1.5 billion cross-licensing agreement signed with Intel in January 2011, which resolved long-standing legal disputes and provided NVIDIA with a significant multi-year revenue stream. The company's stock (NVDA) showed volatility, peaking near $20.00 in May before retracing to approximately $13.86 by December. On the development front, NVIDIA made a major push into mobile by acquiring Icera, a mobile communications equipment maker, for $367 million to bolster its Tegra processor line. This year also saw the announcement of 'Project Denver,' a custom high-performance ARM-based CPU designed for PCs and servers. In the graphics market, the company expanded its Fermi architecture with the launch of the GeForce GTX 580, 570, and 560 Ti. Critically for its future, 2011 was the year AI researchers at institutions like Google and Stanford began utilizing NVIDIA GPUs for deep learning, marking a seismic shift in the technology's application. Additionally, the Tianhe-1A, then the world’s fastest supercomputer, relied on thousands of NVIDIA GPUs for its massive processing power.	2011 was a transformative year for NVIDIA, highlighted by a $1.5 billion cross-licensing settlement with Intel and the $367 million acquisition of mobile chipmaker Icera. The company reported record fiscal year revenue of $3.54 billion. Key technological milestones included the announcement of 'Project Denver' (a custom ARM-based CPU), the launch of the Tegra 2 mobile processor, and the continued expansion of the Fermi GPU architecture with the GeForce GTX 580. Notably, this year also saw the first significant adoption of NVIDIA GPUs by AI researchers for deep learning applications.	['NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal Year 2011 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Annual and Fourth Quarter Fiscal 2012 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'Britannica Money: NVIDIA Corporation | History, GPUs, & Artificial Intelligence', 'NVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) - Price History - Digrin', 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Third Quarter Fiscal 2011 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'NVIDIA CORPORATION 2011 Annual Review', "NVIDIA Blog: Game-Changer: How the World's First GPU Leveled Up Gaming and Ignited the AI Era"]	2026-01-29 15:53:09
10	NVDA	yearly	2008	In 2008, NVIDIA experienced a year of extreme highs and lows, transitioning from a period of record-breaking financial success to facing significant market and technical challenges. Early in the year, the company was lauded as the 'Company of the Year' by Forbes. Financially, NVIDIA reported a record annual revenue of $4.10 billion for its 2008 fiscal year (ending January 27, 2008), a 34% increase driven by strong demand for GPUs across all segments. However, as the year progressed, the global financial crisis and internal product issues took a toll. In July 2008, the company took a significant one-time charge of approximately $196 million (often cited as $200 million) for a multi-chip write-down due to abnormal failure rates in certain mobile chipsets and GPUs. This defect, often referred to as the 'bump' problem, caused significant reputational and financial damage. On the development front, NVIDIA officially introduced its Tesla architecture, which was specifically designed to pivot the company toward high-performance computing (HPC) and data centers. This included the launch of the Tesla Personal Supercomputer, which offered cluster-level performance in a desktop form factor. The company also expanded its consumer lineup with the GeForce 9 series and GeForce 8800 family. By the end of 2008, NVIDIA's stock price had fallen approximately 75% from its early-year levels, reflecting both the broader economic downturn and the impact of its hardware defects.	NVIDIA’s 2008 was defined by a pivot toward data center computing and a major hardware crisis. The company reported a then-record $4.1 billion in annual revenue early in the year and was named Forbes 'Company of the Year'. However, it was forced to take a $200 million write-down in July 2008 due to widespread manufacturing defects in mobile GPUs. Despite these setbacks, NVIDIA formally introduced the Tesla architecture for high-performance computing and launched the Tesla Personal Supercomputer, marking a critical step in its evolution beyond gaming.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'NVIDIA Press Release: Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal Year 2009 (Feb 2009)', 'HEXUS/TechPowerUp: NVIDIA Reports Record Results for Fiscal Year 2008 (Feb 2008)', 'Wikipedia: NVIDIA Corporate Timeline and Tesla Microarchitecture', 'NVIDIA Datasheet: Tesla Personal Supercomputer (Dec 2008)', 'IEEE Micro: NVIDIA Tesla: A Unified Graphics and Computing Architecture (March 2008)', 'Financial Data: NVIDIA Historical Stock Performance 2008']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
11	NVDA	yearly	1994	In 1994, NVIDIA focused on transitioning from a conceptual startup into a functional semiconductor design firm, primarily through strategic partnerships and the research and development of its first silicon. While the company remained in a pre-revenue stage, its activities were critical for its future viability. \n\nKey developments in 1994 included:\n* **Strategic Manufacturing Partnership**: NVIDIA entered into a pivotal strategic partnership with SGS-Thomson Microelectronics (now STMicroelectronics)[cite: 4, 5]. [cite_start]This agreement was essential as NVIDIA was a 'fabless' company, and SGS-Thomson provided the manufacturing capabilities needed to produce NVIDIA’s upcoming chip designs [cite: 4][cite_start].\n* **NV1 Development**: The year was dedicated to the intensive R&D of the NV1 (initially known by the SGS-Thomson designation STG2000), a multi-function PCI card intended to handle 2D/3D graphics, audio, and game controller input [cite: 2, 6][cite_start].\n* **Technological Choice**: The engineering team committed to using quadratic texture mapping for their 3D rendering, a non-standard approach at the time that prioritized curved surfaces over the industry-standard triangles [cite: 61, 3][cite_start].\n* **Operational Growth**: Having secured its initial venture capital in late 1993, the company spent 1994 expanding its engineering talent pool and establishing its corporate headquarters in Sunnyvale, California, to prepare for its first product launch[cite: 1, 5].	1994 was a foundational year of research and development for NVIDIA. [cite_start]The company secured a critical manufacturing partnership with SGS-Thomson Microelectronics to produce its future chips[cite: 4, 5]. [cite_start]Most of the year was spent designing the NV1, its first multimedia accelerator, using a unique quadratic texture mapping architecture[cite: 61, 2, 6]. [cite_start]Operationally, the company expanded its engineering team and solidified its presence in Sunnyvale as it moved toward its 1995 product debut[cite: 1, 5].	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/about-nvidia/corporate-timeline/', 'https://www.computinghistory.org.uk/det/32763/Nvidia-is-founded/', 'https://www.britannica.com/money/NVIDIA-Corporation', 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/stmicroelectronics-and-nvidia-announce-strategic-relationship', 'https://www.thestreet.com/technology/nvidia-company-history-timeline', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NV1']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
12	NVDA	five_year	1993-1997	The first five years of NVIDIA (1993–1997) represent a volatile "make-or-break" era, characterized by a humble start in a diner, a brush with bankruptcy, and a last-minute technological pivot that saved the company.\n\nThe Foundation: From a Diner to Survival (1993–1997)\n1993: Humble Genesis NVIDIA was founded on April 5, 1993, by Jensen Huang, Chris Malachowsky, and Curtis Priem with $40,000 in initial capital. The vision for graphics-based computing was famously hashed out at a Denny’s restaurant in San Jose, leading to $20 million in venture funding from firms like Sequoia Capital.\n\n1994: Building the "Fabless" Model As a fabless semiconductor company, NVIDIA spent 1994 forming a critical manufacturing partnership with SGS-Thomson Microelectronics to produce its future chips. The year was dedicated to the research and development of its first multimedia card, the NV1.\n\n1995: The NV1 Launch and Failure The company released the NV1, an all-in-one card for graphics, audio, and gaming. Despite a strategic partnership with Sega to port Saturn games to PC, the NV1 was a commercial failure because it used non-standard "quadratic texture mapping" instead of the industry-standard triangles.\n\n1996: Near-Bankruptcy and a $5M Lifeline Facing collapse, CEO Jensen Huang executed a drastic restructuring, laying off over half the workforce to reduce headcount to 40 employees. A critical $5 million investment from Sega’s CEO—provided even after NVIDIA's console project was canceled—gave the company a six-month financial lifeline to pivot toward Microsoft’s Direct3D standards.\n\n1997: Redemption with RIVA 128 NVIDIA launched the RIVA 128, its first successful 128-bit Direct3D processor. It sold over one million units in just four months, effectively saving the company from being "30 days from going out of business" and establishing relationships with major OEMs like Dell and Gateway.\n\nNVIDIA Early Financials | Year | Key Event | Financial Status | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | 1993 | Founding | $40,000 initial capital | | 1995 | NV1 Release | $10M development cost | | 1996 | Pivot | $5M "lifeline" investment | | 1997 | RIVA 128 | 1M+ units sold; near-bankruptcy avoided |			2026-01-29 15:53:09
13	NVDA	master	entire_history_summary	NVIDIA has evolved from a 1993 startup conceived in a San Jose diner into the primary engine of the global artificial intelligence revolution. Initially a specialist in PC graphics, the company nearly faced bankruptcy in its early years before finding redemption with the RIVA 128 and the 1999 launch of the GeForce 256, marketed as the world’s first GPU. A critical strategic pivot occurred in 2006 with the introduction of the CUDA architecture, which transformed the GPU from a graphics chip into a general-purpose processor capable of massive parallel computing. This breakthrough effectively laid the groundwork for the modern AI era, as GPUs proved to be the ideal hardware for deep learning and large-scale data processing.\n\nCurrent Strategic Direction\nToday, NVIDIA is a multi-trillion-dollar powerhouse positioned as the foundational infrastructure for the "AI Industrial Revolution". The company achieved historic milestones in 2025, reaching a $5 trillion market capitalization and record revenues of $130.5 billion, driven by the massive rollout of the Blackwell GPU architecture. As of early 2026, NVIDIA’s trajectory is focused on "physical AI" and edge computing, marked by the launch of the Rubin platform. This new platform, featuring the Rubin GPU and Vera CPU, aims to slash AI inference costs by 10x, signaling a shift from merely building AI "brains" to enabling economy-wide deployment in robotics, autonomous systems, and enterprise agents.			2026-01-29 15:53:09
14	NVDA	yearly	2017	In 2017, NVIDIA experienced a landmark year characterized by record-breaking financial performance and the introduction of groundbreaking GPU architecture that solidified its position in the AI and high-performance computing (HPC) sectors. \n\n### Financial Milestones\n* **Record Annual Revenue**: In February 2017, NVIDIA reported record revenue for fiscal year 2017 of $6.91 billion, a 38% increase from the previous year. \n* **Quarterly Growth**: The company achieved record quarterly revenue multiple times throughout the year, including $2.17 billion in Q4 of fiscal 2017 (reported in February) and $2.23 billion in Q2 of fiscal 2018 (reported in August). \n* **Stock Performance**: NVIDIA's stock price saw substantial growth, starting the year at approximately $2.55 and ending at roughly $4.84 (adjusted for splits), representing an increase of nearly 90%. \n* **Shareholder Returns**: During fiscal 2017, the company returned $1.00 billion to shareholders through share repurchases ($739 million) and cash dividends ($261 million). \n\n### Technological and Product Developments\n* **Volta Architecture**: In May 2017, NVIDIA unveiled the Volta GPU architecture, designed specifically to drive the next wave of AI and HPC. \n* **Tesla V100**: Launched alongside Volta, the Tesla V100 data center GPU was the first to feature Tensor Cores, delivering 120 teraflops of deep learning performance. \n* **Gaming Hardware**: NVIDIA released the GeForce GTX 1080 Ti in March 2017, followed by the flagship Titan Xp in April, which was marketed as the world's most powerful graphics card at the time. \n* **Titan V**: In December 2017, the company launched the Titan V, the first Volta-based GPU for PCs, featuring 21.1 billion transistors. \n* **Strategic Partnerships**: The company expanded its influence in autonomous driving, announcing that Toyota had adopted the NVIDIA DRIVE PX platform for its autonomous vehicles. \n* **Software and Simulation**: NVIDIA introduced the Isaac robot simulator for training autonomous machines in virtual environments and Project Holodeck for collaborative, photorealistic VR.	2017 was a transformative year for NVIDIA, defined by record financial success and the launch of the Volta architecture. The company reported a record annual revenue of $6.91 billion for fiscal year 2017, while its stock price nearly doubled over the calendar year. Key technological highlights included the debut of the Tesla V100 GPU—the first to utilize Tensor Cores for AI acceleration—and the release of high-performance consumer cards like the GeForce GTX 1080 Ti and Titan V. Additionally, NVIDIA strengthened its presence in the automotive industry through a major partnership with Toyota for its DRIVE PX autonomous platform.	['NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal 2017 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Second Quarter Fiscal 2018 (sec.gov)', 'NVIDIA Launches Revolutionary Volta GPU Platform, Fueling Next Era of AI and High Performance Computing (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'Supercharged Computing for the da Vincis and Einsteins of our time - NVIDIA (images.nvidia.com)', "Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) Launches 'World's Most Powerful' Graphics Card (investorplace.com)", 'Volta (microarchitecture) - Wikipedia', 'NVIDIA - 27 Year Stock Price History | NVDA - Macrotrends']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
108	AMD	yearly	2009	2009 was a year of financial stabilization and product recovery for AMD, with the GlobalFoundries spin-off completing in March 2009 and removing the capital expenditure and depreciation burden of fab ownership from AMD's balance sheet. Annual revenue came in at approximately $5.4 billion—a modest decline from 2008 despite the global financial crisis—and the company significantly reduced its operating losses compared to the prior two years. The lighter balance sheet and renewed operational focus under CEO Dirk Meyer allowed AMD to begin rebuilding profitability.\n\nThe headline CPU product of 2009 was the Phenom II, launched in January, which corrected the TLB bug that had plagued the original Phenom/Barcelona and provided significant IPC improvements through higher clock speeds and refined manufacturing on 45nm process technology. The Phenom II X4 955 in particular competed credibly against Intel's Core 2 Quad at comparable price points, though Intel's newer Nehalem Core i7 architecture (launched late 2008) was clearly superior in performance. AMD's GPU business performed strongly, with the Radeon HD 4800 series continuing to earn praise as best-in-class value, and AMD launched the Radeon HD 5000 series (Cypress architecture) in Q4 2009—the first GPU family to support DirectX 11. The HD 5870, AMD's flagship, launched ahead of any competing DirectX 11 product from NVIDIA, giving AMD a brief but commercially significant performance and feature lead in graphics. AMD also introduced its Fusion APU (Accelerated Processing Unit) concept—combining CPU and GPU on a single die—which was under active development and previewed to industry partners as AMD's strategic direction for the future.	2009 saw AMD stabilize financially following the GlobalFoundries spin-off in March, with revenue of approximately $5.4 billion and significantly reduced losses. The Phenom II corrected its predecessor's critical TLB bug, and the Radeon HD 5000 series launched as the world's first DirectX 11 GPU family—ahead of NVIDIA—giving AMD a graphics leadership moment while the Fusion APU concept previewed AMD's integrated CPU+GPU future.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations", "AMD - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD: A Complete Overview - Microchip USA", "AMD's 2010-2011 Roadmaps: Llano APU - AnandTech", "Radeon HD 5000 Series - Wikipedia"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
15	NVDA	yearly	2019	In 2019, NVIDIA navigated significant financial volatility and strategic expansion. The year began with a downward revision of financial guidance in January, where the company lowered its fourth-quarter revenue forecast from $2.70 billion to $2.20 billion due to a "turbulent" macro-economic environment and excess gaming inventory following the cryptocurrency mining boom. Despite these headwinds, NVIDIA reported record full-year fiscal 2019 revenue of $11.72 billion on February 14, representing a 21% increase from the previous year. A major milestone occurred on March 11, 2019, when NVIDIA announced its intent to acquire Mellanox Technologies for approximately $6.9 billion in cash, a move designed to strengthen its data center and high-performance computing (HPC) footprint. Throughout the year, the company expanded its technological reach by launching RTX Studio laptops in May for creative professionals and announcing Minecraft RTX in August to bring real-time ray tracing to the gaming mainstream. While trade tensions with China caused a 25.2% stock decline in May, the company's shares recovered strongly, ending the year with a 76% annual return and a stock price rise from approximately $3.41 to $5.88 (split-adjusted).	NVIDIA's 2019 was defined by its record fiscal year revenue of $11.72 billion and the strategic announcement of its $6.9 billion acquisition of Mellanox Technologies. Despite early-year guidance cuts and trade-related market volatility, the company successfully expanded its ecosystem with the introduction of RTX Studio laptops and ray-tracing for Minecraft. These developments fueled a strong recovery, with the stock delivering a 76% annual return by year-end.	['https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-fourth-quarter-and-fiscal-2019', 'https://s201.q4cdn.com/141608511/files/doc_financials/annual/2019/NVIDIA-2019-Annual-Report.pdf', 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-updates-financial-guidance-