16	NVDA	yearly	2001	In 2001, NVIDIA solidified its position as a market leader through aggressive consolidation, major index inclusions, and significant product launches. A defining moment was the acquisition of the assets of its former rival, 3dfx Interactive, which effectively consolidated the graphics market. Financially, NVIDIA reached a major milestone by becoming the fastest semiconductor company to hit $1 billion in annual revenue. The company's growing market influence was recognized by Wall Street as it was added to the Nasdaq-100 Index in May and the S&P 500 Index in November. Additionally, NVIDIA executed a 2-for-1 stock split in September 2001.\n\nOn the development front, 2001 was a year of architectural breakthroughs and strategic partnerships. In February, NVIDIA introduced the GeForce3, the industry's first programmable GPU and the first to be compatible with DirectX 8. This technology powered the graphics for Microsoft’s Xbox console and Apple’s Power Mac G4 'personal supercomputer'. In June, the company entered the integrated graphics market with the introduction of its nForce Platform Processing Architecture. Other notable releases included the Quadro2 Go, the industry's first mobile workstation GPU, and the GeForce Titanium series. Toward the end of the year, however, the company faced an internal investigation following an SEC inquiry into its accounting practices and alleged insider trading by certain employees.	In 2001, NVIDIA achieved record financial growth, becoming the fastest semiconductor company to reach $1 billion in revenue. Key developments included the acquisition of rival 3dfx Interactive's assets, which consolidated the graphics market, and the company's first-time addition to the S&P 500 and Nasdaq-100 indices. Technically, NVIDIA launched the GeForce3—the first programmable GPU—and expanded its reach into game consoles like the Xbox and professional Apple workstations. The company also executed a 2-for-1 stock split in September.	NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf; 2001 - Nvidia Corporate Timeline (nvidia.com); History of Nvidia: Company timeline and facts - TheStreet; History of NVIDIA: Company and Stock - SmartAsset; Nvidia's 2001 Results Exceed Expectations - Game Developer; Nvidia's stock drops 12% on news of probe - SFGATE; Investopedia - If You Had Invested in NVIDIA Right After its IPO; Nasdaq - Nvidia Executes Its 10-for-1 Stock Split; 'The Evolution of NVIDIA Chips' by nehalmr (Medium).	2026-01-29 15:53:09
17	NVDA	five_year	2008-2012	\nBetween 2008 and 2012, NVIDIA underwent a high-stakes transformation, evolving from a graphics specialist into a high-performance computing and AI pioneer.\n\n2008: Record Success and Technical Crisis The year began with NVIDIA reporting a record $4.10 billion in annual revenue and being named Forbes "Company of the Year". However, the company soon faced a major hardware crisis known as the "bump" problem—manufacturing defects in mobile GPUs—which led to a $196 million write-down. By the end of 2008, the stock price had plummeted approximately 75% due to the global financial crisis and these internal hardware issues. Despite the turmoil, NVIDIA introduced the Tesla architecture, its first major pivot toward the data center market.\n\n2009: Strategic Transition and "Fermi" Unveiling Finances remained under pressure as NVIDIA reported a $68 million net loss for fiscal year 2010. Strategically, the company doubled down on GPU computing by unveiling the Fermi architecture, the first GPU designed specifically for general-purpose parallel processing. This year also marked NVIDIA’s entry into the mobile device market with Tegra processors powering the Microsoft Zune HD.\n\n2010: Recovery and Industry Expansion NVIDIA stabilized with $3.3 billion in revenue as it focused on the broad adoption of its CUDA architecture. The company launched the Fermi-based GeForce GTX 400 series and the Tegra 2 mobile processor, which secured a major automotive partnership with Audi. This period solidified NVIDIA's intent to target new industries like high-volume data storage and early-stage AI research.\n\n2011: Landmark Settlements and Mobile Growth Revenue grew to $3.54 billion, bolstered by a historic $1.5 billion cross-licensing settlement with Intel that resolved long-standing legal disputes. To strengthen its mobile capabilities, NVIDIA acquired Icera for $367 million and announced "Project Denver". Crucially, 2011 saw the first major wave of AI researchers using NVIDIA GPUs for deep learning, setting the stage for the company's future dominance.\n\n2012: The AI Breakthrough and Shareholder Returns NVIDIA reached a new revenue record of $4.00 billion and initiated its first-ever quarterly cash dividend. This year is widely considered the start of the modern AI era: the AlexNet neural network won the ImageNet competition using NVIDIA GPUs, proving they were the ideal hardware for deep learning. The company also launched the highly efficient Kepler architecture and the VGX cloud virtualization platform.			2026-01-29 15:53:09
18	NVDA	yearly	2023	In 2023, NVIDIA underwent a historic financial transformation, evolving from a leading semiconductor firm into a multi-trillion-dollar backbone of the global artificial intelligence infrastructure. The year was defined by a surge in generative AI demand, catalyzed by the popularity of ChatGPT, which led to unprecedented financial growth and market valuation. \n\nKey financial and corporate milestones in 2023 included:\n* **Market Capitalization**: In May 2023, NVIDIA reached a $1 trillion market capitalization milestone, driven by soaring demand for its AI-specialized chips. By the end of the year, its stock had surged approximately 239%.\n* **Revenue Growth**: For the fiscal year ending in early 2024 (largely reflecting 2023 performance), total revenue reached $60.9 billion, a massive increase from $27 billion in the previous fiscal year. Quarterly revenue essentially tripled over the course of the year, with Data Center revenue alone rising 217% to a record $47.5 billion.\n* **H100 Dominance**: The H100 GPU, based on the Hopper architecture, became the industry 'gold standard' for training Large Language Models (LLMs). NVIDIA was estimated to ship approximately 550,000 H100 units throughout 2023 to meet global demand from major tech firms and sovereign nations.\n* **Strategic Partnerships**: NVIDIA expanded its influence through high-profile collaborations, including a 10-year agreement with Microsoft to bring Xbox PC games to the GeForce NOW cloud service. It also partnered with Foxconn to develop automated vehicle platforms and with ServiceNow to integrate generative AI into enterprise IT.\n* **Product Developments**: The company launched the RTX 40 Series for laptops and the GeForce RTX 4070 Ti, introducing the Ada Lovelace architecture and DLSS 3 technology to the consumer market. Additionally, NVIDIA introduced the DGX Cloud service to provide enterprises with instant access to AI supercomputing.	2023 was a landmark year for NVIDIA, highlighted by its ascent to a $1 trillion market capitalization in May as demand for generative AI chips skyrocketed. Driven by the 'AI Data Center boom' and the dominance of its H100 GPU architecture, the company's revenue for the fiscal year climbed to $60.9 billion. Beyond finance, NVIDIA secured strategic partnerships with Microsoft and Foxconn, while expanding its consumer presence with the RTX 40 series and DLSS 3 technology.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-fourth-quarter-and-fiscal-2023', 'https://finimize.com/content/nvidia-blew-the-doors-off-2023-heres-what-next-year-might-be-like', 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/_gallery/download_pdf/65d669a33d63329bbf62672a/', 'https://it.mexem.com/blog/nvidias-financial-surge-a-comprehensive-performance-update', 'https://investor.nvidia.com/news/press-release-details/2024/NVIDIA-Announces-Financial-Results-for-Fourth-Quarter-and-Fiscal-2024/', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2023/6/1/visualising-nvidias-rise-to-the-1-trillion-market-value-club', 'https://www.nasdaq.com/articles/nvidia-has-gained-over-$1-trillion-in-market-cap-in-2024.-can-it-do-it-again-to-surpass', 'https://investor.nvidia.com/news/press-release-details/2023/NVIDIA-Announces-Financial-Results-for-First-Quarter-Fiscal-2024/default.aspx', 'https://wccftech.com/nvidia-expects-to-ship-550000-h100-ai-gpus-in-2023-generating-over-25-billion/', 'https://www.tomshardware.com/news/nvidia-to-sell-550000-h100-compute-gpus-in-2023-report']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
19	NVDA	yearly	1997	The year 1997 was the most critical turning point in NVIDIA's history, as the company teetered on the edge of total collapse before achieving its first major commercial success. Early in the year, NVIDIA was nearly bankrupt, with founder Jensen Huang famously stating the company was '30 days from going out of business'. This financial crisis was precipitated by the failure of their first product (NV1) and a flawed technical direction. However, a strategic $5 million investment from Sega—provided even after Huang admitted NVIDIA's technology for their console project would not work—gave the company a vital six-month financial runway. With this lifeline, NVIDIA launched the RIVA 128 (NV3) on April 1, 1997. The RIVA 128 was the company's first 128-bit Direct3D processor and a massive hit, selling over one million units within just four months of its release. This success effectively saved the company from bankruptcy. By the end of 1997, NVIDIA had established key relationships with major OEMs including Dell, Gateway, and Micron, and was recognized by the Fabless Semiconductor Association as the 'World's Most Respected Private Fabless Semiconductor Company'.	In 1997, NVIDIA survived a near-bankruptcy crisis to launch its first successful product, the RIVA 128. After receiving a critical $5 million investment from Sega that provided a necessary financial lifeline, NVIDIA pivoted its technology to industry standards. The RIVA 128 launched in April and sold over one million units in four months, establishing NVIDIA as a serious competitor in the graphics market and securing partnerships with major computer manufacturers like Dell and Gateway.	NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf; NVIDIA Corporate Timeline (nvidia.com); Britannica Money (britannica.com); Wikipedia (Nvidia); Jon Peddie Research (jonpeddie.com); Moomoo/Finance Association Interview.	2026-01-29 15:53:09
20	NVDA	yearly	2000	In 2000, NVIDIA experienced a transformative year characterized by major strategic partnerships and market consolidation. \n\n### Financial Milestones\n* **Microsoft Xbox Contract**: NVIDIA was awarded the high-profile contract to develop graphics hardware for Microsoft's first Xbox game console. As part of this agreement, the company received a significant $200 million advance.\n* **First Stock Split**: Following a surge where the stock gained more than 700% after its 1999 IPO, NVIDIA executed its first-ever stock split (2-for-1) in June 2000.\n* **Sega Divestment**: After Sega's president, Shoichiro Irimajiri, left the company in 2000, Sega sold its NVIDIA stock for $15 million—a notable return on their earlier $5 million investment that had previously saved NVIDIA from bankruptcy.\n\n### Strategic & Technical Developments\n* **Acquisition of 3dfx**: In December 2000, NVIDIA reached a definitive agreement to acquire the intellectual property and assets of its former chief rival, 3dfx Interactive. This move effectively consolidated the consumer 3D graphics market under NVIDIA’s leadership.\n* **Product Innovation**: The company released the GeForce 2 GTS in the summer of 2000, continuing the momentum of the GeForce line introduced the previous year.\n* **Market Demand**: Throughout the year, NVIDIA's Graphics Processing Units (GPUs) saw soaring demand from the gaming community for their advanced image processing capabilities.	The year 2000 was a landmark period for NVIDIA, highlighted by three major events: receiving a **$200 million advance** from Microsoft for the **Xbox graphics contract**, executing its **first 2-for-1 stock split** in June, and reaching an agreement to **acquire the assets of its main rival, 3dfx Interactive**. Additionally, NVIDIA launched the **Georce 2 GTS** and saw Sega sell its stake in the company for $15 million.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf (Sources 69, 70)", 'Wikipedia: Nvidia (Source 1.1)', 'Baker City Herald: Nvidia company history & timeline (Source 1.2)', 'Britannica Money: NVIDIA Corporation (Source 1.3)', 'TheStreet: History of Nvidia (Source 1.6)', "Nasdaq: If You'd Invested $100 in Nvidia Stock 25 Years Ago (Source 2.2)", 'Investopedia: If You Had Invested in NVIDIA Right After its IPO (Source 2.4)']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
21	NVDA	five_year	2003-2007	Between 2003 and 2007, NVIDIA underwent a radical transformation, evolving from a PC graphics specialist into a diversified computing powerhouse. This era was defined by the birth of the CUDA architecture and a strategic pivot toward high-performance computing.\n\n2003–2004: Expansion and Market Turbulence\nIn 2003, NVIDIA aggressively expanded into the mobile and automotive sectors, acquiring MediaQ for $70 million to bolster handheld graphics and securing its first major mobile win with Apple's PowerBook line. By mid-2003, the company reached a market value of approximately $2.47 billion. However, 2004 brought financial challenges; despite achieving $1.82 billion in fiscal revenue, the company’s stock plummeted in August after a nearly 80% drop in quarterly profit due to fierce competition from ATI and Intel. On the product front, the launch of the GeForce 6 series introduced SLI technology, and a landmark agreement was signed with Sony to develop the graphics processor for the PlayStation 3.\n\n2005–2006: The CUDA Revolution and Strategic 'What-Ifs'\nThe company rebounded in 2005, with first-quarter net income tripling to $64.4 million and the acquisition of ULI Electronics to strengthen its chipset business. This year also saw a major missed opportunity for Intel, as its board rejected a proposed $20 billion acquisition of NVIDIA. 2006 marked a historic turning point with the unveiling of CUDA (Compute Unified Device Architecture), which transformed GPUs into general-purpose processors. This year also saw the launch of the GeForce 8800 GTX and the collapse of merger talks with AMD after CEO Jensen Huang reportedly insisted on leading the combined company.\n\n2007: Record Growth and Industry Leadership\nBy 2007, NVIDIA’s strategic bets began to pay off significantly. The company reported record annual revenue of $3.07 billion, a 29% increase driven by the GeForce 8 series and a 58% market share in notebook GPUs. This performance led Forbes to name NVIDIA its "Company of the Year". The year also saw the commercialization of the Tesla product line, the first specialized hardware for high-performance computing using the CUDA architecture, and the $357 million acquisition of PortalPlayer.			2026-01-29 15:53:09
22	NVDA	yearly	2021	In 2021, NVIDIA experienced a year of record-breaking financial performance and strategic technological shifts, solidifying its transition from a gaming-centric company to an accelerated computing leader. The year was defined by a massive surge in Data Center and Gaming revenue, the execution of a major stock split, and the announcement of its first-ever data center CPU. \n\n### Financial Milestones\n* **Record Revenue Growth**: For the fiscal year ended January 31, 2021, NVIDIA reported a record $16.68 billion in revenue, a 53% increase from the previous year. \n* **4-for-1 Stock Split**: In May 2021, NVIDIA announced a four-for-one stock split to make ownership more accessible to investors and employees; the split was officially executed on July 20, 2021.\n* **Quarterly Records**: Throughout calendar year 2021, the company consistently beat analyst expectations, with quarterly revenue climbing from $5.00 billion in January to over $7.1 billion by November.\n* **Market Segments**: Data Center revenue saw explosive growth, rising 124% to a record $6.70 billion for the fiscal year, while Gaming revenue grew 41% to $7.76 billion.\n\n### Key Technological Developments\n* **Grace CPU Announcement**: At GTC in April 2021, NVIDIA unveiled 'Grace,' its first ARM-based data center CPU designed for giant-scale AI and high-performance computing.\n* **RTX 30-Series Expansion**: The company expanded its Ampere architecture lineup with the launch of the GeForce RTX 3060, 3080 Ti, and 3070 Ti GPUs, as well as over 70 new laptop models.\n* **Omniverse Enterprise**: NVIDIA announced the general availability of Omniverse Enterprise, a platform for real-time 3D design collaboration and 'digital twin' simulations.\n* **BlueField DPUs**: The company introduced the BlueField-3 DPU (Data Processing Unit) to accelerate, isolate, and unload data center infrastructure tasks.\n* **Cryptomining Mitigations**: To address GPU shortages for gamers, NVIDIA launched Lite Hash Rate (LHR) versions of its GeForce GPUs in 2021 to limit their effectiveness for Ethereum mining.\n\n### Strategic Partnerships & Corporate Events\n* **Arm Acquisition Progress**: Throughout 2021, NVIDIA continued to pursue its $40 billion acquisition of Arm Limited, though the deal faced intensifying regulatory scrutiny in the U.S., UK, and EU.\n* **Automotive Integration**: NVIDIA DRIVE technology was integrated into the new Mercedes-Benz MBUX Hyperscreen, which debuted in the EQS model in the first half of 2021.	2021 was a landmark year for NVIDIA, characterized by record financial results and a major shift into the CPU market. The company reported a 53% increase in annual revenue to $16.68 billion, driven by massive demand for Gaming and Data Center chips. Key highlights included the execution of a 4-for-1 stock split in July, the unveiling of the 'Grace' data center CPU, and the expansion of the RTX 30-series GPU lineup. Despite these successes, the company's proposed $40 billion acquisition of Arm faced significant regulatory hurdles throughout the year.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", "Counterpoint Research: Gaming, Data Center Shine in NVIDIA's 2021 Show", 'NVIDIA Newsroom: Financial Results for Q3 and Q4 Fiscal 2021', 'Investopedia: Nvidia Earnings and Q4 FY 2021 Analysis', 'SmartAsset: History of NVIDIA Company and Stock', 'NVIDIA 2021 Annual Review', 'SEC.gov: NVIDIA Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements 2021']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
23	NVDA	yearly	1993	1993 marked the genesis of NVIDIA, a year characterized by its humble origin in a San Jose diner and the securing of its first major financial backing. On April 5, 1993, the company was officially founded by Jensen Huang, Chris Malachowsky, and Curtis Priem. The founders, who shared a vision that specialized graphics-based computing was the future, famously conceived the idea for the company while meeting at a Denny’s restaurant in San Jose, California. \n\nFinancial and organizational milestones in 1993 included:\n* **Founding Capital**: The company was launched with an initial $40,000 in seed capital. In a more granular breakdown of the startup phase, each of the three founders contributed $200 for their respective shares, totaling a $600 initial legal stake.\n* **Venture Funding**: Shortly after its founding, NVIDIA secured approximately $20 million in venture capital funding from prominent firms, including Sequoia Capital and Sutter Hill Ventures.\n* **Company Naming**: The founders originally considered the name 'NVision,' but upon discovering it was already in use by a paper manufacturer, they settled on 'NVIDIA'. The name is an amalgamation of 'NV' (representing 'next vision') and 'invidia' (the Latin word for envy).\n* **Incorporation and Location**: Jensen Huang signed the original articles of incorporation on April 5, 1993. While the idea was born in San Jose, the company established its first official operations in Sunnyvale, California.	NVIDIA was founded on April 5, 1993, by Jensen Huang, Chris Malachowsky, and Curtis Priem. Conceived at a Denny's diner in San Jose, the company was established with $40,000 in initial capital and soon secured $20 million in venture funding from firms like Sequoia Capital. Originally focused on bringing 3D graphics to the gaming and multimedia markets, the founders chose the name NVIDIA—a blend of 'NV' for 'next vision' and 'invidia' for envy—after their first choice, NVision, was unavailable.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'https://www.computinghistory.org.uk/det/32763/Nvidia-is-founded/', 'https://eqvista.com/nvidias-ownership-structure/', 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/bios/jensen-huang', 'https://www.thestreet.com/technology/nvidia-company-history-timeline', 'https://quartr.com/insights/edge/the-story-of-jensen-huang-and-nvidia', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jensen_Huang', 'https://pestel-analysis.com/blogs/brief-history/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nvidia', 'https://www.nasdaq.com/articles/nvidias-revenue-growth-over-last-25-years-will-blow-your-mind', 'https://www.dcfmodeling.com/blogs/history/nvda-history-mission-ownership', 'https://www.britannica.com/money/NVIDIA-Corporation', 'https://bakercityherald.com/2024/06/20/nvidia-company-history-timeline-from-gpu-maker-to-ai-leader/']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
24	NVDA	yearly	2010	In 2010, NVIDIA underwent a significant strategic and financial transition, focusing on the adoption of its CUDA architecture and expanding into mobile and high-performance computing. For the fiscal year ending January 31, 2010, the company reported total revenue of $3.3 billion. While this was a slight 3% decrease from the previous year, the company saw a strong recovery in its fourth quarter, where revenue rose 9% sequentially to $982.5 million. \n\nA cornerstone of 2010 was the launch of the Fermi architecture, which significantly enhanced NVIDIA's parallel computing capabilities and served as the foundation for the world's first computational GPUs. In March 2010, the company released the GeForce GTX 480 and 470 graphics cards based on Fermi, which featured 3 billion transistors and aimed to bridge the gap between gaming and high-performance GPU computing. Later that year, the more cost-effective GTX 460 was launched to critical acclaim. \n\nIn the mobile sector, NVIDIA unveiled the second-generation Tegra processor (Tegra 2) at CES in January 2010. Designed for the burgeoning tablet and smartphone market, Tegra 2 featured a dual-core ARM Cortex A9 processor and was selected by Audi to power its 2012 vehicle infotainment systems. Strategically, this period marked a definitive move toward industry-oriented practices, as NVIDIA began targeting entirely new software industries such as high-volume data storage and early-stage artificial intelligence research through its CUDA line.	In 2010, NVIDIA focused heavily on the adoption of its CUDA architecture, reporting a total fiscal year revenue of $3.3 billion. Key technological milestones included the launch of the Fermi architecture, which enhanced parallel computing for research and high-performance tasks, and the unveiling of the Tegra 2 mobile processor at CES to target the growing tablet market. The year also saw a major partnership announcement with Audi, which committed to using Tegra processors in its upcoming vehicle line.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal Year 2010 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)', 'History of Nvidia: Company timeline and facts (thestreet.com)', 'NVIDIA officially unveils Tegra 2 (m.hexus.net)', 'The 10 Biggest Chip Stories Of 2010 (crn.com)', 'History of NVIDIA: Company and Stock (smartasset.com)', 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Third Quarter Fiscal Year 2010 (nvidianews.nvidia.com)']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
25	NVDA	yearly	2003	In 2003, NVIDIA solidified its transition from a pure desktop graphics company to a diversified multimedia and platform provider, marked by key acquisitions and major entries into the mobile and automotive sectors. \n\n### **Financial Performance and Market Position**\nAs of July 25, 2003, NVIDIA's aggregate market value was approximately $2.47 billion. During this period, the company focused on expanding its market share beyond the high-end PC segment, reaching a discrete graphics card market share of over 60% by the early 2000s.\n\n### **Strategic Acquisitions**\n* **MediaQ Acquisition:** In August 2003, NVIDIA completed the acquisition of MediaQ, a leader in wireless graphics and multimedia technology. This move was designed to accelerate NVIDIA's entry into the handheld and smartphone market, integrating MediaQ's fabrication partnerships with UMC to continue producing media processors like the MQ1000 and MQ9000.\n* **Hybrid Graphics:** NVIDIA also acquired Hybrid Graphics in 2003 to further integrate its stack of GPU technologies, specifically targeting mobile and embedded systems.\n\n### **Key Product Launches and Tech Demos**\n* **GeForce4 Go and Apple:** In January 2003, NVIDIA's GeForce4 Go mobile GPU was designed into Apple's new PowerBook notebooks, marking the first time NVIDIA's mobile hardware was incorporated into an Apple mobile platform.\n* **nForce3 Platform:** In September 2003, NVIDIA announced the nForce3 platform processor, developed to complement the latest 64-bit CPUs and provide advanced networking and storage solutions for AMD64-based systems.\n* **GeForce FX Series:** The year saw the release of several iconic graphics demos for the GeForce FX series, including the 'Dawn,' 'Toys,' and 'Time Machine' demos, which showcased advanced shader and rendering capabilities.\n\n### **Major Partnerships and Specialized Projects**\n* **NASA Collaboration:** NVIDIA worked with NASA in 2003 to develop a photorealistic Mars simulation, demonstrating the utility of its GPUs for high-level scientific visualization.\n* **Automotive Sector:** The company became a major graphics chip supplier for Audi, beginning its long-standing presence in the automotive technology industry.	NVIDIA's 2003 was defined by strategic expansion into mobile and enterprise platforms. Key highlights include:\n* **Acquisitions**: Completed the $70 million purchase of MediaQ in August to boost handheld graphics capabilities and acquired Hybrid Graphics.\n* **Market Milestones**: Reached a market value of ~$2.47 billion by July and secured its first major mobile win with Apple for the PowerBook line.\n* **Product Development**: Launched the nForce3 platform for 64-bit computing and showcased the GeForce FX architecture through the 'Dawn' tech demo.\n* **Strategic Projects**: Partnered with NASA for a Mars simulation and established a supply relationship with Audi.	[{'index': '1.1', 'source_title': 'NVIDIA CORPORATION - IIS Windows Server', 'url': 'http://media.corporate-ir.net/media_files/NSD/NVDA/reports/10k_04.pdf'}, {'index': '1.2', 'source_title': 'Annual Report on Form 10-K - SEC.gov', 'url': 'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1045810/000104596903001196/d10k.htm'}, {'index': '1.3', 'source_title': 'NVIDIA Corporation | History, GPUs, & Artificial Intelligence | Britannica Money', 'url': 'https://www.britannica.com/money/NVIDIA-Corporation'}, {'index': '1.6', 'source_title': 'ANNUAL REPORT ON FORM 10-K - SEC.gov', 'url': 'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1045810/000104581005000008/annualreport.htm'}, {'index': '3.1', 'source_title': 'Nvidia signs UMC as chip producer - The Register', 'url': 'https://www.theregister.com/2003/08/22/nvidia_signs_umc_as_chip/'}, {'index': '3.2', 'source_title': '(PDF) Business Analysis: A Comparative Analysis of AMD and NVIDIA - ResearchGate', 'url': 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387718779_Business_Analysis_A_Comparative_Analysis_of_AMD_and_NVIDIA'}, {'index': '3.4', 'source_title': '7035841994202230titlenerd | PDF | Graphics Processing Unit | Mergers And Acquisitions', 'url': 'https://www.scribd.com/document/920368860/7036610-1187747406-7035841994202230Titlenerd'}, {'index': '4.4', 'source_title': 'Evolution of NVIDIA Tech Demos 1998-2025 - YouTube', 'url': 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DfWSJvKFMPE'}]	2026-01-29 15:53:09
26	NVDA	yearly	2025	The year 2025 marked a historic epoch for NVIDIA as it solidified its position as the engine of the global AI economy. Financially, the company achieved unprecedented heights, reporting a record full-year revenue of $130.5 billion for Fiscal Year 2025, representing a 114% increase year-over-year. This growth was primarily driven by the Data Center segment, which saw annual revenue rise to $115.2 billion. In the public markets, NVIDIA became the first company in history to surpass the $4 trillion market capitalization milestone on July 9, 2025, and later crossed the $5 trillion threshold in late October, briefly eclipsing both Apple and Microsoft as the world’s most valuable company.\n\nTechnological development was headlined by the massive rollout of the Blackwell architecture. By May 2025, NVIDIA reported over 3.6 million orders for Blackwell GPUs from major cloud providers like Amazon, Google, Microsoft, and Oracle. At CES 2025, the company introduced the GeForce RTX 50 Series, including the flagship RTX 5090, which featured 92 billion transistors and specialized AI capabilities for consumer PCs. Simultaneously, NVIDIA launched 'Project DIGITS' (later branded as DGX Spark), a desktop AI supercomputer designed to allow developers to train large language models locally. In a significant strategic move to dominate AI inferencing, NVIDIA announced its largest acquisition to date in late 2025: a $20 billion cash deal for the startup Groq, aimed at integrating low-latency processing into its AI factory architecture.	In 2025, NVIDIA reached a pinnacle of financial and technological dominance, becoming the first company to achieve $4 trillion and $5 trillion market capitalizations. The company reported record fiscal year revenue of $130.5 billion, a 114% surge driven by the explosive demand for AI data center hardware. Key developments included the full-scale launch of the Blackwell GPU architecture, the introduction of the GeForce RTX 50 series for consumers, and the landmark $20 billion acquisition of inferencing specialist Groq.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf (Sources 95, 96, 99)", 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal 2025 (Source 1.2, 2.3)', 'NVIDIA (NASDAQ: NVDA) Price Prediction and Forecast 2026-2030 (Source 1.3)', 'Nvidia - Wikipedia (Source 1.4)', 'Nvidia, in the Last Days of 2025, Just Made a Game-Changing Move | Nasdaq (Source 1.5)', 'Nvidia becomes first US company to reach $4 trillion market cap - Al Jazeera (Source 2.1)', "Nvidia's Market Cap Hit $4 Trillion for the First Time Today - Investopedia (Source 2.4)", "Nvidia's New AI Releases at CES 2025: Explored - AI Magazine (Source 3.1)", 'All the announcements by NVIDIA at CES 2025 - Techloy (Source 3.2)', 'Everything Nvidia announced at CES 2025 - Mashable (Source 3.4)', "Nvidia's New AI Innovations at CES 2025: Explained - Technology Magazine (Source 3.6)", 'NVIDIA GTC 2025 Wrap-up: 18 New Products to Watch - MinIO Blog (Source 3.7)']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
27	NVDA	yearly	2007	In 2007, NVIDIA experienced a pivotal year that saw its transformation from a graphics-focused startup into a diversified technology leader. A primary development was the launch of the **Tesla** product line, which introduced specialized hardware for high-performance computing (HPC) and scientific research, marking the first major commercialization of the **CUDA** architecture. In January 2007, the company was recognized for its technical innovation with a **Technology and Engineering Emmy Award** for its role in graphics processor development. \n\nFinancially, the company achieved record-breaking milestones. For the fiscal year ending January 28, 2007, NVIDIA reported record revenue of **$3.07 billion**, representing a 29% increase over the previous year. GAAP net income reached **$448.8 million**, driven by strong demand for the GeForce 8 series and the company's 58% market share in the notebook GPU segment. This performance led **Forbes** to name NVIDIA its **'Company of the Year'** in December 2007, highlighting its growth and strategic positioning at the center of visual computing.\n\nCorporate expansion was also a major theme through strategic acquisitions. In January, NVIDIA completed its acquisition of **PortalPlayer, Inc.**, a supplier of semiconductors for personal media players, for approximately $357 million. Later in December, the company acquired **Mental Images**, a leader in professional rendering software, further strengthening its software ecosystem. Throughout the year, NVIDIA also focused on the industry-wide transition to **DirectX 10** and the launch of the Windows Vista operating system.	NVIDIA's 2007 was a milestone year characterized by record financial growth and a strategic pivot into high-performance computing with the launch of the **Tesla** GPU line. The company reported record annual revenue of **$3.07 billion** and was named **'Company of the Year'** by Forbes. Key developments included the completion of the **PortalPlayer** acquisition, the purchase of **Mental Images**, and receiving a **Technology and Engineering Emmy Award** for innovation in graphics processing.	['NVIDIA Reports Record Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal Year 2007 | TechPowerUp', 'FISCAL 2007 ANNUAL REPORT ON FORM 10-K - SEC.gov', "Nvidia Named Forbes' Company Of The Year - Game Developer", 'Nvidia company history & timeline: From GPU maker to AI leader | Baker City Herald', 'Timeline: Nvidia | AI Magazine', 'History of Nvidia: Company timeline and facts - TheStreet', "A Comprehensive Analysis of Nvidia's Technological Innovations - ResearchGate", 'Nvidia Subsidiaries and Acquisitions - Insights;Gate']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
28	NVDA	yearly	2006	In 2006, NVIDIA underwent a fundamental strategic transformation that laid the groundwork for its future dominance in artificial intelligence and high-performance computing. The most significant development was the launch of CUDA (Compute Unified Device Architecture) on November 8, 2006, which opened NVIDIA GPUs for general-purpose parallel processing beyond traditional graphics. This year also saw the release of the GeForce 8800 GTX, the first GPU based on the Tesla architecture, which provided the hardware foundation for the CUDA platform. From a corporate perspective, NVIDIA acquired Hybrid Graphics in March 2006 to strengthen its position in the mobile graphics software market. However, the company faced regulatory challenges in December 2006 when it received a subpoena from the U.S. Department of Justice regarding a potential antitrust investigation into the graphics card industry. Additionally, 2006 was the year of a notable failed acquisition attempt by AMD; reports indicate that negotiations ended because NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang insisted on being the CEO of the combined entity. Financially, NVIDIA's stock performed exceptionally well, with its split-adjusted price nearly doubling from approximately $0.32 at the start of the year to $0.62 by the end of December.	The year 2006 was a turning point for NVIDIA, highlighted by the unveiling of the CUDA architecture, which transitioned GPUs into general-purpose processors. Key developments included the launch of the GeForce 8800 series, the acquisition of Hybrid Graphics in March, and a December subpoena from the U.S. Department of Justice regarding antitrust concerns. This year also marked a significant 'what-if' in corporate history with the collapse of potential merger talks between NVIDIA and AMD.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'Nvidia company history & timeline: From GPU maker to AI leader | Baker City Herald', 'NVIDIA UNVEILS CUDA™ – THE GPU COMPUTING REVOLUTION BEGINS