29	NVDA	yearly	2002	In 2002, NVIDIA reached several significant milestones while navigating regulatory scrutiny. A defining corporate event was the company's addition to the S&P 500 for the first time. Financially, the company announced record results for its fiscal year 2002 (which ended January 27, 2002), reporting $1.37 billion in revenue and $177.1 million in net profit. However, the year also brought challenges: in February, the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) launched an investigation into the company's accounting practices for the years 2000 and 2001, causing the stock price to drop 12%. This investigation led to a financial restatement on April 29, 2002, which corrected previously overstated profits.\n\nTechnologically, the year was prolific for product releases. On February 6, 2002, NVIDIA launched the GeForce 4 series, featuring both the 'MX' and 'Ti' architectures. This was followed by the release of the mid-range GeForce 4 Ti 4200 in April. In July, the company introduced its second-generation platform processor, the nForce2, which supported dual DDR400 memory. By November 2002, NVIDIA unveiled its first fully programmable cinematic GPU, the GeForce FX, which was the first in the industry to use 0.13-micron process technology. Additionally, NVIDIA's mobile technology gained traction in November when the GeForce2 Go was selected for the HP Compaq Tablet PC TC1000, one of the first tablet notebooks in the industry.	2002 was a transformative year for NVIDIA, highlighted by its first-time inclusion in the S&P 500 and the launch of the GeForce 4 series in February. Despite reporting record annual revenue of $1.37 billion, the company faced a federal accounting probe that led to a stock drop in February and a restatement of previous earnings in April. The year concluded with the November introduction of the GeForce FX, a flagship 'cinematic' GPU that debuted the 0.13-micron manufacturing process.	[" (NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf)", 