30	NVDA	yearly	2020	The year 2020 was a transformative period for NVIDIA, characterized by a massive strategic shift toward data centers and the completion of its largest acquisition at the time. In April 2020, NVIDIA finalized its $7 billion acquisition of Mellanox Technologies, a move designed to unify high-performance computing and networking for next-generation data centers. Later that year, in September, the company announced a definitive agreement to acquire Arm Limited from SoftBank for $40 billion, though this deal faced significant regulatory scrutiny. On the technological front, May 2020 saw the unveiling of the Ampere architecture and the A100 Tensor Core GPU, which became a foundational tool for AI and data analytics. This was followed by the September launch of the GeForce RTX 30-series, which NVIDIA described as the greatest generational leap in performance for its consumer graphics cards. Financially, the company reached new heights, reporting record quarterly revenues throughout the calendar year. By the end of the third quarter of fiscal 2021 (October 2020), Data Center revenue had surged 162% year-over-year, largely due to the integration of Mellanox and the demand for AI infrastructure. Other notable developments included the introduction of the BlueField-2 DPU (Data Processing Unit) and the open beta launch of NVIDIA Omniverse.	In 2020, NVIDIA solidified its position as a data center powerhouse by completing the $7 billion acquisition of Mellanox Technologies and announcing a $40 billion bid for Arm Limited. The company launched several landmark products, including the A100 Tensor Core GPU for AI workloads and the GeForce RTX 30-series for gaming. Financially, NVIDIA saw record growth, with Data Center revenue jumping 162% by late 2020 as AI demand accelerated globally.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal 2020', 'NVIDIA Corporation Business Report FY ended Jan. 2020 - MarkLines', 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Second Quarter Fiscal 2020', "Nvidia's 8 Biggest GTC 2020 Product Announcements You Might Have Missed | CRN", 'NVIDIA Completes Acquisition of Mellanox, Creating Major Force Driving Next-Gen Data Centers', 'NVIDIA: From Gaming Pioneer to AI Powerhouse | Medium', 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal 2021', 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Third Quarter Fiscal 2021', 'NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Second Quarter Fiscal 2021', 'NVIDIA Delivers Greatest-Ever Generational Leap with GeForce RTX 30 Series GPUs', 'GeForce RTX 30 Series Graphics Cards: The Ultimate Play - NVIDIA', "NVIDIA to Acquire Arm for $40 Billion, Creating World's Premier Computing Company for the Age of AI", "Nvidia Announces ARM Acquisition for $40 Billion | Tom's Hardware", 'Proposed acquisition of ARM Limited by NVIDIA Corporation: public interest intervention - GOV.UK']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
31	NVDA	yearly	1995	In 1995, NVIDIA marked its transition from a quiet startup to a visible player in the semiconductor industry with the launch of its inaugural product and the securing of vital early-stage capital. \n\n### **Technological Milestones**\n* **Launch of the NV1:** NVIDIA officially announced the NV1 on May 22, 1995, and released it in November of that year. It was a groundbreaking multimedia PCI card that integrated 2D/3D graphics, 32-channel wavetable audio, and a Sega Saturn-compatible joypad port into a single chip.\n* **Quadratic Texture Mapping:** The NV1 was unique for using quadratic texture mapping (curved surfaces) as its geometric primitive rather than the industry-standard triangles. This design choice ultimately led to commercial difficulties when Microsoft's later DirectX API exclusively supported triangle-based rendering.\n* **Strategic Manufacturing Partnership:** As a fabless company, NVIDIA partnered with SGS-Thomson Microelectronics to manufacture the NV1 (also marketed as the STG2000).\n\n### **Financial and Corporate Developments**\n* **Venture Capital Funding:** The company secured its first major round of outside funding in 1995 from prominent venture capital firms Sequoia Capital and Sierra Ventures.\n* **Sega Licensing Agreement:** On July 31, 1995, NVIDIA and Sega reached an exclusive licensing agreement to port popular Sega Saturn titles, such as *Virtua Fighter*, to the PC platform specifically for the NV1 hardware.\n* **Market Entry:** The hardware reached consumers via retail partners like Diamond Multimedia, which shipped the 'Diamond Edge 3D' series based on NVIDIA's technology starting in November 1995 with a launch price of approximately $299.	1995 was a foundational year for NVIDIA, defined by the launch of its first product, the NV1, and the acquisition of critical venture capital. The NV1 was an ambitious all-in-one multimedia card that integrated 3D graphics, audio, and gaming ports. While the chip utilized non-standard quadratic texture mapping—which would later prove a commercial liability against Microsoft's triangle-based DirectX—it successfully attracted a strategic partnership with Sega and secured the company's first round of funding from Sequoia Capital and Sierra Ventures.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'https://www.nvidia.com/content/timeline/time_95.html', 'https://www.thestreet.com/technology/nvidia-company-history-timeline', 'https://pestel-analysis.com/blogs/brief-history/nvidia', 'https://www.computinghistory.org.uk/det/32765/Nvidia', 'https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/nv1.c2015', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NV1', 'https://www.vogons.org/viewtopic.php?t=77479', 'https://www.fundinguniverse.com/company-histories/nvidia-corporation-history/', 'https://www.oreateai.com/blog/the-development-history-and-technological-evolution-of-nvidia-corporation-part-one-company-overview-and-historical-changes/c89e1908d0112ed54b96412df3077ff1']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
32	NVDA	yearly	2016	In 2016, NVIDIA experienced a pivotal year that transitioned the company from a gaming-centric hardware manufacturer to a leader in the global AI revolution. Financially, the company reported record revenue for its fiscal year 2016 (ending January 31, 2016) of $5.01 billion, a 7% increase year-over-year. Throughout the calendar year, NVIDIA's stock price nearly tripled as investors recognized its dominance in deep learning and autonomous driving. \n\nKey developments included the launch of the Pascal GPU architecture, which powered the new Tesla P100 for data centers and the GeForce GTX 10-series for gamers. A major historical milestone occurred when CEO Jensen Huang personally delivered the world's first DGX-1 AI supercomputer to OpenAI, marking the beginning of the generative AI era. In the automotive sector, NVIDIA launched the DRIVE PX 2, an AI-powered engine for autonomous vehicles, and secured a partnership with Volvo to power a fleet of self-driving SUVs. \n\nOn the corporate and legal front, NVIDIA settled a long-standing patent dispute with Samsung in May 2016 and reached a settlement regarding a class-action lawsuit over the advertising of its GTX 970 graphics card's memory. Strategically, the company completed its exit from the mobile modem market by winding down its Icera operations to focus entirely on specialized accelerated computing. Total capital returned to shareholders through dividends and share repurchases reached $800 million for the fiscal year.	2016 was a transformative year for NVIDIA, marked by its definitive pivot toward artificial intelligence and record financial performance. The company reached a record $5.01 billion in fiscal year revenue and saw its stock price triple as it established dominance in deep learning. Key technological milestones included the introduction of the Pascal architecture, the launch of the DRIVE PX 2 for self-driving cars, and the delivery of the first DGX-1 AI supercomputer to OpenAI. Additionally, NVIDIA settled major intellectual property litigation with Samsung and strategically exited the mobile modem market to focus on high-growth AI and gaming sectors.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-the-fourth-quarter-and-fiscal-2016', 'https://www.techpowerup.com/220158/nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-the-fourth-quarter-and-fiscal-2016', 'https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-announcements-at-2016-gpu-technology-conference/', 'https://s201.q4cdn.com/141608511/files/doc_financials/annual/2016/NV2016_AnnualReport_and_Proxy.pdf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nvidia', 'https://www.fool.com/investing/2017/01/03/why-nvidia-stock-tripled-in-2016.aspx', 'https://www.nasdaq.com/articles/how-nvidia-corporation-may-have-just-lost-billions-2016-05-17']	2026-01-29 15:53:09
33	NVDA	five_year	1998-2002	Between 1998 and 2002, NVIDIA transformed from a struggling startup into a dominant force in the semiconductor industry, marked by the birth of the GPU, a successful IPO, and significant market consolidation.\n\n1998: Establishing the Foundation\nNVIDIA released the RIVA TNT in August 1998, the industry's first multi-texturing 3D processor, which established the company as a serious competitor to market leader 3dfx. This year also saw a critical strategic manufacturing partnership with TSMC and the company's reincorporation in Delaware. Although NVIDIA filed for its initial public offering (IPO) in early 1998, the listing was delayed until the following year.\n\n1999: IPO and the World's First GPU\nOn January 22, 1999, NVIDIA went public on the NASDAQ with shares priced at $12, generating $158 million in total revenue during its first year. The most significant milestone was the October release of the GeForce 256. Marketed as the "world's first GPU," it introduced hardware transform and lighting (T&L), which offloaded rendering tasks from the CPU and revolutionized 3D graphics.\n\n2000: Consolidating the Market\nNVIDIA secured a massive contract to develop graphics hardware for the Microsoft Xbox, receiving a $200 million advance. Following a 700% surge in stock price, the company executed its first 2-for-1 stock split in June. By December, NVIDIA reached a definitive agreement to acquire the assets of its rival, 3dfx Interactive, effectively consolidating the consumer graphics market.\n\n2001: Financial Records and New Milestones\nNVIDIA became the fastest semiconductor company to reach $1 billion in annual revenue. Its growing influence was recognized by Wall Street through inclusion in the Nasdaq-100 and the S&P 500 indices. On the product front, it launched the GeForce3, the industry’s first programmable GPU, and the nForce platform. However, the year also brought an SEC inquiry into the company's accounting practices.\n\n2002: Growth Amidst Regulatory Scrutiny\nFor fiscal year 2002, NVIDIA reported record revenue of $1.37 billion and a net profit of $177.1 million. Despite officially joining the S&P 500, the company faced challenges from an SEC investigation regarding accounting practices from 2000 and 2001, leading to a financial restatement in April. Technologically, the company remained prolific, launching the GeForce 4 series and the GeForce FX, the industry's first fully programmable cinematic GPU.			2026-01-29 15:53:09
34	NVDA	yearly	1999	The year 1999 was a transformative period for NVIDIA, marked by its transition into a public company and the introduction of a new category of technology. On January 22, 1999, NVIDIA launched its Initial Public Offering (IPO) on the NASDAQ, with shares priced at $12 each. During its first year as a public entity, the company generated approximately $158 million in total revenue. \n\nTechnologically, the year was defined by the release of two major product lines. In March 1999, NVIDIA launched the RIVA TNT2, codenamed 'NV5', which succeeded the RIVA TNT and established the company as a serious competitor in the graphics market. However, the most significant milestone occurred on October 11, 1999, with the release of the GeForce 256. Marketed as the 'world's first GPU' (Graphics Processing Unit), the GeForce 256 was the first chip to integrate hardware transform and lighting (T&L), which offloaded complex 3D rendering tasks from the CPU. This architectural shift not only revolutionized gaming performance for titles like Quake III Arena but also laid the technical foundation for the future of parallel computing and artificial intelligence.	In 1999, NVIDIA successfully transitioned from a private startup to a public company, launching its IPO on January 22 at $12 per share and ending the fiscal year with $158 million in revenue. This year also saw the launch of the RIVA TNT2 in March and the revolutionary GeForce 256 in October. Marketed as the world's first GPU, the GeForce 256 introduced hardware transform and lighting, a development that redefined gaming graphics and established the foundation for modern AI computing.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf", 'WisdomTree: Nvidia: We are watching history', 'Nasdaq: If You Invested $10000 in Nvidia in 1999', "The Motley Fool: Nvidia's Revenue Growth Over the Last 25 Years", 'TechPowerUp: NVIDIA Riva TNT2 Specs', 'Wikipedia: RIVA TNT2', "Quantum Zeitgeist: NVIDIA's GeForce 256 Revolutionize