39	NVDA	yearly	2009	In 2009, NVIDIA navigated a transition from a challenging financial period into a strategic focus on 'visual computing' and the nascent GPU computing market. Financially, for the full fiscal year 2010 (which largely spanned the calendar year 2009), the company reported total revenue of approximately $3.3 billion. Despite a GAAP net loss of $68.0 million for that fiscal year, the company saw significant sequential revenue growth throughout 2009 as markets recovered, with revenue rising from $664.2 million in Q1 to $982.5 million by Q4. Key strategic and technical developments included:\n\n* **Fermi Architecture Unveiling**: At the inaugural GPU Technology Conference (GTC) in September 2009, NVIDIA introduced the 'Fermi' architecture. This ground-up redesign was marketed as the foundation for the world's first computational GPUs, featuring a true cache hierarchy to significantly accelerate general-purpose parallel processing (CUDA).\n* **Tegra and Mobile Expansion**: The company began volume shipments of its Tegra 'computer-on-a-chip'. Notably, Microsoft's Zune HD media player, released in September 2009, was revealed to be powered by Tegra. Earlier in the year, NVIDIA also demonstrated a port of Google's Android OS to the Tegra platform.\n* **NVIDIA Ion and 3D Vision**: Early in 2009, NVIDIA launched the Ion platform, which paired GeForce 9400 GPUs with Intel Atom CPUs to enable premium performance in mini-notebooks. They also introduced GeForce 3D Vision, the first high-definition 3D stereo solution for home gaming and entertainment.\n* **HPC and Networking**: In November 2009, NVIDIA and Mellanox Technologies announced software to optimize communication between Tesla GPUs and InfiniBand networks, improving cluster performance by up to 30%. The company also unveiled the Tesla 20-series parallel processors based on the Fermi architecture.\n* **Corporate Actions**: In May 2009, the company executed a cash tender offer to purchase employee stock options, resulting in a non-recurring charge of $140.2 million.	2009 was a pivotal year for NVIDIA as it transitioned toward high-performance computing and mobile integration. The company reported annual revenue of $3.3 billion for fiscal year 2010, though it recorded a net loss of $68 million. Technological highlights included the unveiling of the groundbreaking Fermi architecture at the first-ever GTC, the launch of the Ion platform for netbooks, and the first major design wins for the Tegra mobile processor in devices like the Microsoft Zune HD. Additionally, the company deepened its focus on GPU computing (CUDA) and announced a strategic networking partnership with Mellanox to enhance Tesla GPU performance in data centers.	["NVIDIA's Financial and Development Timeline.pdf (Source 99)", 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal Year 2010 (Source 2.4)', 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for First Quarter Fiscal Year 2010 (Source 2.1)', 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Second Quarter Fiscal Year 2010 (Source 2.2)', "NVIDIA Unveils Next Generation CUDA GPU Architecture – Codenamed 'Fermi' (Source 3.1)", '2009 Press Releases - NVIDIA (Source 1.3)', 'Tegra - Wikipedia (Source 3.5)', 'NVIDIA Reports Financial Results for Fourth Quarter and Fiscal Year 2009 | TechPowerUp (Source 4.4)', "NVIDIA's Fermi: The First Complete GPU Computing Architecture (Source 3.2)"]	2026-01-29 15:53:09
