l shift occurred in March with the launch of the Hopper architecture and the H100 GPU, which became the industry benchmark for training large language models.			2026-01-29 15:53:09
41	AMD	yearly	2013	2013 was a pivotal year for AMD as the company's semi-custom SoC strategy began paying dividends at massive scale: the Sony PlayStation 4 (launched November 15, 2013) and Microsoft Xbox One (launched November 22, 2013) both contained custom AMD APUs—combining Jaguar-based CPU cores with AMD's GCN (Graphics Core Next) GPU architecture on a single die. These semi-custom wins gave AMD a guaranteed revenue stream for the following 5-7 years of console generation lifecycle and demonstrated the company's ability to compete for high-volume system-on-chip designs beyond traditional PC components. The console launches immediately began contributing to the Enterprise, Embedded and Semi-Custom segment revenue.\n\nFull-year revenue was approximately $5.30 billion—a slight decline from 2012—and AMD posted a GAAP net loss of $83 million, a significant improvement from 2012's deeper losses as cost restructuring and the console ramp offset continued PC market challenges. Rory Read's diversification strategy was gaining traction, though the CPU business was still under pressure from Intel's Haswell architecture which firmly held performance leadership across all price tiers. AMD launched the Kaveri APU in late 2013 (shipping in January 2014), which marked the first full implementation of AMD's Heterogeneous System Architecture (HSA)—allowing the CPU and GPU to share the same memory space and work together on compute tasks. AMD also launched the Radeon R9 and R7 200 series GPUs based on the GCN 1.0 architecture, which were well-received in the mid-range and enthusiast gaming segments. The question facing AMD as 2013 ended was whether these diverse bets could provide a foundation for sustained recovery.	2013 was defined by the launch of AMD-powered PlayStation 4 and Xbox One consoles in November, validating AMD's semi-custom SoC strategy and providing a multi-year revenue floor that helped narrow losses to $83 million on approximately $5.30 billion in revenue. The Kaveri APU neared commercial launch with full HSA support, while CEO Rory Read's diversification push positioned AMD for what would become Lisa Su's successful turnaround.	["AMD Reports 2013 Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD: A Complete Overview - Microchip USA", "AMD Annual Report 2013", "PlayStation 4 - Wikipedia", "Xbox One - Wikipedia", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/476/amd-reports-2013-fourth-quarter-and-annual-results"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
42	AMD	yearly	2022	2022 was AMD's biggest year by revenue but also one of its most turbulent, as extraordinary growth from the Xilinx acquisition closed in February confronted a dramatic second-half reversal driven by a global PC market downturn and aggressive inventory corrections. Annual revenue grew 44% to $23.60 billion—the first time AMD had ever reported more than $20 billion in revenue—but the growth figure was heavily influenced by the inclusion of approximately 10.5 months of Xilinx revenue. On February 14, 2022, AMD completed its acquisition of Xilinx in an all-stock transaction, a deal that had grown to approximately $49 billion in value, representing the largest semiconductor acquisition in history at the time. The combined company added Xilinx's portfolio of FPGAs, adaptive SoCs, and AI engines to AMD's CPU and GPU lineup.\n\nProduct launches were significant across multiple fronts. In September 2022, AMD launched the Ryzen 7000 series desktop processors based on the Zen 4 architecture on TSMC's 5nm process—the first mainstream desktop platform from AMD to use DDR5 memory and PCIe 5.0. While Zen 4 delivered strong performance improvements, the ecosystem was expensive at launch due to new DDR5 memory pricing and new AM5 motherboards, and Intel's competing 13th Gen Core (Raptor Lake) processors offered stiff competition on price-to-performance. In November 2022, AMD launched the 4th Gen EPYC 'Genoa' server processors with up to 96 Zen 4 cores per socket, targeting hyperscale and enterprise data centers and delivering substantial performance-per-watt improvements.\n\nThe second half of 2022 delivered a significant financial shock. A rapid deterioration in PC market demand—the sharpest single-year PC shipment decline in decades—combined with high channel inventory led AMD to issue a major Q3 revenue warning in October, slashing its forecast by approximately $1.1 billion. The Client (consumer CPU) segment was particularly hard hit. AMD's stock fell approximately 55% during 2022, from near its all-time high of ~$161 to around $65, tracking broader tech sector declines and AMD-specific inventory concerns. GAAP net income was $1.32 billion for the full year, significantly impacted by Xilinx acquisition amortization charges.	2022 was AMD's largest-ever revenue year at $23.60 billion (up 44%), driven by the $49 billion Xilinx acquisition closing in February—then the largest deal in semiconductor history. However, a sharp PC market downturn in H2 caused a severe earnings warning and sent AMD's stock down ~55% for the year, while strong launches in Zen 4 Ryzen 7000 and EPYC Genoa offset some of the Client segment weakness.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Full Year 2022 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD Completes Acquisition of Xilinx - AMD Investor Relations", "$50bn Xilinx Acquisition is Shaping the Future of Computing - SGFER Research", "AMD Completes Record ~$50 Billion Acquisition of Xilinx - Data Center Knowledge", "AMD Posts 70% Year-Over-Year Revenue Increase as EPYC CPUs Skyrocket - Tom's Hardware", "AMD Reports Second Quarter 2022 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations", "AMD 2022 Annual Report on Form 10-K - SEC Filing", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/1115/amd-reports-fourth-quarter-and-full-year-2022-financial-results"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
