44	AMD	yearly	1988	In 1988, Advanced Micro Devices released the Am29000 RISC processor for general availability in May 1988. The processor showed impressive performance capabilities and was positioned for use in medium- to high-performance embedded applications. The Am29000 was used in various products including X terminals, laser printer controller cards, graphics accelerator cards, optical character recognition solutions, and network bridges. The processor demonstrated impressive performance, sustaining 10-25 million instructions per second (MIPS). AMD continued its ongoing reverse-engineering effort on the 80386 processor, making significant progress toward creating a fully compatible version. The company was also engaged in significant legal disputes with Intel regarding the scope of their technology exchange agreements and AMD's rights to manufacture the 386. AMD continued to generate substantial revenue from its second-source 8086, 8088, 80186, and 80286 processors, which remained in high demand for IBM PC-compatible computers and embedded systems. The semiconductor industry remained generally strong during 1988, with growing demand for processors of all types. AMD's diversification into RISC processors represented a strategic move to reduce dependence on Intel's x86 architecture and to compete in adjacent markets.	AMD released the Am29000 RISC processor for general availability in May 1988, which was used in embedded applications and achieved 10-25 MIPS performance. The company continued reverse-engineering the 386 processor.	["Wikipedia - AMD Am29000", "WikiChip - Am29000", "CPU Museum - Am29000"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
