48	AMD	yearly	1996	1996 was a challenging transition year for AMD as the company launched its first fully internally designed Pentium-generation CPU—the K5—to a mixed reception, while Intel's Pentium Pro and forthcoming Pentium II raised the competitive bar significantly. The K5, which combined a RISC execution core with an x86 frontend, launched later than planned and delivered solid integer performance but was hampered by mediocre floating-point performance, inadequate clock speeds relative to competing Intel Pentium chips, and manufacturing challenges that limited supply. The K5's commercial impact was modest, and AMD recognized it would need the NexGen-derived K6 to be competitive in the mainstream Pentium-class market.\n\nRevenue fell to approximately $1.6 billion as the K5's disappointing performance and the transition away from the Am486 product cycle created a gap in competitive CPU offerings. The broader semiconductor market was also experiencing pricing pressure. However, AMD was actively integrating the NexGen team and accelerating K6 development; internal engineering results on K6 performance were encouraging, and AMD was cautiously optimistic that K6 could match or exceed Intel's Pentium II clock-for-clock in many workloads. AMD also settled a trademark dispute with Intel in 1996, acknowledging Intel's MMX trademark while securing rights to use the MMX designation in marketing its own K6 MMX-capable processors. Flash memory operations continued to contribute revenue through the Fujitsu joint venture.	AMD launched the K5 processor in 1996 as its first fully internally designed Pentium-class CPU, but the product suffered from delayed delivery, limited clock speeds, and mediocre floating-point performance—resulting in a revenue decline to approximately $1.6 billion. The NexGen-derived K6 was in active development as AMD's next competitive CPU response to Intel's Pentium II.	["The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia", "AMD vs Intel - Dif