ld later attract global regulatory action.	["Opteron - Wikipedia", "Athlon 64 - Wikipedia", "AMD All-Time High Revenue Driven by Record Sales of Opteron - SupercomputingOnline", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia", "AMD Annual Report 2004"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
50	AMD	five_year	1984-1988	Between 1984 and 1988, AMD's relationship with Intel collapsed into one of the most consequential legal battles in semiconductor history. Intel's refusal to grant AMD second-source rights to the 386 processor sparked a decade of litigation while AMD undertook the enormously difficult challenge of reverse-engineering Intel's processors without access to Intel's source code. The period also saw AMD diversify into RISC architectures and flash memory, laying the groundwork for product lines that would survive independently of the Intel legal dispute.\n\n1984: Intel Cuts Off the 386 Revenue grew to approximately $710 million, and AMD created the world's first 512K EPROM—demonstrating continued technical innovation. However, the defining event of 1984 was Intel's decision not to extend the 1982 technology exchange agreement to cover the upcoming 80386 processor. Intel argued the agreement did not obligate it to share future generations; AMD argued the opposite. The legal and commercial battle that would define both companies' trajectories for the next decade had effectively begun, even before formal litigation. AMD's 286 manufacturing continued at pace while engineering teams began studying the 386 architecture for potential reverse-engineering.\n\n1985: Recession and the Seeds of Litigation A cyclical semiconductor industry downturn hit AMD hard in 1985, with revenue declining and workforce reductions required for the first time since the 1974 recession. Intel formally terminated the technology exchange agreement, citing AMD's alleged breach of contract in sublicensing arrangements. AMD immediately filed for arbitration, and the legal dispute entered the formal adversarial phase. Despite the financial and legal turbulence, AMD continued research into the Am29000 RISC processor architecture and next-generation memory technologies, diversifying its R&D beyond the x86 clone strategy.\n\n1986: Reverse-Engineering and the Am386 Project AMD committed