sitioned itself for what it hoped would be a major legal and commercial breakthrough in the early 1990s.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
51	AMD	yearly	2018	2018 was a year of continued momentum for AMD, with full-year revenue reaching $6.48 billion—a 23% increase from 2017—as Ryzen and EPYC product lines continued to ramp, and GPU demand was amplified by a cryptocurrency mining boom in the first half of the year. The company achieved its strongest gross margins in years, at approximately 37%, and posted a GAAP net income of $337 million, a strong follow-up to its return to profitability in 2017. However, the year also brought challenges: a sharp crash in cryptocurrency markets in the second half of 2018 created a significant GPU inventory glut across the industry, dampening graphics revenue heading into 2019.\n\nOn the product front, AMD launched Ryzen 2000 'Pinnacle Ridge' in April 2018—a refined version of Zen using GlobalFoundries' 12nm process, delivering meaningful performance and efficiency improvements over Ryzen 1000. The higher-end Ryzen Threadripper 2000 series offered up to 32 cores for the HEDT (high-end desktop) market, a segment AMD essentially owned as Intel struggled to compete. AMD also launched the Radeon RX Vega 56 and 64 GPUs, its high-end architecture for the discrete GPU market—though Vega underperformed NVIDIA's competing products in certain workloads and suffered from supply constraints. For the data center, AMD began ramping EPYC deployments, with cloud providers including Microsoft Azure, Baidu, and China Telecom announcing or expanding EPYC-powered instances.\n\nAMD also revealed 'Rome'—the second-generation EPYC processor based on the Zen 2 architecture and TSMC's 7nm process—at its Financial Analyst Day in November 2018, generating significant industry excitement with projections of over 2x performance-per-socket improvement over Naples. The company's stock performance was volatile in 2018, hitting a high of about $34 before pulling back to around $18 by year-end amid the broader technology sector sell-off and the cryptocurrency hangover. Still, AMD ended the year with far greater scale, profitability, and technical credibility than it had possessed just two years prior.	2018 saw AMD continue its recovery arc with revenue growing 23% to $6.48 billion and GAAP net income of $337 million, supported by Ryzen 2000 and EPYC ramp-up alongside a crypto-mining boom in GPUs. The year was marked by volatility as the crypto collapse created a GPU oversupply in H2, while AMD previewed its transformative 7nm Zen 2 'Rome' EPYC architecture that would define 2019.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2018 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD Financial Analyst Day 2018 - AMD", "AMD Ryzen 2000 Series Review - AnandTech", "AMD Radeon RX Vega 64 Review - TechSpot", "AMD EPYC Azure, Baidu, China Telecom announcements - AMD newsroom", "AMD Revenue 2012-2025 - MacroTrends", "electroiq.com AMD Statistics By Revenue", "https://ir.amd.com/financial-information/historical-financials"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
52	AMD	yearly	2011	2011 was a pivotal year for AMD marked by two product generations that defined the company's near-term trajectory in very different ways. Annual revenue reached $6.57 billion, AMD's highest since 2000, with growth driven by the Llano APU launch and continued GPU demand. The Llano (codenamed A-series Fusion APU), launched in Q2 2011, was AMD's first commercially available APU combining K10-class CPU cores with a Radeon HD 6000-class GPU on a single die using GlobalFoundries' 32nm process. For mainstream laptops and desktops, Llano delivered exceptional integrated graphics performance—significantly superior to Intel's Sandy Bridge integrated graphics—at competitive prices, and it was well-received by reviewers and OEM partners. The Llano APU validated AMD's Fusion strategy and established a product line that resonated strongly in the mobile and thin-and-light PC segment.\n\nIn sharp contrast, AMD launched the Bulldozer CPU architecture in Q4 2011 for desktop processors (branded FX-series), and it proved deeply disappointing. Bulldozer introduced a radical new 'module' design where pairs of integer cores shared a floating-point unit—a design intended to increase throughput for multi-threaded workloads. In practice, Bulldozer delivered IPC performance well below AMD's own previous generation, and its gaming and single-threaded performance was significantly behind Intel's Sandy Bridge architecture. The FX-8150 Bulldozer flagship was slower than the Phenom II X4 in many tasks and was consistently beaten by Intel's Core i5-2500K despite AMD's higher core count. CEO Dirk Meyer resigned unexpectedly in January 2011 and was replaced by Rory Read in August 2011—another major leadership transition that AMD needed to navigate amid the competitive pressure.	AMD achieved revenue of $6.57 billion in 2011—its highest since 2000—but the year told two divergent stories: the Llano APU succeeded as AMD's first fusion CPU+GPU product with best-in-class integrated graphics, while the Bulldozer FX-series desktop CPU launched to widespread disappointment for poor IPC and gaming performance. CEO Dirk Meyer resigned and Rory Read was appointed as his replacement.	["AMD Q4'11 & FY 2011 Earnings Report: 1.69B Revenue For Q4, 6.57B Revenue For 2011 - AnandTech", "AMD - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "The History of AMD: A Complete Overview - Microchip USA", "Bulldozer - Wikipedia"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
