53	AMD	yearly	1984	In 1984, a major turning point in AMD's history occurred when Intel and AMD amended their existing contract to allow AMD to be a second-source manufacturer of successive generations of the x86 processor architecture, specifically the 80186 and 80286, in exchange for substantial royalties to Intel. However, this same year marked the beginning of a contentious relationship between the two companies. Intel began developing the 80386 processor, the next generation of the x86 architecture, and made the decision not to grant AMD a second-source license for this processor. By the end of 1984, AMD's revenue reached approximately $709.6 million for the year, demonstrating the company's massive growth from second-sourcing the 8086 and 8088. The company created the world's first 512K EPROM in 1984. However, the refusal to include the 386 in the technology exchange agreement set the stage for years of litigation and conflict between AMD and Intel. AMD would spend considerable resources reverse-engineering the 386 without Intel's permission. This dispute would fundamentally change the competitive dynamics of the semiconductor industry and AMD's strategic focus.	AMD achieved revenue of approximately $709.6 million in 1984 while creating the world's first 512K EPROM. However, Intel terminated second-source rights to the 386 processor, sparking future litigation.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "History of Advanced Micro Devices - FundingUniverse", "Harvard Journal of Law & Technology - Intel and the x86 Architecture"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
54	AMD	yearly	2008	2008 was a year of dramatic restructuring for AMD, centered on a strategic decision that would reshape the company's fundamental identity: the spin-off of its manufacturing operations into a separate company. In October 2008, AMD announced an agreement with the Abu Dhabi-based Advanced Technology Investment Company (ATIC) to create an independent foundry called GlobalFoundries. The deal, which closed in March 2009, transferred AMD's fabrication assets in Dresden, Germany and Milpitas, California to the new entity while raising approximately $6 billion in cash for AMD. Going 'fab-lite' (and eventually 'fabless') allowed AMD to drastically reduce the capital expenditure burden that had been crushing its balance sheet since the Fab 30 era. In exchange, AMD became a major customer of GlobalFoundries under long-term supply agreements.\n\nFinancially, 2008 was very difficult: revenue fell to approximately $5.8 billion amid the global financial crisis and continued CPU market share losses to Intel. AMD posted a GAAP net loss of approximately $3.1 billion, though this figure was heavily influenced by charges related to the GlobalFoundries transaction and goodwill impairments. New CEO Dirk Meyer, who had been AMD's chief architect for the K7 Athlon and K8 Athlon 64, worked to stabilize the organization and focus resources. On the GPU side, AMD launched the Radeon HD 4870 and 4850 in June 2008—GPUs that received exceptional reviews for delivering high-end performance at mid-range prices and reasserting AMD's graphics competitiveness against NVIDIA. Intel was also fined a record €1.06 billion by the European Commission in the ongoing antitrust case related to AMD's 2004 lawsuit.	2008 was AMD's year of structural transformation: the spin-off of manufacturing operations into GlobalFoundries (with Abu Dhabi capital) freed AMD from the capital-intensive fab ownership model that had burdened its balance sheet for a decade. Revenue was approximately $5.8 billion with heavy net losses, but the Radeon HD 4800 series GPU launched to rave reviews, and Intel was fined a record €1.06 billion by European regulators in the AMD antitrust case.	["AMD - Wikipedia", "GlobalFoundries - Wikipedia", "ATI Technologies - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD: A Complete Overview - Microchip USA", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations", "The Rise and Fall of AMD - TechSpot"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
55	AMD	yearly	1994	1994 was a year of legal resolution and strategic transition for AMD. In a landmark verdict, a jury found in AMD's favor in the 287 Microcode Litigation, affirming AMD's right to use Intel's microcode in its processors through the 486 generation—a critical legal victory that legitimized AMD's Am486 product line and provided a foundation for subsequent settlements. AMD's Am486 processors continued to ramp commercially, with the high-clock-speed variants (including an Am486DX4-120 clocked higher than any Intel 486) proving popular among cost-conscious PC manufacturers. Annual revenue grew to approximately $1.64 billion as Am486 unit volumes expanded and the company maintained roughly 20% of the 486 CPU market.\n\nAMD's longer-term CPU strategy was in flux. The K5 processor—AMD's first fully internally designed fifth-generation processor, intended to compete with Intel's Pentium—was in advanced development but running late. AMD had combined a RISC execution core based on the Am29000 architecture with an x86 front-end decoder, an innovative architectural approach but one that proved difficult to bring to market on schedule. The delays would prove costly as Intel's Pentium rapidly became the dominant standard for PC performance. AMD also had preliminary work underway on what would become the K6 after its acquisition of startup NexGen—a strategic move to accelerate fifth-generation development. AMD's flash memory business, developed in partnership with Fujitsu through a joint venture, also began contributing meaningful revenue in 1994.	AMD won a landmark jury verdict in the Intel microcode litigation in 1994, legitimizing its x86 clone strategy, while Am486 sales drove revenue to approximately $1.64 billion. The K5 Pentium competitor was in development but running behind schedule, and AMD's partnership with Fujitsu on flash memory began contributing to revenue diversification.	["AMD-Intel Litigation History - Victor John Yannacone Jr. (yannalaw.com)", "