56	AMD	master	entire_history	Advanced Micro Devices has spent over five decades as the semiconductor industry's most persistent underdog—repeatedly challenging giants far larger than itself, surviving near-bankruptcy multiple times, and periodically achieving technological leadership that reshaped how the world computes. Founded in a Sunnyvale garage in 1969 by eight engineers who left Fairchild Semiconductor, AMD built its first decades on a reputation for quality second-source manufacturing before evolving, through legal battles, architectural innovations, and strategic acquisitions, into a full-stack computing company capable of competing at the frontier of AI, data center, and consumer computing.\n\nThe first era of AMD (1969–1982) was defined by Jerry Sanders' high-energy leadership and the company's role as Intel's most important manufacturing partner. AMD went public in 1972, survived the 1974 semiconductor recession without mass layoffs, and parlayed second-source agreements for the Intel 8080, 8085, 8086, and 80286 into rapid revenue growth. The IBM PC's 1981 launch—requiring Intel to second-source its 8086—was AMD's jackpot, making AMD a mandated supplier for the exploding PC market and driving revenue toward $900 million annually by 1983.\n\nThe second era (1984–1993) was defined by the brutal legal conflict with Intel. Intel's refusal to extend second-source rights to the 386 processor sparked a decade of litigation while AMD undertook the near-impossible task of reverse-engineering Intel's processors without source code. The company survived near-bankruptcy, launched the Am386 and Am486 successfully through clean-room reverse-engineering, and emerged from the legal battles with a settlement in 1995 and approximately $1.6 billion in annual revenue—battle-hardened but independent.\n\nThe third era (1994–2006) represented AMD's most creatively productive period. After the disappointing K5, the acquisition of NexGen led to the K6 (1997), which restored AMD's competitive credibility. The K7 Athlon (1999) was AMD's finest hour: the fastest x86 processor on the planet, validating AMD's independent architecture capabilities and powering a revenue surge toward $4.6 billion by 2000. The K8 Athlon 64 and Opteron (2003) introduced AMD64—the 64-bit x86 architecture that the entire industry adopted, forcing Intel to abandon Itanium for general computing. AMD held the CPU performance and server market share crown for the longest sustained period in its history from 2003 to 2006, achieving its highest-ever server market share around 25%.\n\nThe fourth era (2006–2014) was AMD's most turbulent. The $5.4 billion acquisition of ATI Technologies (2006) was strategically visionary but financially crippling, arriving simultaneously with Intel's Core 2 Duo architecture that reversed AMD's CPU performance lead. Years of losses followed, compounded by the troubled Bulldozer CPU architecture (2011), the deepest PC market downturn in decades (2012–2013), and the need to spin off manufacturing operations into GlobalFoundries (2008–2009). AMD survived primarily on its flash memory business, Radeon GPU franchise, and the critical semi-custom SoC wins for PlayStation 4 and Xbox One (2013).\n\nCurrent Strategic Direction\nAMD's fifth era (2014–present) is its most financially successful. Dr. Lisa Su became CEO in October 2014 and executed one of the most celebrated corporate turnarounds in technology history. The Zen CPU architecture—launched in 2017 as Ryzen for consumers and EPYC for servers—matched and then surpassed Intel's per-core performance for the first time in a decade. Successive generations (Zen 2 in 2019, Zen 3 in 2020, Zen 4 in 2022, Zen 5 in 2024) delivered consistent 15-25% generational IPC improvements and drove AMD from roughly 2% server market share to over 20% by 2024. The $49 billion acquisition of Xilinx (2022) added FPGAs and adaptive computing to AMD's portfolio. The MI300X AI accelerator (2023) established AMD as the only credible alternative to NVIDIA for large language model workloads, with Instinct GPU revenue exceeding $5 billion in 2024. AMD reported record annual revenue of $34.6 billion in 2025, driven by Data Center revenue of $16.6 billion, and the company is positioned as a foundational platform for the ongoing artificial intelligence infrastructure build-out.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
57	AMD	yearly	2017	2017 was the year AMD's long-promised turnaround became undeniable reality. The company reported full-year revenue of $5.33 billion, a 25% increase from 2016, driven by the landmark launches of the Ryzen consumer CPU family and the EPYC server processor family—both based on the Zen architecture that AMD had been developing for over four years. The fourth quarter alone delivered $1.48 billion in revenue, up 34% year-over-year. AMD returned to GAAP profitability, posting a net income of $43 million for the year, ending a string of net losses that stretched back to 2011.\n\nThe Ryzen processor launch in March 2017 sent shockwaves through the PC industry. After years of Intel dominance, Ryzen's Zen architecture delivered IPC (instructions-per-clock) performance that was genuinely competitive with Intel's Skylake microarchitecture, at often significantly lower price points. The flagship Ryzen 7 1800X competed directly with Intel's much more expensive Core i7-6900K. Tech reviewers and enthusiasts responded with enormous enthusiasm, and AMD's share of the desktop CPU market began recovering for the first time in years. The mid-range Ryzen 5 lineup proved especially popular, offering exceptional value for gaming and content creation workloads.\n\nIn June 2017, AMD launched EPYC, its first server processor in the Zen family. EPYC 7001 'Naples' offered up to 32 cores per socket and brought competitive per-core performance and memory bandwidth to the datacenter for the first time in years. While EPYC's initial server market share gains were modest—convincing hyperscalers and enterprises to switch from Intel required time—the architecture and ecosystem were validated as credible. AMD's stock reached a 10-year high of $15.61 following strong Q2 2017 earnings, and full-year stock performance was approximately +45%. Lisa Su was recognized as one of Fortune magazine's top executives, and AMD's comeback was cited as one of the most dramatic in semiconductor history.	2017 was AMD's breakthrough comeback year, with the Ryzen CPU and EPYC server processor launches—both based on the Zen architecture—ending Intel's years of unchallenged dominance. Annual revenue surged 25% to $5.33 billion, AMD returned to GAAP profitability with net income of $43 million, and the stock hit a 10-year high as investor confidence in the company's turnaround was fully restored.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2017 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2017 Financial Results - GlobeNewswire", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2017 Financial Results - TechPowerUp", "AMD Stock Price Nears 10 Year High on Ryzen, EPYC Chip Sales - Fortune", "AMD Reports Second Quarter 2017 Financial Results - TechPowerUp", "EPYC - Wikipedia", "AMD EPYC Processors - amd.com", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/811/amd-reports-fourth-quarter-and-annual-2017-financial-results"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
58	AMD	yearly	1991	In March 1991, Advanced Micro Devices released the Am386 processor for general availability, marking a major achievement in the company's competition with Intel. The Am386 was a fully compatible 386-clone developed entirely through reverse-engineering, demonstrating AMD's exceptional engineering capabilities. AMD gained market share despite ongoing legal uncertainty, as customers recognized the value of having an alternative source for 386 processors. The Am386 offered performance comparable to Intel's 386 at more competitive pricing. Intel's October 1991 copyright infringement lawsuit against AMD alleged that AMD had violated copyrights on the 386's logic programming and Intel microcode. The litigation created significant uncertainty regarding AMD's legal right to continue selling the Am386. However, arbitration proceedings were also underway, with AMD hopeful that the arbitrator would rule in its favor based on the breach of the 1982 technology exchange agreement. AMD's stock price experienced volatility as investors considered the legal risks posed by Intel's lawsuits. The company began work on the Am486, the next-generation x86 processor, while simultaneously defending against Intel's legal challenges. AMD's management believed that reverse-engineering was a legitimate competitive strategy that had been validated by the 1990 arbitration finding that Intel had breached the technology exchange agreement.	AMD released the Am386 processor in March 1991, gaining market share despite ongoing legal battles with Intel. The Am386 was a reverse-engineered 386-compatible processor offering competitive pricing.	["Silicon Underground - AMD Am386 Release March 2, 1991", "Wikipedia - Am386", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
59	AMD	yearly	2019	2019 was a landmark year for AMD, confirming the company's resurgence as a genuine technology leader. Annual revenue grew to $6.73 billion, up 4% from 2018, with GAAP operating income of $631 million, GAAP net income of $341 million ($0.30 diluted EPS), and non-GAAP net income of $756 million ($0.64 diluted EPS). The year's results were anchored by two historic product launches: the Ryzen 3000 series consumer CPUs based on the 7nm Zen 2 architecture, and the EPYC 'Rome' server processors—both manufactured on TSMC's 7nm process node, making AMD the first x86 processor company to move to 7nm at scale.\n\nThe Ryzen 3000 'Matisse' processors, launched in July 2019, were a sensation. The Ryzen 9 3900X with 12 cores and 24 threads directly competed with Intel's highest-end consumer CPUs at a fraction of the cost. Zen 2's architectural improvements—including a dramatically enlarged floating-point pipeline, improved cache hierarchy, and chiplet-based design—delivered IPC gains exceeding AMD's own promises. The Ryzen 3000 launch fundamentally disrupted Intel's stranglehold on the high-end consumer CPU market, with AMD gaining the fastest-growing market share of any CPU maker in over a decade. The Computing and Graphics segment delivered Q4 revenue of $1.66 billion, up 69% year-over-year.\n\nEPYC 'Rome' launched in August 2019, offering up to 64 cores per socket and delivering 2x the performance-per-socket over its predecessor 'Naples.' Major cloud providers—including Google Cloud, Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Alibaba Cloud—announced EPYC Rome-based instances, signaling a major breakthrough in AMD's enterprise penetration. The Enterprise, Embedded and Semi-Custom segment saw significantly higher EPYC processor sales throughout the year. AMD's stock rose approximately 148% in 2019, and CEO Lisa Su was named Fortune magazine's Businessperson of the Year—a remarkable recognition for a leader who had inherited a company seemingly on the brink of irrelevance just five years earlier.	2019 was AMD's defining year, with the 7nm Ryzen 3000 and EPYC Rome processors cementing the company's return to genuine CPU leadership. Annual revenue grew to $6.73 billion with GAAP net income of $341 million, AMD's stock rose ~148%, and CEO Lisa Su was named Fortune's Businessperson of the Year—validating one of the semiconductor industry's greatest corporate comebacks.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2019 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2019 Financial Results - GlobeNewswire", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2019 Financial Results - HPCwire", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual 2019 Financial Results - guru3d.com", "AMD Q4 2019 results: raises revenues, margins, and hopes - GraphicSpeak", "Lisa Su Named Fortune Businessperson of the Year 2019 - Fortune", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/930/amd-reports-fourth-quarter-and-annual-2019-financial-results", "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2020/01/28/1976408/0/en/AMD-Reports-Fourth-Quarter-and-Annual-2019-Financial-Results.html"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
60	AMD	five_year	1989-1993	Between 1989 and 1993, AMD fought and largely won its decade-long legal and commercial battle with Intel over x86 processor rights, launching Am386 and Am486 clone processors that captured significant market share and firmly established AMD as Intel's primary x86 competitor. The period ended with AMD generating approximately $1.6 billion in annual revenue, a transformed company that had proven it could design and manufacture competitive microprocessors through its own engineering efforts rather than relying solely on Intel's cooperation.\n\n1989: Lawsuit Filed AMD escalated its legal conflict with Intel by filing a comprehensive lawsuit alleging breach of contract regarding 386 processor rights under the 1982 technology exchange agreement. AMD argued that Intel was contractually obligated to provide second-source rights to all successive processor generations. Parallel arbitration proceedings were also active. Meanwhile, AMD's engineering teams continued the reverse-engineering effort on the 386 and began work on an Am486-compatible design. Revenue from 8086/8088/286 compatible chips remained substantial, funding the company's legal and engineering campaigns.\n\n1990: Am386 Launches Despite Legal Uncertainty AMD launched the Am386 processor—developed entirely through clean-room reverse-engineering of Intel's 386—amid ongoing litigation and without a licensing agreement. Intel immediately countersued, alleging copyright infringement of microcode. The Am386 offered competitive performance with Intel's 386 at lower prices, and AMD rapidly attracted PC manufacturers eager for an alternative x86 supplier. The PC clone market was enormous by 1990, and AMD's Am386 quickly captured meaningful market share even under the cloud of legal uncertainty. AMD's stock and revenue stabilized as the Am386 generated substantial commercial success.\n\n1991: Courtroom Victories Begin The Am386 gained further market acceptance as AMD won key preliminary legal rulings. A California Superior Court arbitration panel ruled in AMD's favor on significant aspects of the 1982 technology exchange agreement, finding Intel had breached certain obligations. While Intel appealed and the battle was far from over, AMD's legal position strengthened meaningfully. The company began shipping Am486 development samples to key customers, preparing for a major commercial launch. AMD's revenue grew on Am386 sales.\n\n1992: Am486 in Development; Partial Legal Resolution Mixed legal developments characterized 1992: courts ruled in AMD's favor on certain microcode copyright questions (finding that AMD had the right to use Intel's microcode in 386-compatible chips), while other aspects of the dispute continued. AMD launched Am486 products for OEM sampling and began building manufacturing capacity for volume production. The legal clarity provided by favorable rulings enabled AMD to market its x86-compatible processors more aggressively, and the company began the Am486's commercial ramp.\n\n1993: Am486 Commercially Launched AMD released the Am486 processor for general commercial availability in April 1993, pricing aggressively—the Am486DX at 40 MHz was priced at or below Intel's 33 MHz part, offering approximately 20% better performance for the same cost. The strategy worked: Am486 revenue reached approximately $250 million and captured around 20% of the 486 market by year-end, as PC manufacturers embraced competitive pricing. Annual revenue reached approximately $1.6 billion—AMD's highest ever to that point. The legal battles with Intel were moving toward settlement, though litigation would formally continue into the mid-1990s. AMD had proven it could survive and compete without Intel's cooperation.\n\n| Year | Key Event | Approximate Revenue |\n|:-----|:----------|:--------------------|\n| 1989 | Intel lawsuit filed; Am386 reverse-engineering complete | ~$1.1B |\n| 1990 | Am386 launched without license; Intel countersues | ~$1.2B |\n| 1991 | Arbitration ruling in AMD's favor on key points | ~$1.2B |\n| 1992 | Am486 sampling; favorable microcode ruling | ~$1.3B |\n| 1993 | Am486 launched commercially; 20% market share | ~$1.6B |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
61	AMD	yearly	2001	2001 was a difficult year for AMD as the dot-com bubble burst, corporate IT spending collapsed, and the September 11 attacks further disrupted the global economy. Annual revenue fell to approximately $3.9 billion—a significant decline from 2000's peak—and AMD swung to a GAAP net loss as demand for PCs softened dramatically across both consumer and enterprise markets. The AMD Fab 30 facility in Dresden came online during 2001, giving AMD additional 180nm manufacturing capacity, but this timing proved unfortunate as demand fell precisely when new capacity became available, compressing utilization rates and margins.\n\nOn the product side, AMD launched the Athlon XP on October 9, 2001, based on the 'Palomino' core. The Athlon XP introduced AMD's new performance rating system, labeling processors with a 'PR' (performance rating) number representing the equivalent Intel Pentium 4 speed they could match—a marketing response to Intel's aggressive MHz clock speed lead with the Pentium 4's Netburst architecture, which ran at very high clocks but achieved lower IPC than the Athlon. The Athlon XP was well-received by enthusiasts and reviewers as offering excellent value versus the Pentium 4. AMD also introduced mobile Athlon 4 processors for laptops. However, Intel's aggressive Pentium 4 ramp and the economic downturn combined to pressure AMD's top-line growth and profitability throughout the year. AMD ended 2001 with significant debt from Fab 30 construction and losses that would accelerate in 2002.	The dot-com bust and the September 11 shock contributed to a painful revenue decline to approximately $3.9 billion for AMD in 2001, resulting in net losses and reduced capacity utilization at the newly opened Fab 30 in Dresden. The Athlon XP launched with a performance rating marketing system to counter Intel's MHz race, but economic conditions limited its commercial impact.	["Athlon - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia", "T