62	AMD	five_year	1974-1978	Between 1974 and 1978, AMD navigated a severe industry-wide recession while simultaneously making the strategic moves that would define its future: a critical second-source agreement with Intel covering the 8080 and 8085 processors, entry into the memory chip market, and the introduction of the Am2900 bit-slice processor family. By the end of this period, AMD had established itself as Intel's most important second-source manufacturing partner and was growing steadily in both scale and product sophistication.\n\n1974: Recession and the Am2900 The semiconductor industry experienced a sharp cyclical downturn in 1974–1975, and AMD was not immune to the headwinds. Despite reduced demand, CEO Jerry Sanders refused to implement the mass layoffs that characterized competitor responses to the recession—instead drawing inspiration from Japanese manufacturing practices and maintaining workforce stability to preserve institutional knowledge and morale. AMD introduced the Am2900 bit-slice processor family in 1974, a technically innovative family of chips that allowed customers to build custom processors by connecting multiple Am2900 "slices." The Am2900 found a strong niche in minicomputers and embedded control systems.\n\n1975: Recovery and Resilience The semiconductor industry began recovering from the 1974 downturn, and AMD's strategy of maintaining its workforce through the downturn paid dividends. The company emerged with intact engineering teams, deepened customer relationships, and renewed energy. AMD continued expanding the Am2900 family and growing its bipolar logic product catalog. Revenue recovered toward pre-recession levels, and AMD began evaluating opportunities in newer semiconductor technologies including MOS memory.\n\n1976: Intel Second-Source Agreement AMD signed a landmark second-source agreement with Intel in 1976, securing rights to manufacture and sell Intel's 8080A microprocessor—one of the most important 8-bit processors of the era and the precursor to the x86 family. This agreement gave AMD access to the heart of the emerging microprocessor market and established a commercial relationship with Intel that would alternately define and bedevil AMD for the next two decades. AMD also entered the RAM and ROM memory market, diversifying beyond logic chips.\n\n1977: Memory and Growth AMD expanded aggressively into the semiconductor memory market in 1977, offering SRAM and ROM products to complement its logic and microprocessor lineup. The company's breadth as a supplier made it an increasingly attractive one-stop shop for electronics manufacturers. Revenue grew steadily and AMD's manufacturing facilities in Sunnyvale were operating at high utilization rates. Jerry Sanders' charismatic leadership style and AMD's unique corporate culture continued to attract top engineering talent.\n\n1978: 8085 Second-Source Rights AMD secured second-source rights to Intel's 8085 microprocessor in 1978, extending and deepening its role as Intel's most important compatible chip manufacturer. By the end of the decade, AMD was manufacturing and selling Intel-compatible processors across the 8008, 8080, and 8085 families. The company's revenue had grown to well over $100 million annually, and AMD was establishing itself as the preeminent second-source supplier in the x86-compatible chip market—a position that was about to become far more valuable when IBM made a fateful choice about its personal computer's processor.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
64	AMD	yearly	2023	2023 was a year of transition and strategic repositioning for AMD, as the company navigated a prolonged PC market downturn and gaming GPU softness while investing heavily in a new frontier: artificial intelligence accelerators. Full-year revenue came in at $22.68 billion, a slight 4% decline from the record $23.60 billion in 2022, reflecting continued weakness in the Client and Gaming segments amid inventory digestion. GAAP net income fell to $854 million due to ongoing Xilinx acquisition amortization and restructuring charges. However, the year's most important development was AMD's emergence as a credible challenger to NVIDIA in the AI/ML accelerator market with the Instinct MI300X GPU.\n\nThe MI300X, launched commercially in Q4 2023, represented AMD's most sophisticated chip to date—integrating compute dies and HBM3 memory in a multi-die package using AMD's advanced 3D chiplet architecture. With 192 GB of HBM3 memory (compared to 80 GB on NVIDIA's H100 SXM), the MI300X offered a compelling advantage for large language model inference workloads where GPU memory capacity was a critical bottleneck. AMD's ROCm software ecosystem also matured significantly, with PyTorch and other major AI frameworks adding improved AMD GPU support. Major hyperscalers and AI companies began evaluating and deploying MI300X systems, with Microsoft Azure, Meta, and others announcing or piloting AMD-powered AI infrastructure.\n\nOn the CPU side, AMD launched EPYC 'Bergamo' in June 2023—a variant of the Genoa platform featuring 128 'Zen 4c' cores per socket (using smaller, more area-efficient cores), specifically designed for cloud-native workloads requiring maximum vCPU density. This gave AMD an answer for hyperscalers running containerized and virtualized workloads at scale. In the client market, AMD launched the Ryzen 7000 series mobile processors (Phoenix and Dragon Range) with on-chip XDNA AI accelerator hardware—an early preview of AMD's AI PC ambitions. AMD's data center segment revenue reached approximately $6.5 billion for the year, setting the stage for a much larger AI-driven expansion in 2024.	2023 saw AMD's revenue dip slightly to $22.68 billion amid a prolonged PC market slump, but the year's defining story was the launch of the MI300X AI accelerator—with its industry-leading 192 GB HBM3 memory capacity—which positioned AMD as the only credible alternative to NVIDIA for large language model inference workloads. EPYC Bergamo with 128 Zen 4c cores also expanded AMD's hyperscale CPU footprint.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Full Year 2023 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations", "AMD Expands Leadership Data Center Portfolio with New EPYC CPUs and MI300 Details - AMD Newsroom", "AMD data center & AI technology premiere 2023 - TechRadar", "AMD Reaffirms EPYC Bergamo CPUs In 1H 2023, Instinct MI300 APUs In 2H 2023 - WCCFTech", "AMD Instinct MI300 Series Accelerators - amd.com", "How The Antares MI300 GPU Ramp Will Save AMD's Datacenter Business - The Next Platform", "AMD Revenue 2012-2025 - MacroTrends", "https://ir.amd.com/financial-information/historical-financials"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
65	AMD	yearly	1997	1997 marked AMD's strongest competitive CPU performance since the Am486 era, with the launch of the K6 processor restoring AMD's credibility as a legitimate alternative to Intel. The K6, largely derived from NexGen's sixth-generation architecture with AMD enhancements including MMX multimedia instruction support, launched in April 1997 and delivered impressive performance: clock-for-clock, it matched the Intel Pentium II in integer workloads while carrying a significantly lower price tag, and it was compatible with existing Socket 7 motherboards—making it an attractive upgrade option for PC builders and system integrators. The K6 proved especially popular in value-segment and sub-$1,000 PC platforms, where AMD's pricing advantage was most compelling.\n\nRevenue rebounded strongly to approximately $2.4 billion as K6 volumes ramped and flash memory continued to contribute. AMD also settled an MMX trademark dispute with Intel in April 1997, officially acknowledging Intel's trademark while securing rights to use the MMX branding on its own K6 MMX processors. Jerry Sanders remained in his characteristic high-visibility role as CEO, promoting AMD's comeback narrative enthusiastically. The K6 was not without limitations—its floating-point performance trailed the Pentium II and its design was limited in scalability to higher clock frequencies—but it represented a critical step in rebuilding AMD's CPU franchise after the K5 disappointments. AMD was already working on the K6-2 (with an enhanced 3DNow! SIMD instruction set) and the K7 'Athlon' long-range architecture that would deliver a true performance crown.	The K6 processor launch in April 1997 restored AMD's competitive credibility, delivering clock-for-clock integer performance matching Intel's Pentium II at significantly lower price points. Revenue rebounded to approximately $2.4 billion, the MMX trademark dispute with Intel was settled, and AMD was developing the enhanced K6-2 and the landmark K7 Athlon architecture.	["The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia", "AMD Processor History - intel-amd-info.blogspot.com", "AMD-Intel Litigation History - Victor John Yannacone Jr.", "The Rise and Fall of AMD - TechSpot"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
66	AMD	yearly	1981	In 1981, IBM introduced the IBM PC using Intel's 8086 processor, and IBM's procurement requirements mandated the use of second-source components. IBM's policy required that critical components have at least two approved manufacturers to ensure supply continuity and create competitive pricing pressure. This requirement proved transformative for AMD, as it meant IBM would purchase 8086 processors from AMD as well as from Intel. In October 1981, AMD and Intel announced a major second-sourcing deal that extended their earlier 1976 agreement. This new arrangement would last for ten years starting in 1982. The IBM PC's explosive success created massive demand for 8086 processors, and AMD was well-positioned to supply this market as an approved second source. This was a pivotal moment in AMD's history, as it meant the company would participate in one of the most important technology developments of the era. AMD's manufacturing facilities were running at or near capacity to meet demand. The combination of IBM's requirement for second sources and the PC's rapid market adoption created unprecedented growth opportunities for AMD.	IBM introduced the IBM PC using Intel's 8086 processor in 1981, requiring second-source components. AMD and Intel announced a major second-sourcing agreement in October 1981, setting the stage for AMD's growth.	["Wikipedia - IBM Personal Computer", "WikiChip - Am8086", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
