69	AMD	yearly	2021	2021 was another record-breaking year for AMD, with annual revenue reaching $16.43 billion—a remarkable 68% increase from 2020—and GAAP net income of $3.16 billion. The growth was broad-based: the Computing and Graphics segment saw exceptional demand for Ryzen processors and Radeon GPUs driven by pandemic-era PC demand, a global GPU shortage, and strong gaming activity, while the Enterprise, Embedded and Semi-Custom segment delivered record revenue of $2.5 billion, up 88% year-over-year, fueled by explosive EPYC server CPU adoption and continued PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series production ramp.\n\nThe EPYC 'Milan' third-generation server processor, based on Zen 3 architecture, launched in March 2021 and became AMD's most commercially successful server platform to date. EPYC 7003 Milan offered up to 64 cores per socket with Zen 3's architectural improvements, and was adopted by all major OEM partners and cloud providers. By end of 2021, AMD's EPYC had surpassed a 10% share of the x86 server CPU market—still modest in absolute terms but a dramatic recovery from near-zero years earlier—and over 70 new AMD-powered cloud instances were launched by hyperscalers throughout the year. AMD's data center CPU revenue growth consistently outpaced the broader server market.\n\nOn the Xilinx acquisition front, AMD and Xilinx spent 2021 navigating the regulatory approval process across multiple jurisdictions, including the United States, European Union, and China. The deal's valuation rose substantially as AMD's stock price appreciated, reaching approximately $49 billion at close. AMD revealed the Zen 4 CPU architecture and 'Genoa' EPYC roadmap at various investor events, and previewed the Radeon RX 6000 series mobile GPUs and expanded desktop lineup. AMD's stock reached an all-time high of approximately $161 in late 2021, giving the company a market capitalization exceeding $250 billion—a staggering rise from its lows of around $2 per share just five years earlier.