85	AMD	yearly	2016	2016 marked the beginning of AMD's financial recovery and the dawn of its most consequential product cycle in over a decade. Annual revenue recovered to $4.27 billion, up 7% from the 2015 trough, with growth in both the Computing and Graphics segment (driven by Polaris GPU sales) and the Enterprise, Embedded and Semi-Custom segment (driven by game console SoC demand). The company significantly narrowed its losses, posting a GAAP net loss of approximately $497 million for the year, but on a non-GAAP basis, operating results were improving as new product cycles gained traction.\n\nThe standout product launch of 2016 was the Radeon RX 480, based on the new 'Polaris' architecture built on GlobalFoundries' 14nm FinFET process—a dramatic jump from the 28nm process AMD had used for years. Launched in June 2016 at $199, the RX 480 offered performance competitive with NVIDIA cards costing significantly more, and became one of AMD's most popular GPU releases in years, particularly in the mainstream gaming market. The broader Radeon RX 400 and 500 series expanded this lineup throughout the year. On the CPU side, AMD made major strides in revealing the Zen architecture publicly—debuting it at the Hot Chips conference and providing the first public demonstrations at Computex 2016. AMD previewed performance claims showing Zen would match or exceed Intel's Broadwell-era CPUs in instructions-per-cycle, a claim that was initially met with skepticism.\n\nAMD's stock staged a dramatic comeback in 2016, rising over 300% from its all-time lows of around $2 to approximately $11 by year-end, making it the best-performing stock in the S&P 500 for the year. This explosion was driven by mounting investor excitement around Ryzen (based on Zen) and Polaris, along with short-covering as the bear thesis on AMD's survival began to collapse. AMD also secured a deal to provide GPU technology for China's National University of Defense Technology, generating some geopolitical buzz.