70	AMD	yearly	1993	In April 1993, Advanced Micro Devices released the Am486 processor for general availability, marking another major achievement in the company's competition with Intel. The first-generation Am486 was introduced with clock frequencies of 25, 33, and 40 MHz. AMD priced the Am486 aggressively, setting the price of the 40 MHz Am486 at or below Intel's price for a 33 MHz chip, offering approximately 20% better performance for the same price. This aggressive pricing strategy helped AMD gain significant market share in the 486 market segment. By year-end 1993, AMD's revenue had reached approximately $1.6 billion, representing the company's largest annual revenue to date. The Am486 contributed significantly to this revenue growth, with estimates suggesting that Am486 revenue reached $250 million and captured approximately 20% of the market by year-end 1993. AMD's manufacturing operations were running at or near capacity to meet demand for Am386 and Am486 processors. Settlement discussions with Intel were actively underway, with both companies recognizing that resolution of their legal disputes would be beneficial. The Am486 launch demonstrated that AMD had successfully transitioned from a second-source manufacturer of Intel's processors to a full competitor capable of designing and manufacturing advanced microprocessors that matched or exceeded Intel's offerings in both performance and value.	AMD shipped Am486 processors in April 1993 with aggressive pricing, capturing approximately 20% market share by year-end. Annual revenue reached approximately $1.6 billion, with Am486 contributing $250 million.	["Wikipedia - Am486", "WikiChip - Am486", "A1 Electronics - AMD CPU Generations"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
71	AMD	yearly	2010	2010 was AMD's strongest financial year in the post-ATI acquisition era, with annual revenue reaching approximately $6.49 billion and the company returning to GAAP profitability. Strong demand for Phenom II processors in the mid-range desktop market, robust GPU sales driven by the Radeon HD 5000 and 6000 series, and continued game console SoC contributions all contributed to the top-line growth. The Radeon HD 6000 'Northern Islands' series launched in Q4 2010, featuring VLIW5 and VLIW4 GPU architectures that offered competitive performance across price points, though NVIDIA's GF104-based GeForce GTX 460 provided stiff competition in the crucial $200 segment.\n\nThe most strategically important development of 2010 was AMD's public announcement and preview of 'Fusion' APUs—processors that integrated x86 CPU cores with AMD Radeon GPU cores and a memory controller on a single die. AMD unveiled the 'Ontario' (codename) low-power Fusion APU for notebooks and the 'Llano' APU for mainstream desktops and laptops, both targeting launch in 2011. The Fusion APU concept represented AMD's clearest answer to Intel's integrated graphics efforts (Sandy Bridge) and was positioned as AMD's primary competitive differentiator in the mainstream PC market. AMD also resolved its antitrust dispute with Intel in November 2009/2010, reaching a settlement in which Intel paid AMD $1.25 billion, cross-licensed patents, and agreed to change certain business practices—a significant vindication of AMD's six-year legal campaign, though financial analysts debated whether the settlement fully reflected AMD's potential damages.	AMD reached approximately $6.49 billion in revenue in 2010 and returned to profitability, driven by Phenom II CPU and Radeon GPU demand. The Fusion APU concept (CPU+GPU on one die) was publicly previewed with Ontario and Llano ahead of 2011 launches, and AMD's six-year antitrust battle with Intel concluded with a landmark $1.25 billion settlement and Intel agreeing to change certain business practices.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations", "AMD - Wikipedia", "AMD's 2010-2011 Roadmaps: Llano APU - AnandTech", "Radeon HD 6000 Series - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD: A Complete Overview - Microchip USA"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
72	AMD	yearly	2003	2003 was a year of dramatic technical vindication for AMD, marked by the launch of two processors that fundamentally changed the trajectory of the x86 computing industry. On April 22, 2003, AMD launched the Opteron—the world's first x86-64 processor, built on the K8 architecture. The Opteron's defining innovation was AMD64: native 64-bit computing extensions fully backward-compatible with existing 32-bit x86 software, available on a processor that could be deployed immediately in standard servers without requiring software recompilation. This was in stark contrast to Intel's competing IA-64 (Itanium) strategy, which required 64-bit software to be rewritten from scratch. The industry response was enormously positive: server vendors including HP, Sun, and IBM began qualifying Opteron-based systems, and Linux distributions quickly added AMD64 support.\n\nOn September 23, 2003, AMD launched the Athlon 64—the consumer version of the K8 architecture—becoming the first 64-bit processor targeted at mainstream PC users. The Athlon 64 also introduced an integrated memory controller (eliminating the separate north bridge and dramatically reducing memory latency), HyperTransport interconnect technology, and a Cool'n'Quiet power management feature. Performance was exceptional: the Athlon 64 regularly defeated Intel's Pentium 4 in both 32-bit and 64-bit benchmarks at equivalent price points. Revenue recovered to approximately $3.1 billion as these launches reignited customer and investor enthusiasm. Microsoft announced it would add AMD64 support to Windows XP Professional and Windows Server 2003 x64 Edition, validating AMD's architectural bet and putting enormous pressure on Intel's Itanium-centric 64-bit strategy.	2003 was AMD's most architecturally significant year in a decade: the Opteron server processor (April) and Athlon 64 consumer CPU (September) introduced AMD64—the first practical, backward-compatible 64-bit x86 architecture—instantly obsoleting Intel's Itanium strategy. Revenue recovered to approximately $3.1 billion, and Microsoft's commitment to AMD64 support validated AMD's industry-reshaping bet.	["Opteron - Wikipedia", "Athlon 64 - Wikipedia", "An AMD64 Platform Primer - CPUplanet", "Opteron - AMD - WikiChip", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
73	AMD	yearly	2025	2025 was a record-breaking year for AMD across virtually all financial metrics, with total annual revenue growing 34% to $34.639 billion—the company's highest ever—demonstrating the sustained breadth of demand for AMD's computing portfolio. GAAP operating income reached $3.7 billion, net income was $4.3 billion, and diluted earnings per share came in at $2.65. The performance was driven by two dominant segments: Data Center (up 32% year-over-year to $16.6 billion) and a combined Client and Gaming segment (up 51% to $14.6 billion), which reflected surging demand for Ryzen processors across notebooks, desktops, and semi-custom console SoCs alongside AMD's expanded gaming GPU presence.\n\nIn the data center, the transition from MI300X to the MI350 Series Instinct accelerators accelerated in 2025, with the MI350 offering significant performance improvements for AI inference workloads and expanded memory capacity. EPYC Turin (Zen 5) adoption accelerated further throughout the year, with AMD capturing growing share of the x86 server market as hyperscale cloud providers expanded their AMD-based infrastructure. By mid-2025, AMD's EPYC CPUs had crossed meaningful share thresholds with several major cloud providers, and AMD's data center revenue per quarter set new records multiple times throughout the year. AMD also previewed the MI400 series (CDNA 4 architecture) and the next-generation 'Venice' EPYC platform (Zen 6) at major conferences, with early customer engagement described as very strong.\n\nOn the client side, AMD expanded its Ryzen AI product family throughout 2025, releasing Ryzen AI 300 PRO series for commercial notebooks and Ryzen AI 9 HX variants for high-performance mobile workstations. The AI PC category grew significantly across the industry, and AMD's early investment in on-chip NPU technology positioned it favorably alongside Intel and Qualcomm. AMD also made strides in its Embedded (Xilinx) recovery, with FPGA and adaptive SoC demand recovering as customers depleted their excess inventory. CEO Lisa Su continued to articulate AMD's long-term ambition to capture a share of what AMD described as a $500 billion AI compute opportunity, emphasizing its open ROCm software ecosystem as a key differentiator against NVIDIA's proprietary CUDA platform.	2025 was AMD's best year ever by every major financial metric, with revenue growing 34% to $34.639 billion, led by Data Center revenue of $16.6 billion including MI350 Instinct GPU deployments and accelerating EPYC Turin server adoption. The Client and Gaming segment also surged 51% as Ryzen AI processors drove strong PC demand, establishing AMD as the premier AI computing platform alternative to NVIDIA.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Full Year 2025 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD (NASDAQ: AMD) revises 10-K as 2025 revenue hits $34.6B - Stock Titan", "AMD reports record 2025 revenues, driven by strong demand for EPYC and Ryzen CPUs - TechSpot", "AMD posts record data center revenue for Q3 2025, as compute demand shows no signs of slowing - Data Center Dynamics", "AMD Q3 FY 2025 Earnings: Broad-Based Compute Momentum - Futurum", "AMD Reports Third Quarter 2025 Financial Results - amd.com", "AMD Unveils Strategy to Lead the $1 Trillion Compute Market - AMD Investor Relations", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/1276/amd-reports-fourth-quarter-and-full-year-2025-financial-results"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
74	AMD	yearly	2007	2007 was a year of mounting financial difficulty for AMD, as the company struggled under the weight of ATI acquisition debt while Intel's Core 2 architecture continued to expand its performance lead over AMD's K10 (Phenom) processors, which arrived later than anticipated and with a significant bug. Full-year revenue was approximately $6.0 billion—the first full year including ATI—but operating losses were severe, approaching $3 billion for the year when including ATI goodwill impairments and restructuring charges. AMD's stock fell substantially, erasing much of the value that had built up during the Athlon 64 era.\n\nThe most damaging product event of the year was the troubled launch of AMD's 'Barcelona' native quad-core processor (commercially branded EPYC Phenom X4 for desktop and the 3rd generation Opteron for servers) in September-November 2007. While Barcelona was technically innovative—the first true native quad-core x86 processor with all four cores on a single die, shared L3 cache, and other K10 improvements—it launched at lower-than-expected clock speeds due to manufacturing difficulties at 65nm, making it less competitive with Intel's Core 2 Quad than anticipated. Worse, a 'TLB Bug' (Translation Lookaside Buffer erratum) was discovered that caused stability issues in certain workloads, requiring a BIOS workaround that significantly impacted server performance. The bug further damaged Barcelona's reputation in the enterprise market. AMD's GPU business (inherited from ATI) performed reasonably well with the Radeon HD 2000 and 3000 series, but NVIDIA's competing products remained strong. The year ended with AMD CEO Hector Ruiz announcing his decision to step down, with Dirk Meyer named as his successor.	2007 was a deeply troubled year for AMD, with the Barcelona/Phenom K10 processor arriving late, at disappointing clock speeds, and plagued by a critical TLB bug that hurt enterprise adoption. Despite approximately $6.0 billion in full-year revenue including ATI, massive operating losses from goodwill impairments and ATI integration costs sent AMD's stock plummeting, culminating in CEO Hector Ruiz announcing his departure.	["AMD's Quad-Core Barcelona: Defending New Territory - AnandTech", "AMD - Wikipedia", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations", "The History of AMD: A Complete Overview - Microchip USA", "The Rise and Fall of AMD - TechSpot"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
75	AMD	yearly	1989	In 1989, Advanced Micro Devices filed a major lawsuit against Intel for breach of contract, alleging that Intel had violated the terms of their 1982 technology exchange agreement by failing to grant AMD rights to the 80386 processor. AMD's lawsuit claimed that Intel was obligated under the agreement to grant AMD second-source rights to all successive generations of the x86 processor architecture, not just the 80186 and 80286. The company continued its reverse-engineering efforts on the 386 despite the legal dispute. AMD also engaged in parallel arbitration proceedings to resolve the contract disputes with Intel regarding the scope and applicability of the technology exchange agreement. The dispute centered on complex questions of contract interpretation, intellectual property rights, and fair competition in the semiconductor industry. AMD's management maintained confidence that the company could successfully create a 386-compatible processor through reverse-engineering and that AMD would ultimately prevail in the legal disputes with Intel. The company continued to generate substantial revenue from its existing 8086, 8088, 80186, and 80286 compatible processors. The legal battle would consume significant management attention and legal resources for years to come, but AMD remained committed to becoming a full competitor to Intel in the x86 processor market.	AMD filed a major lawsuit against Intel in 1989 alleging breach of contract regarding 386 processor rights. The company continued reverse-engineering efforts while engaged in parallel arbitration with Intel.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "Harvard Journal of Law & Technology - Intel and the x86 Architecture", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
76	AMD	five_year	1979-1983	Between 1979 and 1983, AMD's fortunes were transformed by a single historic event: IBM's selection of Intel's 8086 processor for its personal computer, which IBM required Intel to second-source—creating an obligation that made AMD's Intel-compatible manufacturing capability extraordinarily valuable. By the end of this period, AMD was manufacturing x86-compatible processors at massive scale and had signed a comprehensive 10-year technology exchange agreement with Intel, providing contractual rights to the 80286. AMD's revenue grew from approximately $125 million to over $900 million in just five years.\n\n1979: NYSE Listing and Austin Expansion AMD listed on the New York Stock Exchange (upgrading from its earlier market), providing enhanced access to capital for continued expansion. The company established a major new semiconductor fabrication plant in Austin, Texas, reflecting the need for additional manufacturing capacity and geographic diversification. AMD's negotiations with Intel about second-source rights for next-generation processors continued, and the company's Austin facility would become one of its most important long-term manufacturing centers, eventually hosting R&D operations as well.\n\n1980: Manufacturing the 8086 AMD began manufacturing Intel 8086-compatible chips in 1980, entering the 16-bit microprocessor market. The 8086 had been designed by Intel as a 16-bit successor to the 8080/8085, and while it had not yet achieved the dominant market position it would attain after the IBM PC launch, AMD was positioning itself as a critical second-source. This entry into 16-bit processors represented a significant technological step up from AMD's prior work and demonstrated the company's manufacturing sophistication.\n\n1981: The IBM PC and the Second-Source Imperative IBM launched the IBM Personal Computer in August 1981 powered by Intel's 8088 processor (an 8-bit external bus variant of the 8086), instantly creating enormous market demand for compatible x86 chips. IBM, as a matter of corporate purchasing policy, required dual-source supply for critical components. Intel was obligated to license the 8086/8088 architecture to AMD, making AMD a mandated supplier for the rapidly growing PC market. AMD ramped production of 8086 and 8088 clones dramatically, and revenue surged as PC manufacturers flooded into the market.\n\n1982: The 10-Year Technology Exchange Agreement In 1982, AMD and Intel signed a comprehensive 10-year technology exchange agreement that gave AMD explicit rights to manufacture Intel's 80286 processor and established a framework for mutual cross-licensing of processor-related technologies. The agreement was a milestone for AMD: it provided contractual certainty for continued x86-compatible manufacturing and briefly appeared to guarantee AMD's participation in successive generations of Intel's processor roadmap. Revenue grew substantially as 286-era PC manufacturing scaled up. AMD's revenue had grown to approximately $600 million annually.\n\n1983: The 286 Era and Peak Second-Source AMD began manufacturing Am286 processors in 1983, timed to meet the market's transition from 8086/8088 to 286-based PC systems. The PC market was exploding: IBM PC clones from Compaq and countless Asian manufacturers drove demand for x86 processors to unprecedented levels, and AMD's manufacturing capacity was running at full tilt. Annual revenue reached approximately $900 million, an extraordinary growth trajectory from just four years earlier. However, storm clouds were forming: Intel was already working on the 80386, and growing tensions with AMD about whether the 10-year agreement covered future processor generations were becoming apparent.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
77	AMD	five_year	2014-2018	Between 2014 and 2018, AMD navigated one of the most dramatic corporate near-death experiences and subsequent comebacks in semiconductor history. Beginning the period as a loss-making company trading near all-time lows with an uncertain future, AMD ended 2018 as a profitable, technically credible challenger to both Intel and NVIDIA—anchored by a new CPU architecture (Zen) that would define the industry for years.\n\n2014: Lisa Su Takes the Helm AMD posted full-year revenue of approximately $5.51 billion, buoyed by semi-custom SoC demand from the Sony PS4 and Microsoft Xbox One ramps, but a full-year GAAP net loss of $403 million exposed the fragility of the PC processor business. In October 2014, Dr. Lisa Su was appointed President and CEO, replacing Rory Read. Her immediate priorities: simplify the roadmap, increase R&D investment, and bet the company on Zen and next-generation graphics architectures. The Kaveri APU—AMD's most HSA-compliant processor to date—launched to modest acclaim.\n\n2015: The Dark Valley Revenue collapsed 28% to $3.99 billion and the stock hit an all-time low near $1.61 per share, with the net loss widening to $660 million. AMD was widely written off by investors and analysts. Amid the gloom, however, AMD launched the world's first HBM-powered graphics card—the Radeon R9 Fury X—and formally announced the ground-up Zen CPU architecture, planting the seeds of its recovery. Su's restructuring consolidated AMD's business segments and eliminated non-essential spending.\n\n2016: The First Signs of Revival Revenue recovered to $4.27 billion (up 7%) and AMD's stock surged over 300% during the year—making it the best-performing stock in the S&P 500—driven by investor excitement ahead of the Zen and Polaris launches. The Radeon RX 480 (Polaris architecture, 14nm FinFET) launched at $199 and proved highly competitive against NVIDIA's offerings in the mainstream gaming market. AMD provided its first public Zen architecture demonstrations, with performance claims that the market initially viewed with skepticism.\n\n2017: The Comeback Proven Revenue surged 25% to $5.33 billion and AMD returned to GAAP profitability with net income of $43 million. The Ryzen consumer CPU line (Zen architecture) launched in March and fundamentally disrupted Intel's dominance of the desktop CPU market, delivering competitive multi-threaded and gaming performance at dramatically lower prices. EPYC Naples server CPUs launched in June, beginning AMD's long campaign to recapture x86 server market share from Intel. AMD's stock hit a 10-year high and Lisa Su became one of the most celebrated executives in Silicon Valley.\n\n2018: Scale and Volatility Revenue grew 23% to $6.48 billion with GAAP net income of $337 million, as Ryzen 2000 and EPYC Naples continued to ramp. A cryptocurrency mining boom in H1 supercharged GPU demand, while the subsequent crypto crash created an inventory overhang in H2. AMD previewed the 7nm Zen 2 'Rome' EPYC architecture and Radeon RX Vega launched for high-end gaming, though it faced stiff competition from NVIDIA's Pascal-generation products. AMD's stock was volatile, reaching a high of ~$34 before retreating to ~$18 by year-end.\n\n| Year | Revenue | Net Income / (Loss) | Key Architecture |\n|:-----|:--------|:--------------------|:----------------|\n| 2014 | ~$5.51B | ($403M) | Kaveri APU, GCN |\n| 2015 | $3.99B  | ($660M) | Fury X / HBM, Zen announced |\n| 2016 | $4.27B  | (~$497M loss) | Polaris (14nm) |\n| 2017 | $5.33B  | $43M | Zen (Ryzen + EPYC Naples) |\n| 2018 | $6.48B  | $337M | Zen+ (Ryzen 2000), Vega |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
78	AMD	yearly	2006	2006 was a year of enormous ambition and significant risk for AMD, defined by its landmark acquisition of ATI Technologies—the Canadian GPU and graphics chipset maker—for approximately $5.4 billion in cash and stock. The deal, announced July 24, 2006 and completed October 25, 2006, was by far the largest in AMD's history and immediately doubled the company's headcount and engineering footprint. AMD's rationale was to create an integrated semiconductor company capable of delivering processors, graphics, and platform-level solutions that could challenge Intel more broadly—particularly as Intel moved into integrated graphics and platform-level competition. The acquisition also brought ATI's Radeon discrete GPU line, CrossFire multi-GPU technology, and extensive mobile graphics IP.\n\nHowever, 2006 also marked the arrival of Intel's Core 2 Duo (Conroe) architecture in July—a devastating product that dramatically reversed AMD's performance leadership. Core 2 Duo delivered 20-40% better performance than the Athlon 64 X2 at equivalent clock speeds, with superior power efficiency. Intel's architectural resurgence, combined with aggressive pricing, rapidly eroded AMD's consumer CPU market share gains. AMD's revenue for the full year was approximately $5.6 billion (including a partial year of ATI revenue post-October close), but operating losses began to mount as Intel's competitive pressure intensified and AMD grappled with the financial burden of the ATI acquisition debt. Q4 2006 alone saw an operating loss of $527 million. The AMD-ATI merger was strategically visionary but financially risky, arriving precisely as Intel reversed the competitive situation dramatically.	2006 was AMD's most consequential strategic year: the $5.4 billion acquisition of ATI Technologies created an integrated CPU+GPU semiconductor company, but Intel's simultaneous launch of the Core 2 Duo (Conroe) architecture handed AMD a devastating competitive setback. Revenue was approximately $5.6 billion including partial ATI contribution, but operating losses began as the combined debt load and Intel's resurgence created serious financial pressure.	["ATI Technologies - Wikipedia", "AMD & ATI: The Acquisition from all Points of View - AnandTech", "ATI's Acquisition To AMD - WCCFTech", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
79	AMD	five_year	1999-2003	Between 1999 and 2003, AMD experienced its most dramatic boom-and-bust cycle to that point, riding the Athlon K7's performance supremacy to record revenues before the dot-com collapse and Intel's competitive response sent the company into its darkest financial years since the 1980s. The period ended with AMD's most architecturally consequential product ever: the Opteron and Athlon 64, which introduced AMD64 64-bit computing to the industry.\n\n1999: The Athlon Arrives; Performance Crown Won The June 23 launch of the Athlon K7 was AMD's finest product achievement: benchmarks confirmed it was the fastest x86 processor on the planet, besting Intel's Pentium III by 10-20% in key workloads with a clean-sheet architecture featuring a true out-of-order engine, 128-bit FPU, and Alpha EV6 bus. Revenue grew to approximately $2.9 billion. AMD's stock surged and the company entered a new era as a genuine peer to Intel rather than a subordinate second-source supplier.\n\n2000: Peak Revenue; Athlon Dominates The dot-com boom and explosive PC market growth drove AMD to approximately $4.6 billion in revenue—a 59% surge—as Athlon Thunderbird (180nm) dominated performance charts and the Duron conquered the value segment. AMD's highest CPU market share in years was achieved. Fab 30 in Dresden broke ground to meet surging demand, and AMD appeared poised for a sustained period of financial strength.\n\n2001: Dot-Com Bust; Athlon XP The dot-com bubble's collapse, September 11, and corporate IT spending cuts drove revenue down to approximately $3.9 billion and pushed AMD to a net loss. The Athlon XP launched with a performance rating system to counter Intel's Pentium 4 GHz marketing, and while the product was technically solid, the economic environment sharply constrained its commercial impact. Fab 30's completion added capacity precisely as demand fell.\n\n2002: The Valley Floor Revenue collapsed to approximately $2.7 billion and AMD posted severe losses as Intel gained back server and desktop market share and the post-dot-com recession deepened. AMD's balance sheet strained under Fab 30 debt. Quietly, the K8 architecture with AMD64 64-bit extensions was in advanced development—AMD's biggest architectural bet in years and a direct challenge to Intel's Itanium 64-bit strategy.\n\n2003: AMD64 Changes the Industry The Opteron (April) and Athlon 64 (September) launched as the world's first x86-64 processors: natively 64-bit yet fully backward-compatible with all 32-bit x86 software, requiring no recompilation. The industry immediately recognized AMD64 as the superior 64-bit computing path versus Intel's Itanium. Microsoft committed to AMD64 support in Windows. Revenue recovered to approximately $3.1 billion, and AMD had permanently reshaped the trajectory of computing architecture.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
80	AMD	yearly	1985	In 1985, Advanced Micro Devices achieved a major milestone by being included on the Fortune 500 list for the first time, recognizing its status as a major U.S. corporation. However, 1985 also presented significant challenges. The semiconductor industry experienced a severe downturn, driven partly by aggressive trade practices from Japanese manufacturers (known as 'dumping') and partly by oversupply and lack of innovation in the U.S. semiconductor market. AMD implemented workforce reductions and cost-cutting measures to weather the downturn. Simultaneously, Intel cut off AMD from access to the technical specifications and source code for the 386 processor, preventing AMD from easily creating a compatible processor. Without access to Intel's detailed technical documentation and microcode, AMD would need to reverse-engineer the 386, a task that would require substantial investment and several years of engineering effort. The Fortune 500 achievement validated AMD's status as a major industrial company, but the combination of industry recession, Intel's refusal to license the 386, and the need to undertake massive reverse-engineering efforts created a challenging period for the company.	AMD made the Fortune 500 list for the first time in 1985, but faced a severe semiconductor industry downturn. Intel cut off AMD from 386 technical specifications, forcing future reverse-engineering efforts.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "History of Advanced Micro Devices - FundingUniverse", "Harvard Journal of Law & Technology - Intel and the x86 Architecture"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
81	AMD	yearly	1992	In 1992, the legal battle between AMD and Intel continued to dominate the company's relationship with its biggest rival, but events began to move in AMD's favor. During 1992, a neutral arbitrator issued rulings in AMD's favor on several counts regarding the 1982 technology exchange agreement and Intel's obligations to grant AMD rights to the 386 processor. These favorable arbitration decisions strengthened AMD's legal position and gave the company greater confidence in its right to continue selling the Am386 and to develop the next-generation Am486. AMD began development and early production of the Am486 processor, the next-generation x86 clone that would compete directly with Intel's i486. The Am486 would be more advanced than the 386, incorporating higher clock speeds and additional features. AMD continued to gain market share with the Am386 as customers appreciated the alternative source and competitive pricing. The litigation between AMD and Intel, while ongoing, appeared increasingly likely to be resolved in AMD's favor based on the arbitration findings. AMD's stock price generally moved upward during 1992 as investors gained confidence in the company's legal position and its ability to continue competing with Intel. Settlement discussions between the two companies began, though a final settlement would not be reached until 1995.	AMD continued development of the Am486 processor in 1992 as court rulings began favoring AMD in its disputes with Intel. Settlement discussions between AMD and Intel began during this period.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "Harvard Journal of Law & Technology - Intel and the x86 Architecture", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
82	AMD	yearly	2002	2002 was AMD's most financially strained year since the late 1980s, with revenue declining sharply to approximately $2.7 billion as the post-dot-com technology sector recession deepened and AMD lost further CPU market share to Intel. The company posted a substantial GAAP net loss and was forced to implement cost-cutting measures including workforce reductions and reduced capital expenditures. The debt burden from the Fab 30 Dresden construction—incurred during the boom years—weighed heavily on the balance sheet. AMD's stock fell to multi-year lows amid investor concern about the company's long-term viability.\n\nDespite the financial pressure, AMD's engineering teams made quiet but pivotal progress during 2002 that would define the next several years of the industry. The K8 architecture—which would launch as Opteron and Athlon 64—was in advanced development, and AMD was preparing to make a fundamental strategic bet: introducing native 64-bit extensions (AMD64, later standardized as x86-64) to its processors while maintaining backward compatibility with 32-bit x86 software. This architectural decision, if adopted by the market, would allow AMD to leapfrog Intel's competing 64-bit strategy (Itanium, which required recompiling software) and offer the industry a more pragmatic 64-bit computing path. Microsoft's eventual support for AMD64 in Windows XP and Server 2003 would validate the bet. AMD also exited the mobile Duron market and focused its flash memory operations through the Fujitsu joint venture.	2002 was AMD's hardest financial year in over a decade, with revenue falling to approximately $2.7 billion and significant net losses as the post-dot-com recession and Intel competition eroded market position. Behind the scenes, the K8 architecture with AMD64 64-bit extensions was in advanced development, preparing for what would become one of AMD's greatest technical achievements.	["AMD - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "Opteron - Wikipedia", "The Rise and Fall of AMD - TechSpot", "AMD Annual Report 2002"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
83	AMD	yearly	2020	2020 was AMD's most successful year in its history to that point, with annual revenue surging to $9.76 billion—a 45% increase from 2019—and GAAP net income of $2.49 billion. The extraordinary growth was driven by an unprecedented combination of consumer CPU and GPU demand, accelerating EPYC enterprise adoption, and surging semi-custom revenue from the next-generation Sony PlayStation 5 and Microsoft Xbox Series X|S consoles, which both launched in November 2020. The pandemic-driven acceleration of remote work, cloud computing, and PC purchases significantly benefited AMD's product portfolio.\n\nOn the CPU side, AMD launched the Ryzen 5000 series 'Vermeer' processors in November 2020, based on the Zen 3 architecture. Ryzen 5000 was a historic milestone: the Ryzen 9 5900X and 5950X became definitively the fastest gaming CPUs on the market—a title Intel had held for many years—and also topped multi-threaded performance benchmarks. The Zen 3 architecture achieved a 19% IPC improvement over Zen 2 and unified the compute die into a single 8-core complex, dramatically improving latency. Demand immediately outstripped supply, with AMD processors selling out at retailers across the globe.\n\nFor GPUs, AMD launched the Radeon RX 6000 series 'Big Navi' in November 2020, based on the RDNA 2 architecture. The RX 6800 XT and 6900 XT finally offered AMD performance parity—and in some cases superiority—with NVIDIA's flagship RTX 3080 and 3090 at competitive prices. RDNA 2 also powered the GPUs inside both next-gen consoles. AMD made its single biggest strategic move of the era in October 2020, announcing a definitive agreement to acquire Xilinx—the leading FPGA manufacturer—in an all-stock transaction originally valued at approximately $35 billion, which would become the largest semiconductor acquisition in history at the time of close. The deal was designed to dramatically expand AMD's addressable market in adaptive computing, networking, and data center infrastructure.	2020 was a transformative year for AMD, with revenue surging 45% to $9.76 billion and net income of $2.49 billion. The Ryzen 5000 (Zen 3) CPUs became the fastest gaming processors on the market, RDNA 2 brought GPU parity with NVIDIA, next-gen PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series consoles launched with AMD silicon, and the company announced its landmark $35 billion acquisition of Xilinx.	["AMD Reports Record Revenue and $35B Deal to Buy Xilinx - HPCwire", "AMD Crushes Earnings With Best Year Ever - HotHardware", "AMD's Ryzen and EPYC Sales Punish Intel, Posts Record Revenue in Q3 Earnings - Tom's Hardware", "AMD in $35 Billion All-Stock Acquisition of Xilinx - AnandTech", "AMD Acquires Xilinx - amd.com", "AMD Q3 2020 results - GraphicSpeak", "AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Full Year 2020 Financial Results - AMD Investor Relations", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/1047/amd-completes-acquisition-of-xilinx"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
84	AMD	yearly	1995	1995 was a landmark year for AMD's legal history: in January, AMD and Intel reached a comprehensive global settlement ending all current outstanding litigation between the companies—covering the Technology Agreement Arbitration, the 286/386/486 Microcode cases, the Antitrust Litigation, and the Intel Business Interference case. The settlement removed a decade-long cloud of legal uncertainty from AMD's CPU business and freed management to focus more fully on products and strategy. AMD agreed to certain limitations on future x86 microcode usage, while Intel acknowledged AMD's right to manufacture and sell its existing product lines.\n\nOn the product side, AMD launched the Am5x86 processor—a 486-class chip running at 133MHz and delivering performance benchmarks comparable to a Pentium 75 despite being officially a 486-generation product. The Am5x86 found a strong market among cost-conscious buyers upgrading from 486-based systems. Revenue grew substantially, reaching approximately $2.4 billion as Am486, Am5x86, and flash memory products all contributed. AMD also completed its $857 million acquisition of NexGen in January 1995, bringing NexGen's CPU engineering team and technology in-house. NexGen had been developing a highly regarded sixth-generation x86 processor architecture that would eventually be refined into the K6—AMD's best hope for competing with Intel's Pentium and Pentium II. The NexGen acquisition was the largest in AMD's history and marked a decisive bet on internal CPU design capabilities.	January 1995 brought two defining events: a comprehensive legal settlement with Intel ending a decade of litigation, and AMD's $857 million acquisition of NexGen to accelerate sixth-generation CPU development. The Am5x86 processor launched at 133MHz, revenue reached approximately $2.4 billion, and the groundwork was laid for the competitive K6 processor.	["AMD-Intel Litigation History - Victor John Yannacone Jr. (yannalaw.com)", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia", "AMD vs Intel - Diffen", "The Rise and Fall of AMD - TechSpot"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
86	AMD	yearly	1987	In 1987, Advanced Micro Devices achieved significant milestones despite ongoing challenges. AMD launched the Am29000 RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer) processor, representing the company's entry into the RISC processor market. The Am29000 was announced in March 1987 and would be released for general availability in May 1988. The processor was designed based on the influential Berkeley RISC architecture and offered high-performance computing capabilities for embedded applications. AMD's revenue reached approximately $1.2 billion in 1987, a significant achievement reflecting the company's substantial size and profitability. The revenue growth was driven primarily by continued strong sales of 8086, 8088, 80186, and 80286 compatible processors, which were in high demand for IBM PC-compatible computers and embedded systems applications. AMD continued its reverse-engineering efforts on the 386 processor, making steady progress toward creating a fully compatible clone. The Am29000 represented AMD's diversification strategy, positioning the company to compete in the emerging RISC processor market that was growing rapidly in workstations and server applications. AMD's stock price continued to appreciate, reflecting investor confidence in the company's growth trajectory and profitability.	AMD achieved revenue of approximately $1.2 billion in 1987 and announced the Am29000 RISC processor in March 1987. The Am29000 was designed for high-performance embedded applications.	["Wikipedia - AMD Am29000", "A1 Electronics - AMD CPU Generations", "History of Advanced Micro Devices - FundingUniverse"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
87	AMD	yearly	1982	In February 1982, AMD formally executed a major 10-year technology exchange and cross-licensing agreement with Intel, becoming a licensed second-source manufacturer of the Intel 8086 and 8088 processors. This landmark agreement gave AMD explicit rights to manufacture Intel's microprocessor designs and access to Intel's microcode. AMD began bulk production of the 8086 in 1982, capitalizing on IBM's mandate for second-source components and the explosive demand for IBM PC-compatible computers. The agreement was crucial because it provided AMD with the legal framework to manufacture Intel-compatible processors and resolved potential intellectual property disputes. The deal also covered the Intel 80286 processor, ensuring AMD would have second-source rights to the next generation of Intel's x86 architecture. This agreement transformed AMD from a manufacturer of Intel-licensed chips into a true competitor in the microprocessor market. Revenue grew significantly during 1982 as AMD ramped up 8086 and 8088 production. The company's Austin, Texas fabrication plant became crucial in meeting the massive demand for IBM PC-compatible processors.	AMD signed a 10-year technology exchange agreement with Intel in February 1982, becoming a licensed second-source manufacturer of the 8086 and 8088 processors. AMD began bulk production immediately.	["WikiChip - Am8086", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD", "Intel & AMD: The First 30 Years - Asianometry"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
88	AMD	yearly	1979	In 1979, Advanced Micro Devices achieved another significant milestone by listing on the New York Stock Exchange, reflecting the company's growth from a startup into an established publicly traded semiconductor manufacturer. AMD also established a new semiconductor fabrication plant in Austin, Texas, representing a major capital investment and demonstrating confidence in the company's future growth. This expansion was necessary to meet growing demand from customers and to support AMD's strategy of becoming a major second-source manufacturer for Intel and other chipmakers. The Austin facility would become one of AMD's most important manufacturing centers. During this period, AMD was negotiating with Intel regarding second-source rights for more advanced processors, as the company sought to expand beyond the 8080 and 8085 into newer x86 processors. The semiconductor industry continued to grow during the late 1970s, driven by the rapid expansion of personal computers, minicomputers, and increased demand for microprocessor-based systems. AMD's expansion and new manufacturing capacity positioned the company well for the explosive growth of the semiconductor industry in the 1980s.	AMD established a new semiconductor fabrication plant in Austin, Texas in 1979. The company continued negotiations with Intel regarding second-source rights for advanced processors.	["Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD", "Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "History of Advanced Micro Devices - FundingUniverse"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
89	AMD	five_year	2019-2023	Between 2019 and 2023, AMD transformed from a recovering challenger into a multi-segment technology powerhouse, executing the industry's most consequential product refresh cycle since Intel's Pentium era. The period encompassed AMD's return to genuine CPU and GPU leadership, a landmark $49 billion acquisition of Xilinx, and an early but decisive pivot toward artificial intelligence computing infrastructure.\n\n2019: 7nm Proves Everything Revenue grew to $6.73 billion with GAAP net income of $341 million and AMD's stock surged approximately 148% for the year. The Ryzen 3000 series (Zen 2, 7nm TSMC) and EPYC 'Rome' (also 7nm, up to 64 cores) launched to universal acclaim, firmly establishing AMD's technical leadership in high-performance x86 computing. EPYC Rome won early major cloud deployments across AWS, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, and Alibaba. CEO Lisa Su was named Fortune magazine's Businessperson of the Year, cementing the narrative of one of tech's greatest corporate turnarounds.\n\n2020: Records Shattered; Xilinx Deal Announced Revenue leapt 45% to $9.76 billion with GAAP net income of $2.49 billion as pandemic-era PC demand, console SoC launches (PS5, Xbox Series X|S), and aggressive Ryzen/EPYC uptake supercharged results. The Ryzen 5000 (Zen 3) CPUs became the fastest gaming processors on the market, ending Intel's gaming performance crown. RDNA 2 GPUs delivered parity with NVIDIA in several high-end benchmarks. In October 2020, AMD announced a $35 billion all-stock acquisition of Xilinx, the leading FPGA manufacturer, in what would become the largest semiconductor deal in history.\n\n2021: Hypergrowth Continues Revenue surged another 68% to $16.43 billion with GAAP net income of $3.16 billion as all business lines fired simultaneously. EPYC 'Milan' (Zen 3 server) became AMD's most commercially successful server CPU, capturing growing hyperscale cloud deployments and pushing AMD's x86 server CPU share past 10% for the first time in over a decade. AMD's stock peaked at an all-time high of approximately $161 per share, giving the company a market cap exceeding $250 billion. Regulatory approvals for the Xilinx acquisition were pursued across the US, EU, and China.\n\n2022: Transformation at Scale Revenue grew 44% to $23.60 billion—AMD's first-ever $20+ billion year—as AMD completed the $49 billion Xilinx acquisition in February. The deal was the largest in semiconductor history and added FPGAs, adaptive SoCs, and AI engines to AMD's portfolio. Ryzen 7000 (Zen 4, 5nm) and EPYC Genoa (up to 96 Zen 4 cores) launched in H2. However, a severe PC market downturn and inventory correction triggered a major Q3 earnings warning, causing AMD's stock to fall approximately 55% for the year. GAAP profitability was significantly impacted by Xilinx acquisition amortization charges.\n\n2023: AI Horizon Dawns Revenue dipped slightly to $22.68 billion as the PC and gaming GPU markets continued their correction and Xilinx's embedded business digested customer inventory. However, AMD's most strategically significant move of the year—the commercial launch of the MI300X AI accelerator with 192 GB HBM3 memory—positioned AMD as the first credible alternative to NVIDIA for large language model inference. EPYC Bergamo (128 Zen 4c cores) expanded AMD's hyperscale server options. Data center revenue reached approximately $6.5 billion, setting the stage for an AI-driven surge in 2024.\n\n| Year | Revenue | Net Income | Key Milestone |\n|:-----|:--------|:-----------|:--------------|\n| 2019 | $6.73B  | $341M  | Ryzen 3000 + EPYC Rome (7nm); Lisa Su Businessperson of Year |\n| 2020 | $9.76B  | $2.49B | Ryzen 5000 fastest gaming CPU; Xilinx deal announced |\n| 2021 | $16.43B | $3.16B | EPYC Milan >10% server share; AMD stock all-time high |\n| 2022 | $23.60B | $1.32B | Xilinx closes ($49B); Zen 4 launches; PC crash |\n| 2023 | $22.68B | $854M  | MI300X AI accelerator commercially launched |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
90	AMD	five_year	2009-2013	Between 2009 and 2013, AMD navigated its post-ATI recovery by diversifying away from its dependence on traditional PC CPU competition—a period of necessary reinvention under two CEOs that set the stage for Lisa Su's successful turnaround. The era was defined by the GlobalFoundries transition, the Fusion APU concept, the disappointing Bulldozer CPU architecture, and the critical semi-custom SoC wins for the PlayStation 4 and Xbox One.\n\n2009: Stability and DirectX 11 First The GlobalFoundries separation completed in March, freeing AMD's balance sheet from fab ownership. Revenue held at approximately $5.4 billion despite the global financial crisis. The Phenom II CPU corrected its predecessor's TLB bug and competed credibly in the mid-range market. The Radeon HD 5000 series launched as the world's first DirectX 11-capable GPU family—ahead of NVIDIA—giving AMD a rare graphics feature leadership moment and excellent sales momentum.\n\n2010: Peak Revenue; APU Strategy Announced Revenue reached approximately $6.49 billion—AMD's best since 2000—and the company returned to GAAP profitability. AMD publicly previewed the 'Fusion' APU concept (CPU + GPU on one die), previewing Ontario and Llano designs ahead of 2011 launches. The Intel antitrust settlement yielded $1.25 billion for AMD and required Intel to reform certain business practices, closing a chapter that had consumed the company for six years. The Radeon HD 6000 series continued AMD's GPU momentum.\n\n2011: APU Success, Bulldozer Failure Revenue grew to $6.57 billion (a post-2000 record), but the year told starkly divergent stories. The Llano APU launched as the first Fusion product combining CPU and GPU cores on one die and delivered best-in-class integrated graphics for mainstream laptops—a genuine hit. In sharp contrast, the Bulldozer FX-series CPU launched in Q4 to widely negative reviews for low IPC and poor gaming performance relative to Intel's Sandy Bridge. CEO Dirk Meyer resigned and was replaced by Rory Read.\n\n2012: Restructuring and Semi-Custom Bets Revenue fell to approximately $5.42 billion with significant net losses as PC market decline, Bulldozer's shortfalls, and Intel's Ivy Bridge advantage combined. CEO Rory Read executed aggressive restructuring and explicitly shifted AMD's strategy toward semi-custom, embedded, and dense server markets. AMD sold its Austin campus to raise cash. Quietly, AMD secured the custom APU contracts for both PlayStation 4 and Xbox One—a lifeline that would sustain the company through its darkest period.\n\n2013: Console SoC Wins Materialize Revenue was approximately $5.30 billion, with losses narrowing to $83 million as the PS4 (November 15) and Xbox One (November 22) launched with AMD APUs inside. The console ramp immediately contributed to semi-custom revenue and validated AMD's strategy of competing for high-volume custom silicon. Kaveri APU (with full HSA integration) prepared for January 2014 launch. AMD ended 2013 with an uncertain CPU competitive position but a semi-custom business that provided a genuine multi-year revenue floor—and a new CEO, Lisa Su, arriving in October 2014 who would capitalize on this foundation.\n\n| Year | Revenue | Net Income | Key Architecture |\n|:-----|:--------|:-----------|:----------------|\n| 2009 | ~$5.4B  | Loss (small) | Phenom II; HD 5000 (DX11) |\n| 2010 | ~$6.49B | Profitable | Fusion APU announced; Intel settlement |\n| 2011 | ~$6.57B | Mixed | Llano APU success; Bulldozer failure |\n| 2012 | ~$5.42B | ~($1.2B) loss | Trinity APU; restructuring; console wins secured |\n| 2013 | ~$5.30B | ($83M) | PS4 + Xbox One launch with AMD APUs |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
91	AMD	yearly	1999	1999 was the most celebrated product year in AMD's history to that point. On June 23, 1999, AMD launched the Athlon K7 processor—internally codenamed 'Argon'—and from the moment independent benchmarks were published, the industry recognized it as a landmark achievement: the Athlon was the fastest x86 processor on the planet. Benchmarks showed the 600MHz Athlon defeating Intel's Pentium III 600MHz in virtually every test category, offering approximately 10% better business application performance and up to 20% superior gaming performance. For the first time since the 386 era, AMD held the CPU performance crown outright—without relying on clone rights or price-cutting strategy, but on pure architectural superiority.\n\nThe Athlon's architecture was a clean-sheet design featuring a true superscalar out-of-order execution engine, a nine-stage pipeline, a 128-bit floating-point unit operating as two independent 64-bit units, and the innovative Alpha EV6 frontside bus operating at 200MHz DDR (double data rate). The processor was manufactured on a 250nm process and later transitioned to 180nm. The Athlon launched at 500, 550, and 600MHz, with higher frequencies following rapidly. Intel responded with its Coppermine Pentium III, which closed the gap at higher clock speeds, and the two companies engaged in an intense public MHz war throughout 1999. Revenue grew to approximately $2.9 billion, and AMD's stock surged significantly as the Athlon generated major commercial and media attention. The Athlon launch validated years of engineering investment and strategic risk-taking by Jerry Sanders and AMD's CPU team.	The June 23, 1999 launch of the Athlon K7 processor was AMD's defining moment—delivering the fastest x86 CPU on the planet, beating Intel's Pentium III on virtually every benchmark. Revenue grew to approximately $2.9 billion, and AMD's stock surged as the Athlon's superior architecture validated AMD's independence from Intel's technology and sparked an intense MHz clock-speed competition.	["Athlon - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "AMD - Wikipedia", "AMD Processor History - intel-amd-info.blogspot.com", "The Rise and Fall of AMD - TechSpot"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
92	AMD	yearly	2012	2012 was a challenging year for AMD as the broader PC market experienced its sharpest demand decline in years, Bulldozer's performance disappointment continued to cost CPU market share, and the company undertook painful financial restructuring to survive. Annual revenue fell to approximately $5.42 billion, and AMD posted a significant GAAP net loss as declining ASPs (average selling prices), market share losses, and restructuring charges impacted results. New CEO Rory Read embarked on an aggressive turnaround plan centered on diversifying AMD beyond traditional PC processors into semi-custom, embedded, and dense server markets—a foreshadowing of the strategic pivot that Lisa Su would later execute more completely.\n\nOn the product side, AMD launched Trinity—the second-generation APU based on Piledriver (an improved Bulldozer derivative) combined with a Radeon HD 7000M GPU—in mid-2012. Trinity delivered meaningful improvements over Llano in both CPU performance (partially recovering from Bulldozer's shortfalls) and GPU performance, and it was adopted by mainstream notebook OEMs including HP, Toshiba, and Lenovo for budget-to-mid-range laptops. AMD also launched Piledriver-based FX desktop CPUs (FX-8350) which improved over Bulldozer but still could not match Intel's Ivy Bridge Core i5 and i7 processors in gaming and single-threaded workloads. AMD sold its Austin, Texas campus property to raise cash—a signal of the financial distress the company was navigating. The semi-custom SoC wins for the upcoming PlayStation 4 and Xbox One (announced internally, not yet public) represented a critical lifeline being quietly secured during this difficult period.	2012 saw AMD's revenue fall to approximately $5.42 billion with significant net losses as PC market decline, Bulldozer's ongoing performance gap versus Intel, and structural restructuring all weighed on results. The Trinity APU improved over Llano in both CPU and GPU performance, and AMD sold its Austin campus to raise capital, while quietly winning semi-custom SoC contracts for the next PlayStation and Xbox consoles.	["AMD Reports Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations", "AMD - Wikipedia", "The History of AMD CPUs - Tom's Hardware", "The History of AMD: A Complete Overview - Microchip USA", "AMD Annual Report 2012"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
93	AMD	five_year	1994-1998	Between 1994 and 1998, AMD transformed from a company dependent on Intel-derived technology into one capable of fielding its own competitive CPU architectures. The period opened with landmark legal resolutions and closed with AMD on the verge of launching the Athlon K7—arguably the most important CPU in its history—following its $857 million acquisition of NexGen and years of engineering investment.\n\n1994: Legal Victory and Am486 Peak A jury verdict affirmed AMD's right to use Intel's microcode through the 486 generation, vindicating AMD's clone strategy and enabling continued Am486 sales that drove revenue to approximately $1.64 billion. The K5—AMD's first fully in-house Pentium competitor—was in development but running behind schedule, and AMD began quiet work on K6 development following the strategic assessment of NexGen's processor technology.\n\n1995: The Grand Settlement and the NexGen Deal January 1995 brought two transformative events: a comprehensive global settlement with Intel ending all outstanding litigation, and AMD's $857 million acquisition of NexGen, whose sixth-generation CPU architecture would become the K6. Revenue grew to approximately $2.4 billion, and the Am5x86—a 486-class chip delivering Pentium 75-class performance at 133MHz—filled the near-term product gap. The legal cloud that had hung over AMD's CPU business for over a decade was finally lifted.\n\n1996: K5 Disappointment, K6 in Preparation AMD launched the K5—its first fully proprietary Pentium-class CPU—but the product arrived late and with mediocre floating-point performance, yielding disappointing commercial results. Revenue fell to approximately $1.6 billion as the competitive gap with Intel's Pentium Pro and Pentium II widened. The NexGen integration was underway, and AMD's engineering teams were making strong progress on K6, which promised genuine Pentium II clock-for-clock competitive performance.\n\n1997: K6 Redeems AMD The K6 launched in April 1997 to strong reviews: clock-for-clock integer performance matching the Pentium II, Socket 7 compatibility (allowing motherboard upgrades), and a significantly lower price. Revenue rebounded to approximately $2.4 billion, the MMX trademark dispute with Intel was settled, and AMD's competitive credibility was meaningfully restored. The K6-2 and the clean-sheet K7 (Athlon) architecture were already in development, promising even greater things ahead.\n\n1998: 3DNow! and the Eve of Athlon The K6-2 added 3DNow! SIMD floating-point instructions and reached clock speeds of 550MHz by year-end, addressing the K6's FP weakness and cementing AMD's position in the value PC market. Revenue reached approximately $2.5 billion. AMD secured an innovative partnership with DEC to use the Alpha EV6 bus for the K7, and engineering momentum on the Athlon was building toward what would be one of AMD's greatest product launches. The sub-$1,000 PC boom was driving enormous volume through AMD's product lines.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
94	AMD	five_year	2004-2008	Between 2004 and 2008, AMD achieved its greatest-ever server market success before making a fateful $5.4 billion acquisition of ATI Technologies that transformed its competitive positioning but also loaded its balance sheet with debt—arriving precisely as Intel launched the Core 2 Duo architecture that reversed AMD's CPU performance leadership. The period ended with AMD spinning off its manufacturing operations into GlobalFoundries, a fundamental restructuring of its business model.\n\n2004: Opteron Ascendant Revenue grew to approximately $5.0 billion as Opteron's AMD64 architecture won enterprise and cloud computing adopters across HP, IBM, Sun, and emerging cloud providers. AMD captured its highest server CPU market share ever, and the Athlon 64 X2 dual-core desktop processor launched ahead of Intel's competing product. AMD filed a comprehensive antitrust lawsuit against Intel alleging illegal monopolistic practices—a legal campaign that would attract regulatory scrutiny from the European Commission and ultimately result in a record-setting fine against Intel.\n\n2005: Dual-Core Leadership Revenue reached approximately $4.8 billion as AMD expanded dual-core Opteron and launched the Athlon 64 X2 consumer dual-core processor—widely praised as technically superior to Intel's Pentium D. AMD's server market share continued growing. The European Commission launched its own Intel antitrust investigation, and AMD's engineering teams were developing the next-generation K10 ('Barcelona') quad-core architecture.\n\n2006: The ATI Gamble; Core 2 Counter-Strike Revenue reached approximately $5.6 billion including partial ATI contribution, but the year's defining events were deeply contradictory. AMD's $5.4 billion acquisition of ATI (completed October) created an integrated CPU+GPU semiconductor company—a visionary strategy. Simultaneously, Intel launched Core 2 Duo (Conroe) in July: a devastating architecture 20-40% faster than AMD's Athlon 64 X2, instantly reversing AMD's CPU performance leadership and triggering rapid market share losses. Q4 operating losses approached $600 million.\n\n2007: Barcelona Bug; Deep Losses Revenue was approximately $6.0 billion (first full ATI year) but operating losses were enormous, approaching $3 billion including ATI goodwill impairments. The Barcelona K10 quad-core processor launched late and with a critical TLB bug that crippled server performance, badly damaging AMD's enterprise reputation. CEO Hector Ruiz announced his departure, with Dirk Meyer named successor. AMD's stock fell dramatically from its 2006 acquisition-era highs.\n\n2008: Manufacturing Spun Off; Radeon Rebounds Revenue was approximately $5.8 billion with large net losses. AMD announced the transformative spin-off of manufacturing operations into GlobalFoundries (with Abu Dhabi capital), freeing AMD from capital-intensive fab ownership. The Radeon HD 4800 series launched to exceptional reviews—delivering high-end GPU performance at mid-range prices—partially rehabilitating AMD's graphics brand. Intel was fined a record €1.06 billion by European regulators in the AMD antitrust case.\n\n| Year | Revenue | Net Income | Key Event |\n|:-----|:--------|:-----------|:----------|\n| 2004 | ~$5.0B | Profitable | Opteron server surge; antitrust suit filed |\n| 2005 | ~$4.8B | Profitable | Athlon 64 X2 dual-core leadership |\n| 2006 | ~$5.6B | Loss | ATI acquisition; Core 2 Duo reverses performance lead |\n| 2007 | ~$6.0B | ~($3.1B) | Barcelona TLB bug; Ruiz departs |\n| 2008 | ~$5.8B | ~($3.1B) | GlobalFoundries spin-off announced |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
95	AMD	five_year	1969-1973	The first five years of AMD (1969–1973) represent the founding and early survival of a semiconductor startup born from the ambitions of eight engineers who left Fairchild Semiconductor to build a more employee-centric, quality-focused chip company. The period ended with AMD's successful IPO and its first year of profitability, vindicating co-founder and CEO Jerry Sanders' belief that reliable second-source manufacturing could be a viable and highly profitable business.\n\n1969: The Founding AMD was incorporated on May 1, 1969 by Jerry Sanders and seven co-founders from Fairchild Semiconductor—John Carey, Sven Simonsen, Jack Gifford, Frank Botte, Jim Giles, Larry Stenger, and Ed Turney. The company was capitalized with $1 million in startup capital from Camelot Ventures and other venture investors. Operations were established at 901 Thompson Place in Sunnyvale, California, and AMD began developing logic chips. The company's strategy was clear from the outset: manufacture second-source versions of popular semiconductors with higher quality standards and more reliable delivery than competitors, building a reputation on execution rather than on novel design.\n\n1970: First Products Ship AMD shipped its first products in early 1970, including the Am2501 logic counter chip and the Am9300 4-bit shift register. These second-source logic chips found immediate market acceptance among electronics manufacturers who valued supply-chain redundancy. The company grew rapidly in its first full year, hiring engineers and expanding manufacturing output. Sanders' emphasis on treating employees well—offering stock options and a company culture centered on performance and celebration—began attracting talent from larger, more hierarchical semiconductor firms.\n\n1971: Expanding the Product Line Building on early success with logic chips, AMD introduced its first proprietary product in 1971, the Am9300 bipolar shift register, and expanded its catalog of second-source products to cover a broader range of logic and memory devices. Revenue grew steadily as AMD's reputation for quality components spread through the electronics industry. The company remained private but grew its headcount significantly, establishing itself as a credible supplier to major electronics companies.\n\n1972: AMD Goes Public The defining event of AMD's early history was its initial public offering on September 27, 1972. AMD listed on the New York Stock Exchange, issuing 620,000 shares at $15 to $15.50 per share and raising approximately $6.7 million in new capital. The IPO gave AMD access to public capital markets for plant and equipment expansion, and 1972 also marked the company's first profitable year of operations—validating the second-source model as not just survivable but genuinely profitable.\n\n1973: Scaling Operations Flush with IPO capital, AMD expanded its manufacturing capabilities and product catalog through 1973, growing revenue to approximately $26.5 million. The company established additional engineering and sales resources and continued deepening relationships with major OEM customers. AMD was becoming a well-regarded name in the bipolar logic and memory segment of the semiconductor industry, a foundation it would use to move into higher-value microprocessors in subsequent years.\n\n| Year | Key Event | Financial Status |\n|:-----|:----------|:----------------|\n| 1969 | Founding by Jerry Sanders + 7 co-founders | $1M startup capital |\n| 1970 | First products ship (Am2501, Am9300) | Early revenue |\n| 1971 | Expanded product line; first proprietary product | Growing |\n| 1972 | NYSE IPO; first profitable year | ~$6.7M raised in IPO |\n| 1973 | Scaling manufacturing; expanded OEM relationships | ~$26.5M revenue |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
96	AMD	yearly	2014	2014 was a year of leadership transition and strategic repositioning for AMD. In October 2014, Dr. Lisa Su was appointed President and CEO, replacing Rory Read who had guided the company through its initial turnaround. Su, who had previously served as AMD's Chief Operating Officer, quickly moved to prioritize high-performance computing and graphics as AMD's core growth pillars. Annual revenue for 2014 came in at approximately $5.51 billion, reflecting the continued strength of AMD's semi-custom business—AMD's SoCs powered both the Sony PlayStation 4 and Microsoft Xbox One, which continued to ramp up production heading into the holiday season.\n\nDespite the semi-custom boost, AMD posted a full-year GAAP net loss of $403 million ($0.53 per share), as the Computing and Graphics segment continued to lose ground to Intel in notebooks and desktops. The year saw the launch of AMD's 'Kaveri' A-series APU, the company's first processor to fully implement Heterogeneous System Architecture (HSA), integrating CPU and GPU cores on a shared memory pool. Kaveri also included AMD's TrueAudio technology and Mantle API support. On the discrete GPU side, AMD launched the Radeon R9 290X and refreshed its lineup, remaining competitive against NVIDIA in the high-end gaming market.\n\nLisa Su's appointment was widely seen as a critical inflection point. She immediately began simplifying AMD's product roadmap, cutting projects that didn't align with the company's core strengths, and increasing investment in future architectures like Zen (CPU) and Polaris/Vega (GPU). By year-end, AMD's stock traded at around $2.50, and the company was widely considered by analysts to be fighting for its survival. Su's focus on engineering excellence and targeted market opportunities began to shift the internal culture toward what would eventually become one of the most dramatic corporate comebacks in semiconductor history.	2014 marked AMD's pivotal CEO transition, with Lisa Su taking the helm in October and immediately refocusing the company on high-performance computing and graphics. Annual revenue was approximately $5.51 billion, buoyed by semi-custom SoCs inside the PS4 and Xbox One, but the company posted a net loss of $403 million as its PC processor business continued to struggle. The Kaveri APU launched as AMD's most advanced HSA-compliant processor to date.	["AMD Reports 2014 Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD Reports 2014 Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AnandTech", "AMD Reports 2014 Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - HPCwire", "AMD Reports 2014 Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - TechPowerUp", "Lisa Su and AMD (A) - Harvard Business School Case Study", "How Lisa Su brought AMD from the brink of bankruptcy - CNN Business", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/589/amd-reports-2014-fourth-quarter-and-annual-results"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
97	AMD	yearly	1974	The semiconductor industry experienced a significant recession in 1974-1975, and AMD faced considerable challenges during this period. Despite the downturn, AMD survived the economic crisis where many competitors struggled or failed. In 1974, AMD introduced the Am2900 bit-slice processor family, a significant achievement in product development during a difficult time. The Am2900 was designed for high-performance custom processor applications and marked AMD's entry into the more complex microprocessor market. CEO Jerry Sanders refused to follow the industry trend of massive layoffs, instead taking inspiration from Japanese manufacturing practices and maintaining AMD's workforce. This commitment to employees during tough times would become a defining characteristic of Sanders' leadership. The company continued investment in manufacturing capabilities and research and development, positioning itself to capitalize on the recovery when it came. AMD's survival through the 1974-1975 recession demonstrated the strength of its business model and the quality of its management.	Despite the semiconductor industry recession, AMD introduced the Am2900 bit-slice processor family in 1974. CEO Jerry Sanders refused to implement massive layoffs, maintaining the company's workforce.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "WikiChip - AMD Am2900", "Business ABC - AMD History"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
98	AMD	yearly	1990	In 1990, AMD successfully reverse-engineered the Intel 80386 processor and began marketing the Am386, a 100%-compatible clone of Intel's 386 design. The company had completed nearly six years of intensive reverse-engineering work to create the Am386. In September 1990, Intel sought injunctive relief and damages from AMD in a copyright infringement action regarding AMD's 80C287 math coprocessor. In October 1991, Intel filed another action against AMD alleging copyright infringement on the 386 processor's logic programming and Intel microcode. More controversially, Intel even sued AMD for trademark infringement of the '386' name, though this claim ultimately failed. A neutral arbitrator found in 1990 that Intel had breached the 1982 contract, and the arbitrator granted AMD permanent, royalty-free rights under Intel copyrights, patents, trade secrets, and maskwork rights contained in AMD's reverse-engineered 386. AMD's Am386 chip offered comparable performance to Intel's 386 at more competitive pricing. The litigation between AMD and Intel became increasingly contentious, with multiple cases proceeding in federal court and arbitration simultaneously. AMD's ability to successfully reverse-engineer the 386 demonstrated remarkable engineering capability and perseverance in the face of powerful opposition from Intel.	AMD began marketing the Am386 in 1990, a 100%-compatible clone developed through reverse-engineering. Intel filed multiple copyright infringement lawsuits, but a neutral arbitrator found Intel had breached their contract.	["Silicon Underground - AMD Am386 Released March 2, 1991", "Harvard Journal of Law & Technology - Intel and the x86 Architecture", "Wikipedia - Am386"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
99	AMD	yearly	1976	In 1976, AMD signed a significant second-source agreement with Intel for the 8080 processor, marking an important milestone in the company's development. The agreement, which included a cross-licensing arrangement effective in October 1976, granted AMD the right to manufacture Intel-compatible microprocessors. This agreement was part of Intel's strategy to provide customers with multiple sources for critical components, and many lucrative contracts, particularly in military applications, required key components to have approved second-source manufacturers. AMD's ability to secure this agreement with Intel demonstrated that the company had proven itself as a reliable and capable manufacturer capable of producing complex integrated circuits. The agreement provided AMD with access to Intel's technology and microcode, allowing the company to develop compatible products. This relationship would eventually form the foundation for AMD's entry into the lucrative microprocessor market. AMD's revenue continued to grow as the company expanded its manufacturing facilities and product portfolio during this period.	AMD signed a cross-licensing agreement with Intel in October 1976, gaining the right to second-source the Intel 8080 processor. This agreement validated AMD's manufacturing capabilities.	["WikiChip - Am8086", "Wikipedia - AMD", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
100	AMD	yearly	1983	In 1983, Advanced Micro Devices continued to benefit from the explosive growth of the IBM PC market and the demand for IBM PC-compatible computers. The company began manufacturing the Am286, which was compatible with Intel's 80286 processor. The 80286 represented the next generation of Intel's x86 architecture, offering 16-bit processing with improved performance and reduced power consumption compared to the 8086. AMD's ability to produce the Am286 was directly enabled by the 1982 technology exchange agreement with Intel. During 1983, AMD introduced INT.STD.1000, the highest manufacturing quality standard in the semiconductor industry at that time, reflecting the company's commitment to product quality and reliability. This achievement demonstrated AMD's manufacturing prowess and helped secure long-term customer relationships. Revenue reached approximately $225 million in 1983, representing substantial growth and establishing AMD as a major semiconductor manufacturer. The company was generating significant profits from its position as the approved second source for IBM PC-compatible processors. AMD's stock price increased substantially as investors recognized the company's success in capturing a significant portion of the rapidly growing microprocessor market.	AMD achieved revenue of approximately $225 million in 1983 while beginning manufacturing of the Am286 processor. The company introduced INT.STD.1000, the highest manufacturing quality standard in the industry.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD", "History of Advanced Micro Devices - FundingUniverse"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
101	AMD	yearly	1969	Advanced Micro Devices was founded on May 1, 1969 by Jerry Sanders and seven co-founders from Fairchild Semiconductor, including John Carey, Sven Simonsen, Jack Gifford, Frank Botte, Jim Giles, Larry Stenger, and Ed Turney. The company was capitalized with $1 million in startup funding from Camelot Ventures and venture capital investors. AMD moved to its headquarters at 901 Thompson Place in Sunnyvale in September 1969, occupying a 15,000 square foot facility. The company began operations as a manufacturer of logic chips and quickly positioned itself as a reliable second-source manufacturer. AMD shipped its first products in March 1970, including the Am9300 4-bit MSI shift register and the Am2501 logic counter chip, which would become highly successful. By the end of 1969, AMD had established the foundation for what would become a major semiconductor manufacturer, though 1969 was characterized primarily by startup activities and product development rather than significant revenue generation.	AMD was founded on May 1, 1969 by Jerry Sanders and seven co-founders from Fairchild Semiconductor with $1 million in startup funding. The company established operations in Sunnyvale and began developing logic chips, shipping its first products including the Am2501 logic counter in early 1970.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "History of Information - Jerry Sanders and Fairchild Semiconductor", "Computer History Museum - Sanders and Advanced Micro Devices: The First Fifteen Years"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
102	AMD	yearly	1986	In 1986, Advanced Micro Devices began the monumental task of reverse-engineering Intel's 80386 processor. Without access to Intel's technical specifications, source code, or microcode, AMD's engineering team had to analyze the 386's physical design, transistor-level logic, and functionality to create a compatible processor. The 386 was substantially more complex than the 8086, containing almost ten times as many transistors. This reverse-engineering effort would ultimately require five or more years of intensive engineering work. AMD continued to manufacture second-source versions of the 8086, 8088, 80186, and 80286 processors, which were generating significant revenue as demand for IBM PC-compatible computers continued to grow worldwide. The semiconductor industry remained in recession during much of 1986, but the personal computer market continued to expand, providing a bright spot for semiconductor manufacturers. AMD invested heavily in its manufacturing facilities and research and development capabilities, positioning the company for future growth. The company's commitment to reverse-engineering the 386 demonstrated management's confidence that the company could successfully compete with Intel through engineering prowess rather than waiting for a negotiated license.	AMD began reverse-engineering Intel's 80386 processor in 1986 without access to Intel's technical specifications. This effort would take five or more years and require substantial engineering resources.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "Harvard Journal of Law & Technology - Intel and the x86 Architecture", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
103	AMD	yearly	1971	In 1971, AMD expanded its product portfolio significantly and entered the RAM chip market. The company introduced the Am3101, a 64-bit bipolar RAM device, marking AMD's entry into memory chip manufacturing. The Am2505 high-speed shift register became one of AMD's bestselling products in 1971, representing the fastest multiplier available in the market at that time. By year-end 1971, AMD had achieved annual sales of $4.6 million, demonstrating solid growth from its founding. The company continued to establish itself as a reliable second-source manufacturer of logic and memory chips. AMD's manufacturing operations in Sunnyvale were running at capacity as demand for its products grew. The company's strategy of providing high-quality alternatives to industry-standard components was gaining traction with customers seeking reliability and competitive pricing.	AMD expanded into the RAM chip market in 1971 with the Am3101 64-bit bipolar RAM and the Am2505 shift register, its bestselling product. By year-end, the company achieved $4.6 million in annual sales.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "History of Advanced Micro Devices - FundingUniverse", "Encyclopedia.com - Advanced Micro Devices Inc"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
104	AMD	yearly	2005	2005 was another strong year for AMD, with revenue reaching approximately $4.8 billion as Opteron and Athlon 64 continued their market share gains and AMD moved aggressively into dual-core processors for both the consumer and server markets. On April 21, 2005, AMD announced the Athlon 64 X2—the first dual-core processor for desktop consumers—with retail sales beginning May 31. The Athlon 64 X2 offered genuine simultaneous multi-threading performance for content creation, scientific, and gaming workloads, arriving slightly ahead of Intel's dual-core Pentium D, which was widely criticized for being two separate dies on a single substrate rather than a true unified dual-core design. The Athlon 64 X2 was broadly regarded by reviewers as the superior product.\n\nIn the server market, AMD launched dual-core Opteron processors based on the 'Toledo' core in April 2005, providing up to eight physical processor cores in a dual-socket configuration—a compelling advantage over Intel's competing dual-core Xeon offering. Enterprise customers adopted these platforms rapidly for virtualization, web serving, and HPC workloads. AMD's antitrust case against Intel continued to build steam, with the European Commission launching its own investigation into Intel's business practices in 2005. AMD also refined the Athlon 64 with new process node shrinks on the 'Venice' and 'San Diego' cores, which improved power efficiency, overclocking headroom, and manufacturing yields. The dual-channel Venice core became one of AMD's most popular consumer processors due to its favorable price-to-performance ratio.	2005 saw AMD reach approximately $4.8 billion in revenue while launching the Athlon 64 X2—the first true dual-core desktop processor widely praised as superior to Intel's competing Pentium D. Dual-core Opteron server CPUs further expanded AMD's enterprise footprint, and the European Commission opened its own antitrust investigation into Intel's business practices following 