105	AMD	yearly	2015	2015 was the darkest financial chapter of AMD's modern era, yet paradoxically laid the technical foundation for the company's eventual resurgence. Full-year revenue fell 28% to $3.99 billion, driven by a collapse in the PC market and AMD's continued loss of CPU market share to Intel. The company posted a GAAP net loss of $660 million ($0.84 per share) and an operating loss of $481 million. AMD's stock hit an all-time low of approximately $1.61 per share in late 2015, with many analysts and investors openly questioning whether the company would survive as an independent entity. Debt restructuring and cost-cutting were urgent priorities.\n\nDespite the dire financial picture, AMD delivered some technically impressive products during the year. The Radeon R9 Fury X, launched in June 2015, was the world's first graphics card to use High Bandwidth Memory (HBM), stacking memory directly on the same package as the GPU. This breakthrough—delivering 512 GB/s of memory bandwidth—demonstrated AMD's leadership in memory innovation and established a critical technical differentiator over NVIDIA's products. The Fury X was accompanied by the broader Radeon 300-series refresh. On the CPU side, AMD officially announced the development of its next-generation 'Zen' architecture, promising a dramatic generational leap in CPU performance that had been absent since the ill-fated Bulldozer era.\n\nLisa Su spent 2015 executing a triage strategy: divesting non-core assets, restructuring the workforce, and realigning AMD's engineering resources toward Zen and next-generation GPU architectures. The company separated its CPU and GPU businesses into two distinct segments—Computing and Graphics, and Enterprise, Embedded and Semi-Custom—to create clearer accountability. AMD also maintained a critical lifeline through its partnership with TSMC for advanced process node access and its semi-custom SoC business for game consoles. The year ended with AMD's fate hanging in the balance, but the technical bets being placed by Su and her engineering team were quietly setting the stage for a historic comeback.	2015 was AMD's most difficult financial year in decades, with revenue collapsing 28% to $3.99 billion and the stock hitting an all-time low near $1.61 per share. The company posted a net loss of $660 million while CEO Lisa Su implemented a survival-focused restructuring. On the technology front, AMD pioneered High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) with the Radeon R9 Fury X and formally announced the ground-up Zen CPU architecture.	["AMD Reports 2015 Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - AMD Investor Relations (ir.amd.com)", "AMD Announces Q4 2015 Earnings - WCCFTech", "AMD Reports 2015 Fourth Quarter and Annual Results - TechPowerUp", "AMD Ushers in a New Era with Radeon R9 and R7 300 Series Including World's First HBM Graphics - AMD Press Release", "AMD Radeon R9 Fury X Review - HotHardware", "AMD Radeon R9 Fury X Review - TechSpot", "Corporate Turnaround Story: Advanced Micro Devices (AMD) - Platform01 Consulting", "https://ir.amd.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/662/amd-reports-2015-fourth-quarter-and-annual-results"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
106	AMD	yearly	1980	In 1980, Advanced Micro Devices began manufacturing Intel 8086-compatible chips, marking the company's serious entry into the microprocessor market. The Am8086 represented AMD's commitment to providing customers with alternative sources for the 8086 processor, which was rapidly becoming the industry standard for high-performance computing applications. This was a critical inflection point for AMD, as it positioned the company to benefit from the explosive growth of the microprocessor market. The 8086 processor had been designed by Intel as a 16-bit upgrade to the earlier 8-bit 8080 and 8085, and it was gaining adoption in minicomputers, workstations, and the emerging personal computer market. AMD's ability to manufacture compatible 8086 chips demonstrated the company's manufacturing prowess and design capabilities. However, the company had not yet secured explicit rights to the 8086 from Intel; this would come through negotiations in 1981-1982. The start of 8086 production in 1980 reflected AMD's confidence in its ability to negotiate favorable terms with Intel and to serve the growing market demand for this processor.	AMD began producing Intel 8086-compatible chips in 1980, marking the company's serious entry into the microprocessor market. This positioned AMD to benefit from the growing demand for 8086-based systems.	["Wikipedia - Advanced Micro Devices", "Popular Timelines - Full History of AMD", "WikiChip - Am8086"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
107	AMD	yearly	1975	In 1975, AMD recovered from the 1974-1975 recession and resumed its growth trajectory. The company publicly announced the Am2900 bit-slice processor family in July 1975, placing a prominent three-page advertisement in Electronic News with the headline 'Bye-Bye MSI,' claiming a revolution in bipolar technology. The Am2900 family consisted of modular components for building custom, high-performance microprogrammable processors and controllers with scalable word lengths in multiples of 4 bits. First silicon arrived in late 1974 or early 1975, with production yields starting low at 3-4 die per wafer but improving rapidly to around 30 die per wafer by mid-1975, enabling volume production. The Am2900 family became a popular embedded processor used in various applications. By mid-1975, the overall semiconductor market had begun to recover. AMD's ability to introduce new, sophisticated products while navigating the recession established the company as an innovative manufacturer capable of competing with much larger competitors.	AMD publicly announced the Am2900 bit-slice processor family in July 1975, representing a major achievement. The company recovered from recession and expanded its product portfolio with this modular processor architecture.	["WikiChip - AMD Am2900", "Computer History Museum - AMD 2900 Microprocessor Oral History", "Wikipedia - Am2900"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
