191	TSLA	yearly	2017	2017 was the year Tesla took its biggest gamble yet: the launch of the Model 3, its first genuinely mass-market vehicle. The outcome would reveal both the extraordinary ambition of Elon Musk's vision and the severe operational limits of Tesla's manufacturing capabilities at the time. The Model 3 was unveiled for customer deliveries in July 2017, but the subsequent production ramp would become one of the most challenging episodes in modern automotive history — a period Musk himself would later call 'production hell.'\n\nThe Model 3 launched to enormous fanfare in July 2017 with a hand-off event at the Fremont factory. However, Tesla had drastically underestimated the complexity of ramping production of a new vehicle from near zero to the thousands of vehicles per week that it had promised. The company had set a target of producing 5,000 Model 3 vehicles per week by the end of 2017 — a goal it spectacularly missed. By the end of Q4 2017, Tesla had produced only 2,425 Model 3 vehicles in the entire quarter, with a production rate near the end of the quarter of roughly 1,000 per week. Elon Musk later disclosed that Tesla had made overly ambitious bets on extensive factory automation, which paradoxically slowed production rather than accelerating it.\n\nDespite the Model 3 struggles, Tesla's overall financial results for 2017 showed strong growth. Total revenue reached approximately $11.76 billion, up approximately 68% from $7 billion in 2016. Model S and Model X together delivered approximately 101,312 vehicles for the year — the first time Tesla had crossed 100,000 annual deliveries, a significant milestone. However, losses also mounted: Tesla reported a net loss of approximately $1.96 billion for 2017, as capital expenditures for the Model 3 ramp, Gigafactory expansion, and infrastructure investments were enormous. Free cash flow was deeply negative at approximately negative $3.5 billion.\n\nIn November 2017, Musk also unveiled the Tesla Semi, an all-electric Class 8 semi-truck, and the second-generation Tesla Roadster — the latter with jaw-dropping claimed performance specs (0–60 mph in 1.9 seconds). Neither would enter production for several years, but the reveals generated tremendous media attention and long reservation lists.\n\nKey milestones in 2017 included:\n* **Model 3 Launch**: First deliveries in July 2017; production ramp plagued by 'production hell.'\n* **101,312 Total Deliveries**: First time Tesla crossed 100,000 annual vehicle deliveries.\n* **Revenue of ~$11.76 Billion**: 68% year-over-year growth, driven by Model S and X volume.\n* **Tesla Semi and New Roadster Revealed**: November 2017 reveals expand Tesla's product roadmap.\n* **Net Loss of ~$1.96 Billion**: Heavy investment in Model 3 ramp and infrastructure.	Tesla launched the Model 3 in July 2017, but a severely underestimated manufacturing ramp — dubbed 'production hell' — resulted in only about 2,425 Model 3s produced in Q4 versus a target of 5,000/week. Total annual deliveries still crossed 100,000 for the first time (101,312 vehicles), and revenue grew 68% to $11.76 billion. Tesla also unveiled the Semi truck and a new Roadster in November, while net losses widened to approximately $1.96 billion.	["https://ir.tesla.com/press-release/tesla-q4-2017-vehicle-production-and-deliveries", "https://yourstory.com/2018/02/tesla-motors-clocked-11-8b-revenues-2017-lost-675-4m-q4-2018-plans/amp", "https://www.fool.com/investing/2018/02/15/5-metrics-highlight-tesla-incs-2017.aspx", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
192	TSLA	yearly	2019	2019 was a year of consolidation and strategic expansion for Tesla, as the company stabilized Model 3 production in the United States and simultaneously executed one of the most audacious manufacturing projects in automotive history: the construction of Gigafactory Shanghai in under one year. It was also the year Tesla delivered on its promise of a truly mass-market vehicle at scale, with Model 3 becoming the best-selling premium sedan in the US.\n\nFor the full year, Tesla delivered approximately 367,500 vehicles — a 50% increase over 2018 — comfortably meeting its annual guidance. The Model 3 was the primary driver: in North America, it outsold the runner-up premium sedan by 60%. Total revenues reached approximately $24.5 billion. Importantly, Tesla achieved four consecutive profitable quarters for the first time — the precise criteria for S&P 500 index eligibility — though full-year 2019 net income was a modest gain after adjustments. The company managed to exit 2019 with more cash on hand than it had started the year with, a first for Tesla.\n\nThe Shanghai Gigafactory (Gigafactory 3) was the year's most remarkable achievement. Ground broke in January 2019, and in an astonishing feat of speed, the factory went from bare ground to initial production of Made-in-China Model 3 vehicles in under 12 months. The first production vehicles rolled out in October 2019, and the first deliveries to customers occurred on December 30, 2019 — just 357 days after groundbreaking, a record for the global automotive industry. The China factory dramatically expanded Tesla's addressable market and significantly reduced the import tariffs that had been making Tesla vehicles expensive for Chinese buyers.\n\nIn November 2019, Elon Musk unveiled the Cybertruck at a splashy event in Los Angeles. The vehicle's angular, stainless-steel exoskeleton design polarized the public — some found it visionary, others found it absurd. The window-smashing demonstration gone wrong (when a steel ball cracked the supposedly unbreakable 'Armor Glass') generated enormous viral attention. Despite the mixed reactions, Tesla reported over 250,000 reservations within days. Production was planned for 2021.\n\nKey milestones in 2019 included:\n* **367,500 Deliveries**: 50% growth year-over-year; Model 3 dominates US premium sedan segment.\n* **Gigafactory Shanghai Opens**: Ground-to-production in 357 days — a global automotive record.\n* **Revenue of ~$24.5 Billion**: Continued strong growth trajectory.\n* **Four Consecutive Profitable Quarters**: First time Tesla achieved this, qualifying for S&P 500 eligibility.\n* **Cybertruck Unveiled**: Polarizing stainless steel pickup truck generates 250,000+ reservations.	Tesla delivered approximately 367,500 vehicles in 2019, a 50% increase year-over-year, on revenue of $24.5 billion, and achieved four consecutive profitable quarters for the first time. The Gigafactory Shanghai went from groundbreaking to producing Made-in-China Model 3s in just 357 days — a global automotive record — with first customer deliveries on December 30. Elon Musk also unveiled the Cybertruck in November, drawing both ridicule and 250,000+ reservations.	["https://ir.tesla.com/press-release/tesla-q4-2019-vehicle-production-deliveries", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigafactory_Shanghai", "https://electrek.co/2019/12/27/tesla-start-made-in-china-model-3-deliveries/", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc."]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
193	TSLA	yearly	2023	2023 was a year of aggressive price competition, record deliveries, the long-awaited Cybertruck launch, and growing profit pressure for Tesla. Elon Musk launched a global price war early in the year, slashing prices on all models to defend market share against a flood of new EV competitors — particularly from Chinese manufacturers. The strategy boosted unit volume but significantly compressed Tesla's margins, sparking debate about the long-term health of its business model.\n\nTesla delivered approximately 1.8 million vehicles for the full year 2023, a 38% increase over 2022's 1.31 million deliveries. The Model Y became not just the world's best-selling electric vehicle but the overall best-selling passenger car globally in 2023 — the first time in history an EV had achieved this distinction. Revenue grew to approximately $96.8 billion, a modest increase from $81.5 billion in 2022. However, net income fell to approximately $15 billion (which included a one-time $5.9 billion tax benefit; adjusted net income was approximately $10.9 billion), down significantly from $12.6 billion in 2022 in cash terms, as the price cuts eroded profitability. Automotive gross margins contracted sharply — from over 25% in 2022 to approximately 18.4% by late 2023.\n\nThe Cybertruck arrived at last on November 30, 2023, at a delivery event at Gigafactory Texas, where Musk handed the first units to customers who had waited years. The vehicle's distinctive stainless-steel exoskeleton had remained essentially unchanged from the 2019 concept, and initial production was limited. The Cybertruck was priced significantly higher than originally promised in 2019 ($35,000); the entry model started at approximately $60,990. Despite the price increase, demand remained strong, and Tesla indicated it aimed to eventually produce 250,000 Cybertrucks annually.\n\nThroughout 2023, Tesla initiated multiple rounds of price cuts on the Model 3, Model Y, Model S, and Model X in the US, Europe, and China. Cuts ranged from $4,400 to $20,000 depending on the model and market. In China, the competition was particularly fierce, with BYD and other domestic manufacturers offering competitive EVs at lower price points. Tesla's China price cuts sparked accusations of dumping from some competitors and prompted retaliatory anxiety across the EV industry.\n\nKey milestones in 2023 included:\n* **1.8 Million Deliveries**: Model Y becomes world's best-selling car of any kind — the first EV to achieve this.\n* **Cybertruck Deliveries Begin**: November 30, 2023 — years-delayed stainless steel pickup begins reaching customers.\n* **Revenue of ~$96.8 Billion**: Continued growth, though margin compression was significant.\n* **Aggressive Price Cuts**: Multiple rounds of global price reductions to defend market share.\n* **Net Income of ~$15B (incl. tax benefit)**: Headline figure inflated by one-time items; underlying profitability declined.	Tesla delivered a record 1.8 million vehicles in 2023, with the Model Y becoming the world's best-selling car of any kind — the first EV to achieve that distinction. The Cybertruck finally began deliveries on November 30, 2023. While revenue grew to approximately $96.8 billion, aggressive global price cuts compressed automotive gross margins significantly, and underlying profitability declined from 2022 highs. BYD and other Chinese EV makers intensified competitive pressure throughout the year.	["https://www.assemblymag.com/articles/98245-tesla-reported-a-total-of-1-8-million-deliveries-in-2023", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesla_Cybertruck", "https://www.npr.org/2023/11/30/1216083781/tesla-cybertruck-release-musk", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000162828024002390/tsla-20231231.htm", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
194	TSLA	yearly	2021	2021 was a landmark year for Tesla on virtually every measure: record deliveries, record revenue, first $1 trillion market capitalization, and the opening of two new Gigafactories under construction. The company transitioned from an aspirational electric vehicle pioneer into one of the most valuable and widely recognized corporations on the planet.\n\nTesla delivered approximately 936,222 vehicles for the full year 2021 — a stunning 87% increase over 2020's 499,550 deliveries. The Model 3 and Model Y together drove almost all of the volume, as Tesla had effectively discontinued the Model S and Model X for a significant portion of the year to retool them with new interiors and the controversial 'yoke' steering wheel. Revenue for 2021 surged to approximately $53.8 billion, a 70.7% increase from $31.5 billion in 2020. Net income grew to approximately $5.6 billion, a massive leap from the $721 million earned in 2020, reflecting Tesla's powerful operating leverage as volume scaled.\n\nThe defining moment of 2021 for investors came in October, when Tesla's stock crossed the $1,000 per share mark and the company's market capitalization surpassed $1 trillion — a milestone achieved after Hertz announced an order for 100,000 Tesla vehicles. Tesla became the trillion-dollar-valuation company with the lowest revenue of any company to achieve that milestone, underscoring how much of Tesla's valuation was premised on future growth expectations rather than current earnings alone. At its peak in 2021, Tesla's market cap approached $1.2 trillion.\n\nConstruction of both Gigafactory Berlin (in Brandenburg, Germany) and Gigafactory Texas (in Austin) made significant progress throughout 2021. Gigafactory Texas broke ground in July 2020 and was already producing limited quantities of Model Y vehicles by year-end 2021. The 'Giga Fest' celebration for the near-completion of Gigafactory Berlin was held in October 2021, with the factory formally opening in March 2022. Tesla also expanded the Shanghai Gigafactory's capacity significantly during 2021, making China its largest single production hub.\n\nKey milestones in 2021 included:\n* **936,222 Deliveries**: Nearly doubles year-over-year; approximately 87% growth.\n* **$1 Trillion Market Cap**: Achieved in October 2021, catalyzed by Hertz order for 100,000 vehicles.\n* **Revenue of ~$53.8 Billion**: 70.7% growth; net income of ~$5.6 billion.\n* **Gigafactory Texas and Berlin**: Both under construction with initial production starting at Texas by year-end.\n* **Hertz Partnership**: 100,000 vehicle order from Hertz rental car company — the largest EV fleet purchase to that date.	Tesla delivered 936,222 vehicles in 2021, an 87% jump year-over-year, and reported revenue of $53.8 billion and net income of $5.6 billion. In October 2021, Tesla's market capitalization surpassed $1 trillion — the first automaker to achieve this milestone and the lowest-revenue company ever to do so — spurred in part by Hertz's order for 100,000 vehicles. Gigafactories in Texas and Berlin advanced toward completion, positioning Tesla for further production expansion.	["https://techcrunch.com/2021/10/25/tesla-1-trillion-market-cap/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesla,_Inc.", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigafactory_Texas", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigafactory_Berlin-Brandenburg"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
195	TSLA	yearly	2010	2010 was Tesla's coming-out year as a public company. On June 29, 2010, Tesla Motors completed its initial public offering on the NASDAQ exchange, becoming the first American automaker to go public since Ford in 1956. Tesla issued 13.3 million shares at $17.00 per share, raising approximately $226 million. The IPO was a landmark moment for the electric vehicle industry and a validation of Elon Musk's vision for mass-market electric cars — though Tesla was, at this point, still a small niche automaker producing a hand-built sports car.\n\nAt the time of the IPO, Tesla's sole product was the Roadster, a high-performance electric sports car based on a modified Lotus Elise chassis. The Roadster had launched in 2008 and was priced around $109,000, making it a luxury performance vehicle. Tesla had nearly gone bankrupt in late 2008 during the financial crisis; Elon Musk had invested his own money to keep the company alive. By 2010, the Roadster had proven that electric vehicles could achieve real-world performance, boasting a 0–60 mph time under 4 seconds and a range of over 200 miles.\n\nFinancially, Tesla reported total revenues of approximately $117 million for full-year 2010, driven almost entirely by Roadster sales and development contracts (including a $465 million Department of Energy loan Tesla received in 2010). The company remained deeply unprofitable, posting a net loss of $154 million for the year. Despite the losses, the IPO proceeds and DOE loan gave Tesla a runway to pursue its next major product: the Model S sedan.\n\nKey milestones in 2010 also included Tesla's partnership with Toyota, which invested $50 million in Tesla and agreed to work together on electric vehicle components. Tesla also announced plans to purchase the former NUMMI manufacturing plant in Fremont, California — a massive facility that would become Tesla's primary factory. These deals signaled that the broader auto industry was beginning to take Tesla seriously. The Fremont factory acquisition was a bold bet; the plant had previously been operated jointly by GM and Toyota and had the capacity to produce hundreds of thousands of vehicles annually.	Tesla went public on June 29, 2010, raising $226 million in its NASDAQ IPO at $17/share — the first American automaker IPO since Ford in 1956. The company reported $117 million in revenue from Roadster sales and secured a $465 million DOE loan and a $50 million investment from Toyota. Tesla also announced the acquisition of the Fremont, California manufacturing plant, setting the stage for mass-market vehicle production.	["https://ir.tesla.com/press-release/tesla-motors-reports-fourth-quarter-and-full-year-2010-results", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://www.thestreet.com/technology/history-of-tesla-15088992", "https://www.britannica.com/money/Tesla-Motors", "https://eqvista.com/tesla-shareholders-from-start-to-ipo/", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
184	TSLA	yearly	2013	2013 was a breakout year for Tesla, marked by its first profitable quarter, surging stock price, and massive acceleration of Model S deliveries. In Q1 2013, Tesla reported its first-ever GAAP quarterly profit — a milestone that proved electric vehicles could be commercially viable. The company earned approximately $11 million in net income for that quarter, a shocking reversal from years of losses, and the stock surged dramatically in response.\n\nFor the full year, Tesla delivered over 22,000 Model S vehicles, beating its initial guidance of 20,000 units. Revenue rocketed to approximately $2.01 billion — nearly a 5x increase from 2012 — as production scaled and average selling prices remained high (the Model S started around $62,400). A significant portion of this revenue also came from the sale of zero-emission vehicle (ZEV) regulatory credits to other automakers, which totaled roughly $194 million for the year. These credits were mandated by California law and provided Tesla with high-margin revenue that competitors had to purchase.\n\nThe Model S continued to accumulate accolades in 2013. Consumer Reports gave it an extraordinary 99 out of 100 rating — the highest score the publication had ever given a car. It won numerous additional automotive awards and became a cultural phenomenon, regularly spotted in Silicon Valley, Hollywood, and increasingly in international markets as Tesla expanded into Europe and Asia.\n\nTesla's stock price rose approximately 344% in 2013, making it one of the best-performing large-cap stocks in the US. The surge in share price created enormous wealth for early investors and employees, and firmly established Elon Musk as a visionary CEO. The company also paid off its entire $465 million DOE loan in May 2013, nine years ahead of schedule, a major symbolic and practical milestone that reduced debt obligations and burnished Tesla's image.\n\nKey milestones in 2013 included:\n* **First Profitable Quarter (Q1 2013)**: Tesla's first-ever GAAP net income, triggering a stock surge.\n* **DOE Loan Repaid**: Tesla repaid its $465 million government loan in full, nine years early.\n* **22,000+ Deliveries**: Exceeded full-year guidance, with Model S becoming the best-selling large luxury sedan in the US by Q4.\n* **Stock Up 344%**: One of the best-performing major US stocks of the year.\n* **International Expansion**: Model S deliveries began in Europe and Asia.	Tesla posted its first profitable quarter in Q1 2013 and delivered over 22,000 Model S vehicles for the year, driving revenue to $2.01 billion — a nearly 5x increase from 2012. The company repaid its entire $465 million DOE loan nine years ahead of schedule, and TSLA stock surged approximately 344% for the year. The Model S received a record-breaking 99/100 Consumer Reports score and became the best-selling large luxury sedan in the US by Q4.	["https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000119312513096241/d452995d10k.htm", "https://motrolix.com//2014/02/tesla-reports-761-million-in-revenue-0-33-earnings-per-share-for-q4-2013/", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://www.thestreet.com/technology/history-of-tesla-15088992", "https://last10k.com/sec-filings/tsla/0001193125-14-069681.htm"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
185	TSLA	yearly	2022	2022 was a year of operational triumphs and financial turbulence for Tesla. The company opened two new Gigafactories, broke delivery records again, and posted record revenue and profits — yet Elon Musk's acquisition of Twitter and the broader tech/growth stock sell-off caused Tesla's share price to collapse by approximately 65% for the year, destroying hundreds of billions in market capitalization and making TSLA the worst-performing stock in the S&P 500 in 2022.\n\nOn the operational side, Tesla had two landmark factory openings in April 2022. Gigafactory Texas opened with a splashy 'Cyber Rodeo' event on April 7, 2022, in Austin. The facility, which had been built in just under two years, began producing Model Y vehicles and was designated as Tesla's new global headquarters (Musk had officially relocated Tesla from California to Texas in late 2021). Just weeks later, Gigafactory Berlin opened on March 22, 2022, after an extended regulatory approval process. By March 2023, the Berlin factory had achieved 5,000 Model Y vehicles per week, hitting a major production milestone.\n\nFull-year deliveries in 2022 reached approximately 1.31 million vehicles, a 40% increase over 2021, setting another annual record. Revenue grew to approximately $81.5 billion, and net income hit a record $12.6 billion — more than double the $5.6 billion earned in 2021. Tesla's gross margins and operating margins were the highest in the company's history at the time, demonstrating the significant operating leverage that came from manufacturing scale.\n\nElon Musk's $44 billion acquisition of Twitter (completed October 2022) was the dominant negative factor for Tesla's stock. Musk sold approximately $15–20 billion worth of Tesla shares throughout 2022 to fund the Twitter deal, directly diluting Tesla investors and raising concerns about divided management attention. The overlap of the Twitter acquisition with a broader market sell-off — driven by rising interest rates and recession fears — proved devastating for Tesla's stock price. Many institutional investors and retail holders who had bought in during the 2020–2021 boom suffered enormous mark-to-market losses. Despite record operational performance, TSLA ended 2022 down approximately 65% from its peak.\n\nKey milestones in 2022 included:\n* **Gigafactory Texas Opens**: April 7, 'Cyber Rodeo'; Tesla's new global HQ in Austin.\n* **Gigafactory Berlin Opens**: March 22, 2022; Model Y production ramps throughout the year.\n* **1.31 Million Deliveries**: 40% year-over-year growth; new delivery record.\n* **Record Revenue of ~$81.5 Billion and Net Income of ~$12.6 Billion**: Best financial results in company history to that point.\n* **Musk Acquires Twitter**: $44B deal completed October 2022; Musk sells $15-20B in TSLA shares. TSLA stock falls ~65% for the year.	Tesla opened Gigafactory Texas and Gigafactory Berlin in April and March 2022, respectively, and delivered a record 1.31 million vehicles on revenue of $81.5 billion with net income of $12.6 billion — all company records. However, Elon Musk's $44 billion acquisition of Twitter, which required massive Tesla share sales, and the broader tech sell-off caused TSLA stock to plunge approximately 65% for the year — the worst performance in the S&P 500.	["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigafactory_Texas", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigafactory_Berlin-Brandenburg", "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/10/19/tesla-tsla-earnings-q3-2022-.html", "https://www.cnbc.com/2023/01/25/tesla-tsla-earnings-q4-2022.html", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
186	TSLA	yearly	2024	2024 was a difficult and defining year for Tesla, marked by its first annual delivery decline in over a decade, continued margin pressure from price competition, and a massive stock rebound driven by expectations around autonomous driving and robotics rather than near-term vehicle sales. The year tested investor conviction in Tesla's long-term narrative and highlighted both the brutally competitive EV market and the diverging paths of Tesla's traditional auto business versus its AI/autonomy ambitions.\n\nFor the full year, Tesla manufactured approximately 1.77 million vehicles and delivered approximately 1.79 million — down roughly 1–4% from 2023's records, marking the first annual delivery decline in the company's modern era. Total revenue was approximately $97.7 billion, essentially flat with 2023's $96.8 billion. Automotive revenues declined by about 6% to approximately $77 billion, as vehicle average selling prices continued to fall; however, the Energy Generation and Storage segment surged approximately 67% to $10.1 billion, and Services and Other grew about 27% to $10.5 billion. These non-automotive segments were emerging as meaningful contributors, partially cushioning the automotive softness. Net income was approximately $7.1 billion, down significantly from 2023, with automotive gross margins declining to approximately 18.4%.\n\nTesla responded to slowing demand by continuing price cuts in the US, Europe, and China, and by introducing new lower-cost variants and attractive financing. The company also announced a more affordable new Model 3 and Model Y refresh in select markets. BYD surpassed Tesla as the world's largest EV seller in several quarters during 2024, a watershed competitive milestone. The Chinese EV market became increasingly cutthroat, with dozens of local manufacturers competing aggressively on price, features, and technology.\n\nFrom an investor perspective, the most pivotal aspect of 2024 was Tesla's pivot in its narrative. Elon Musk increasingly positioned Tesla not as an automaker but as an AI and robotics company. Full Self-Driving (FSD) capability continued to improve, and Tesla announced plans for a purpose-built robotaxi vehicle (codenamed 'Cybercab') and expanded its Optimus humanoid robot program. Musk made bold promises about autonomous ride-hailing launching in 2025. TSLA shares ended 2024 dramatically higher than they started — up approximately 63% for the year — despite the operational headwinds, as investors bet on the autonomous driving future.\n\nKey milestones in 2024 included:\n* **First Annual Delivery Decline**: ~1.79M deliveries, down roughly 1% from 2023 — the first decline in over a decade.\n* **Energy Segment Grows 67%**: Energy storage deployments become a meaningful revenue driver.\n* **Revenue of ~$97.7 Billion**: Essentially flat year-over-year; automotive decline offset by energy and services.\n* **Cybercab and Optimus Reveals**: Tesla reveals purpose-built robotaxi vehicle and ramps humanoid robot development.\n* **Stock +63%**: Driven by autonomy and AI expectations despite weaker core auto results.	Tesla posted its first annual delivery decline in over a decade in 2024, delivering approximately 1.79 million vehicles on revenue of $97.7 billion — essentially flat with 2023 — as automotive revenues fell 6% amid continued price cuts and fierce competition from BYD and Chinese EV makers. The Energy segment surged 67%, and TSLA shares rallied 63% for the year as investors focused on Tesla's autonomous driving and robotics ambitions rather than its current vehicle business.	["https://ir.tesla.com/press-release/tesla-releases-fourth-quarter-and-full-year-2024-financial-results", "https://ir.tesla.com/press-release/tesla-fourth-quarter-2024-production-deliveries-and-deployments", "https://www.electrive.com/2025/01/30/tesla-releases-2024-financial-results-revenue-is-up-but-profits-are-down/", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
187	TSLA	five_year	2020-2024	Between 2020 and 2024, Tesla achieved its first sustained profitability, crossed 1 million and then 1.5 million annual deliveries, joined the S&P 500, opened two new Gigafactories, delivered the Cybertruck, and transitioned its public narrative from electric vehicle leader to autonomous driving and robotics AI company — all while facing intensifying competition from Chinese EV manufacturers led by BYD.\n\n2020: First Full-Year Profit, Model Y, S&P 500 Inclusion Tesla delivered approximately 499,550 vehicles and reported its first full-year GAAP net income of $721 million, proving the electric vehicle business was commercially sustainable at scale. The Model Y compact crossover launched in March 2020 and quickly ramped to become Tesla's highest-volume vehicle. Despite a COVID-19-related factory shutdown in spring 2020, Tesla recovered strongly in H2. The year's climax came on December 21, 2020, when Tesla was added to the S&P 500 index — the most valuable company ever included at the time of addition, with a market cap exceeding $660 billion. TSLA stock surged approximately 740% for the year, one of the largest single-year gains for any mega-cap stock in US history.\n\n2021: Record Deliveries, $1 Trillion Market Cap, New Gigafactories Tesla delivered 936,222 vehicles in 2021 — an 87% increase over 2020 — and reported revenue of $53.8 billion and net income of $5.6 billion. In October 2021, a Hertz order for 100,000 vehicles helped push Tesla's market capitalization past $1 trillion, making it the most valuable automaker by far and the lowest-revenue company ever to achieve a $1 trillion valuation. Gigafactory Texas (Austin) and Gigafactory Berlin (Brandenburg, Germany) were both near completion, with Texas beginning limited Model Y production by year-end and Berlin's 'Giga Fest' celebration held in October.\n\n2022: Two Gigafactories Open, Record Revenue, Twitter Distraction Gigafactory Texas opened in April ('Cyber Rodeo') and Gigafactory Berlin opened in March, giving Tesla four Gigafactories across three continents. Full-year deliveries reached approximately 1.31 million vehicles, a 40% increase, on record revenue of $81.5 billion and record net income of $12.6 billion. The year's dark cloud was Elon Musk's $44 billion acquisition of Twitter in October, for which he sold $15–20 billion in Tesla shares, diluting investors and dividing management attention. TSLA stock fell approximately 65% for the year — the worst performance in the S&P 500 — despite the record operational results.\n\n2023: Cybertruck Deliveries, Record Volume, Margin Pressure Tesla delivered a record 1.8 million vehicles in 2023, with the Model Y becoming the world's best-selling car of any kind — the first time an electric vehicle had ever achieved this global milestone. The long-awaited Cybertruck finally reached customers on November 30, 2023. However, Tesla launched aggressive global price cuts to defend market share against BYD and other competitors, significantly compressing automotive gross margins. Revenue reached approximately $96.8 billion, but underlying profitability declined from 2022 peaks. Price cuts ranging from $4,400 to $20,000 across models sparked debate about the sustainability of Tesla's pricing power.\n\n2024: First Delivery Decline, Energy Surge, Autonomy Pivot Tesla experienced its first annual delivery decline in over a decade in 2024, delivering approximately 1.79 million vehicles on revenue of approximately $97.7 billion — essentially flat with 2023. BYD surpassed Tesla in quarterly global BEV sales multiple times during the year. Automotive revenue declined 6% to approximately $77 billion, partially offset by the Energy Generation and Storage segment surging 67% to $10.1 billion. Elon Musk pivoted Tesla's investor narrative toward autonomy and robotics: the company revealed a purpose-built robotaxi (Cybercab) and accelerated its Optimus humanoid robot program. Despite operational headwinds, TSLA shares rallied approximately 63% for the year on autonomous driving enthusiasm.\n\n| Year | Revenue | Deliveries | Net Income | Key Event |\n|------|---------|------------|------------|-----------|\n| 2020 | $31.5B | ~499,550 | $721M | S&P 500 inclusion; first full-year profit; Model Y launch |\n| 2021 | $53.8B | ~936,222 | $5.6B | $1 trillion market cap; Gigafactories Texas & Berlin near completion |\n| 2022 | $81.5B | ~1.31M | $12.6B | Gigafactories Texas & Berlin open; Twitter acquisition |\n| 2023 | $96.8B | ~1.80M | ~$15B* | Cybertruck deliveries; Model Y world's best-selling car |\n| 2024 | $97.7B | ~1.79M | ~$7.1B | First delivery decline; Energy +67%; autonomy narrative |\n\n*Includes $5.9B one-time tax benefit; adjusted net income approximately $10.9B.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
188	TSLA	yearly	2018	2018 was arguably the most turbulent year in Tesla's history as a public company. The Model 3 production ramp continued to be agonizing in the first half, Elon Musk's erratic behavior on social media led to an SEC enforcement action, and Tesla came dangerously close to running out of cash — yet by year-end the company had largely righted the ship, demonstrating an extraordinary operational turnaround.\n\nThe year began with Tesla still stuck in 'production hell.' In Q1 2018, Tesla produced only about 9,766 Model 3 vehicles — far short of its targets. The company was burning through cash at an alarming rate, and analysts openly debated whether Tesla would need to raise capital or face insolvency. Elon Musk, working 120-hour weeks by his own account and reportedly sleeping at the factory, made the controversial decision to dismantle much of the automated production line and rebuild it with more human workers — an embarrassing admission that the hyper-automated approach had been a costly mistake. The gamble paid off: by Q3 2018, Tesla hit its target of producing 5,000 Model 3s per week, delivering 55,840 Model 3 vehicles in Q3 alone. Q3 2018 also delivered Tesla's first profit in two years, with net income of $311 million.\n\nThe year's most shocking episode came in August 2018, when Musk tweeted that he was considering taking Tesla private at $420 per share and that funding was 'secured' — a claim that turned out to be false. The tweet sent Tesla's stock soaring before the plan was abandoned, and the Securities and Exchange Commission sued Musk for securities fraud. Musk settled with the SEC in September 2018, agreeing to step down as Tesla's chairman for three years (though he remained CEO), and both he and Tesla each paid $20 million fines. Tesla was also required to have a committee review Musk's public communications.\n\nFinancially, 2018 was Tesla's biggest revenue year to date. Total revenues rose to approximately $21.46 billion, nearly doubling from $11.76 billion in 2017. Model 3 had now become the best-selling EV in the United States and the best-selling premium sedan overall by the end of the year. Full-year deliveries reached approximately 245,240 vehicles. Despite the strong revenue growth, Tesla still posted a full-year net loss of approximately $976 million, as the cost of the Model 3 ramp and interest payments weighed heavily.\n\nKey milestones in 2018 included:\n* **Model 3 Ramp Achieved**: 5,000/week production rate hit in Q3; Q3 net income of $311M — first profit in two years.\n* **SEC Settlement**: Musk's 'funding secured' tweet leads to $40M in fines and Musk stepping down as chairman.\n* **Revenue of ~$21.46 Billion**: Revenue nearly doubles year-over-year.\n* **~245,240 Deliveries**: Tesla's largest delivery year by far to that point.\n* **Near-Bankruptcy Fears**: Mid-year cash burn raised serious solvency concerns before Q3 turnaround.	Tesla endured its most turbulent year in 2018 — near-insolvency fears, Elon Musk's SEC settlement over a 'funding secured' tweet, and agonizing Model 3 production struggles — before an extraordinary turnaround in Q3 delivered 5,000 Model 3s per week and a $311 million quarterly profit. Full-year revenues approximately doubled to $21.46 billion with 245,240 vehicles delivered, though the company still posted a net loss of $976 million for the year.	["https://ir.tesla.com/news-releases/news-release-details/tesla-q3-2018-vehicle-production-and-deliveries", "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000156459018024902/tsla-ex991_6.htm", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/", "https://www.thestreet.com/technology/history-of-tesla-15088992"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
189	TSLA	yearly	2011	2011 was a transitional year for Tesla as it wound down Roadster production and prepared for the pivotal launch of the Model S. The company completed delivery of the final Roadsters to customers early in the year; total worldwide Roadster deliveries exceeded 2,150 units over the model's lifetime. While the Roadster had proven Tesla's technical capabilities, it was never intended to be a high-volume product — the company's real ambitions lay with the upcoming Model S sedan.\n\nFinancially, Tesla reported full-year 2011 revenues of approximately $204 million, up roughly 75% from 2010. This growth was driven primarily by continued Roadster deliveries and development services revenue from Toyota and other partners. However, the company posted a net loss of approximately $254 million for the year, reflecting substantial investment in Model S development, manufacturing preparation at the Fremont factory, and expansion of its Supercharger and service network infrastructure. Tesla's workforce grew significantly as it hired engineers and manufacturing talent to prepare for the Model S ramp.\n\nThe Fremont factory (officially purchased in 2010) became a major focus in 2011, with Tesla investing heavily to retrofit and modernize the facility. The factory, which covers 5.3 million square feet, was being transformed into a state-of-the-art electric vehicle manufacturing plant. Tesla also worked on developing its direct sales model — selling cars through company-owned showrooms rather than franchised dealerships — which was a controversial and legally challenged approach in many U.S. states.\n\nOn the product side, Tesla unveiled the Model S prototype and began taking reservations. The Model S was designed from the ground up as an electric vehicle, unlike the Roadster which was adapted from a Lotus chassis. It featured a large touch screen interface, over-the-air software updates, and was planned to offer multiple battery pack options with ranges from 160 to 300+ miles. Elon Musk frequently discussed a long-term product roadmap that would eventually include an affordable mass-market car, foreshadowing the Model 3.	Tesla concluded Roadster production in 2011 with over 2,150 total deliveries and reported $204 million in revenue while investing heavily in Model S development. The company continued to build out its Fremont factory, expanded its team, and took reservations for the upcoming Model S sedan. Net losses widened to approximately $254 million as Tesla laid the groundwork for its transition to a volume manufacturer.	["https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000119312512063402/d299083dex991.htm", "https://www.tesla.com/blog/tesla-motors-reports-first-quarter-2011-results", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://www.thestreet.com/technology/history-of-tesla-15088992"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
190	TSLA	master	entire_history	Tesla, Inc. has traced one of the most dramatic corporate arcs in modern business history: from a scrappy Silicon Valley startup that nearly went bankrupt in 2008 to a company that, at its peak in 2021, commanded a $1.2 trillion market capitalization and fundamentally reshaped the global automotive industry. Founded in 2003 by Martin Eberhard and Marc Tarpenning and transformed by Elon Musk's leadership and investment from 2004 onward, Tesla went public on June 29, 2010 at $17 per share on the NASDAQ — the first American automaker IPO since Ford in 1956. In its first decade as a public company, Tesla launched four transformative vehicles: the Model S luxury sedan (2012), the Model X SUV (2015), the mass-market Model 3 (2017), and the Model Y crossover (2020). Each launch expanded Tesla's addressable market and drove extraordinary revenue growth — from $117 million in 2010 to over $96 billion by 2023 — while also testing the company's manufacturing capabilities to the breaking point. The 2017–2018 'production hell' of the Model 3 ramp, Elon Musk's SEC settlement over a market-moving tweet, near-insolvency scares, and a 65% stock decline in 2022 all underscored the extreme volatility that defined Tesla's journey. Yet the company repeatedly overcame these crises, posting its first full-year profit in 2020 and achieving record deliveries and financial results in 2022–2023.\n\nTesla's geographic expansion was equally ambitious. The Gigafactory Nevada (opened 2016, in partnership with Panasonic) was Tesla's first attempt to vertically integrate battery cell manufacturing at scale. Gigafactory Shanghai (opened 2019, built in under 12 months — a global automotive record) became Tesla's largest single production hub and unlocked the crucial Chinese market. Gigafactory Berlin and Gigafactory Texas both opened in early 2022, giving Tesla manufacturing capacity on four continents and enabling it to deliver over 1.3 million vehicles that year and 1.8 million in 2023. The 2016 acquisition of SolarCity, controversial at the time, broadened Tesla into solar energy and home battery storage; by 2024, the Energy Generation and Storage segment was generating over $10 billion in annual revenue, growing at 67% year-over-year and partially insulating Tesla from automotive headwinds.\n\nBy 2025, Tesla stood at an inflection point. Deliveries had declined for two consecutive years (1.79 million in 2024, 1.636 million in 2025), squeezed by BYD's global overtaking in battery-electric vehicle sales, the elimination of the US federal EV tax credit, and significant brand damage tied to Elon Musk's prominent role in President Trump's Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE). Yet the company's technological ambitions had never been more expansive: a supervised robotaxi service launched in Austin mid-2025, FSD v14 delivered dramatic safety improvements, the Optimus humanoid robot program accelerated toward commercial production, and energy storage deployments more than doubled to 46.7 GWh. Tesla's fundamental question — whether it is ultimately an automaker, an energy company, or an AI and robotics platform — remained unresolved, but the stakes and the ambition had never been higher.\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
196	TSLA	yearly	2025	2025 was one of the most consequential and controversial years in Tesla's history, not primarily for its vehicle business — which continued to struggle — but for the intense intersection of Elon Musk's political activities and Tesla's brand, and for the company's accelerating push into autonomous driving and robotics that investors hope will redefine its long-term trajectory.\n\nOn the financial side, Tesla delivered approximately 1.636 million vehicles for the full year 2025, an 8.6% decline from 2024's 1.79 million deliveries. This was the largest single-year decline in Tesla's modern history and marked the second consecutive year of falling deliveries. Full-year revenue came in at approximately $94.8 billion, down about 3% from 2024's $97.7 billion. The declines were attributed to multiple compounding factors: intensifying competition from BYD (which sold approximately 2.26 million pure battery-electric vehicles in 2025, overtaking Tesla in global BEV sales), the elimination of the $7,500 US federal EV tax credit under the new administration, aging core model lineup, and — critically — significant damage to the Tesla brand associated with Musk's political activities.\n\nElon Musk's prominent role leading President Trump's Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), launched in early 2025, generated enormous controversy. The association with divisive political actions led to organized boycotts of Tesla vehicles in Europe and North America, with some dealerships reporting notable drops in foot traffic and owner communities reporting social stigma. Brand Finance estimated Tesla's brand value fell approximately 36% in 2025, losing approximately $15.4 billion in a single year — one of the largest brand value declines ever recorded. Despite the reputational challenges, Musk maintained that his government role was temporary and essential for US fiscal reform.\n\nOn the technology side, 2025 saw meaningful progress in Tesla's autonomy ambitions. FSD v14, released in late 2025, showed dramatic claimed improvements in safety metrics. A supervised robotaxi service launched in Austin, Texas in mid-2025 using Model Y vehicles with no safety driver, and subsequently expanded to the San Francisco Bay Area. Tesla surpassed one million FSD subscribers — a 38% year-over-year increase. The Optimus humanoid robot program accelerated, with Musk claiming production of several thousand units by year-end for internal use and early commercial deployment. Energy storage deployments more than doubled to a record 46.7 GWh for the year, reaffirming Tesla Energy as a high-growth business separate from its automotive operations.\n\nKey milestones in 2025 included:\n* **1.636 Million Deliveries**: Down 8.6% — second consecutive year of decline and the steepest drop in the company's history.\n* **Revenue of ~$94.8 Billion**: Down ~3% year-over-year; BYD overtakes Tesla in global BEV sales.\n* **Robotaxi Service Launches**: Austin robotaxi service begins mid-2025 with no safety driver; expands to Bay Area.\n* **FSD v14 Released**: Claimed 20-fold improvement in safety; over 1 million FSD subscribers.\n* **Brand Damage from DOGE**: Tesla brand value estimated down 36% ($15.4B loss) tied to Musk's political role.\n* **Energy Storage Record**: 46.7 GWh deployed — more than double prior year.	Tesla delivered 1.636 million vehicles in 2025 — down 8.6%, the steepest annual decline in company history — as BYD overtook Tesla in global battery-electric vehicle sales and Elon Musk's DOGE role triggered significant brand damage. Revenue fell approximately 3% to $94.8 billion. However, Tesla launched a supervised robotaxi service in Austin, released FSD v14 with major safety improvements, crossed one million FSD subscribers, and more than doubled energy storage deployments to 46.7 GWh.	["https://www.teslarati.com/tesla-q4-fy-2025-vehicle-delivery-production-results/", "https://assets-ir.tesla.com/tesla-contents/IR/TSLA-Q4-2025-Update.pdf", "https://www.tesevo.com/blogs/tesla-news/teslas-q3-2025-earnings-advancements-in-fsd-robotaxi-rollout-and-ai-reasoning", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/", "https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2025/04/05/tesla-robotaxi-deadline-looms-as-political-backlash-builds-over-musk.html", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
197	TSLA	yearly	2020	2020 was a watershed year for Tesla — the year the company finally achieved its first full-year profit, launched the Model Y, got added to the S&P 500, and saw its stock price surge nearly 740%. Despite the disruption of the COVID-19 pandemic, which forced a temporary shutdown of the Fremont factory in spring 2020, Tesla executed at a level that transformed it from a growth story into one of the most valuable companies in the world.\n\nTesla delivered approximately 499,550 vehicles for full-year 2020, narrowly missing the symbolic 500,000 milestone. Despite the pandemic-related production halt in Q1 and Q2, Tesla made up lost ground in the second half and ended the year with record quarterly deliveries in Q4. Total revenues grew to approximately $31.5 billion. Crucially, Tesla reported its first full-year GAAP net income of approximately $721 million — small in absolute terms, but enormously significant symbolically, proving that the company's economic model was viable. Operating margins hit an industry-leading 6.3%.\n\nThe Model Y, Tesla's compact crossover SUV based on the Model 3 platform, launched in March 2020, earlier than originally planned. Initial production was at the Fremont factory, and by year-end Tesla had also begun Model Y production at Gigafactory Shanghai. The Model Y would prove to be Tesla's best-selling vehicle and eventually the best-selling car of any kind globally. China production ramped rapidly throughout the year, with the Shanghai factory achieving over 5,000 Model Y units per week by year-end.\n\nThe most dramatic financial event of 2020 was Tesla's inclusion in the S&P 500 index on December 21, 2020. Tesla was the most valuable company ever added to the index at the time of addition, immediately becoming the sixth-largest member by market capitalization. The index addition triggered massive mandatory buying from index funds and contributed to a spectacular stock performance: TSLA shares rose approximately 740% for the year. Tesla's market capitalization exceeded $660 billion by the time of inclusion — more than the next nine largest automakers combined. The company also raised capital multiple times during the year, strengthening its balance sheet.\n\nKey milestones in 2020 included:\n* **First Full-Year GAAP Profit**: $721 million net income — the first in Tesla's history.\n* **~499,550 Deliveries**: Near-miss on 500,000 target despite pandemic disruptions.\n* **Model Y Launch**: March 2020; quickly becomes Tesla's highest-volume vehicle.\n* **S&P 500 Inclusion**: December 21, 2020 — most valuable company ever added to the index.\n* **Stock +740%**: One of the largest single-year gains for a mega-cap stock in US history.	Tesla achieved its first full-year GAAP profit in 2020 ($721 million), delivered approximately 499,550 vehicles, and saw its stock surge nearly 740% for the year. The Model Y launched in March 2020 and quickly ramped to become Tesla's top-selling model. Tesla was added to the S&P 500 on December 21, 2020 — the most valuable company ever included at the time of addition — with a market cap exceeding $660 billion.	["https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000156459021002645/tsla-ex991_99.htm", "https://www.nasdaq.com/articles/with-sp-500-inclusion-tesla-stock-looks-even-more-overvalued-2020-11-19", "https://www.researchaffiliates.com/publications/articles/819-tesla-the-largest-cap-stock-ever", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesla,_Inc.", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
198	TSLA	yearly	2016	2016 was a pivotal and controversial year for Tesla, defined by three major events: the opening of the Nevada Gigafactory, the $2.6 billion acquisition of SolarCity, and the unveiling of the mass-market Model 3. Together, these developments transformed Tesla from an electric luxury carmaker into a vertically integrated clean energy company — though not without significant criticism from investors and analysts.\n\nThe Nevada Gigafactory held its grand opening celebration on July 29, 2016, marking the beginning of a new era in battery manufacturing. Though the facility was only partially complete, it had already begun limited production of Tesla Powerwall and Powerpack energy storage products earlier in the year using cells produced elsewhere. The Gigafactory was central to Tesla's strategy of driving down the cost of battery cells through economies of scale — Musk projected that at full capacity, the facility would produce more lithium-ion batteries annually than the entire global industry had in 2013. Panasonic began manufacturing battery cells at the Nevada site in early 2017.\n\nIn March 2016, Elon Musk unveiled the Model 3 at a dramatic event in Hawthorne, California. Priced starting at $35,000, the Model 3 was Tesla's long-promised affordable electric car for the masses. The reaction was extraordinary: Tesla received approximately 400,000 reservations in the first week, each backed by a $1,000 deposit. It was the largest advance order in automotive history and demonstrated pent-up demand for an affordable Tesla. However, the Model 3 was not scheduled to begin production until late 2017.\n\nThe SolarCity acquisition was the year's most contentious development. Tesla acquired SolarCity — a solar panel installation company co-founded by Musk's cousins and in which Musk himself was chairman — for approximately $2.6 billion in an all-stock deal that closed in November 2016. Critics called it a related-party bailout, as SolarCity had been struggling financially. Proponents, including Musk, argued it created the world's first vertically integrated sustainable energy company, combining solar generation, battery storage, and electric vehicles. Approximately 85% of Tesla shareholders ultimately voted in favor of the acquisition.\n\nTotal vehicle deliveries for 2016 reached approximately 76,230 units, and revenue grew to approximately $7 billion — essentially doubling from 2015's $4.05 billion — buoyed by Model S and Model X volume growth and the addition of SolarCity's revenue. Net losses, however, also deepened significantly.\n\nKey milestones in 2016 included:\n* **Model 3 Unveiled**: March 2016 — 400,000 reservations in the first week at $35,000 starting price.\n* **Gigafactory Nevada Opens**: Grand opening July 29, 2016.\n* **SolarCity Acquired**: $2.6 billion deal closes November 2016, creating Tesla Energy division.\n* **76,230 Deliveries**: Strong growth across Model S and Model X.\n* **Revenue of ~$7 Billion**: Doubled from prior year.	Tesla unveiled the mass-market Model 3 in March 2016, collecting approximately 400,000 reservations in the first week. The Nevada Gigafactory celebrated its grand opening in July, and Tesla acquired SolarCity for $2.6 billion in November, becoming a vertically integrated clean energy company. Total deliveries reached approximately 76,230 vehicles and revenue approximately doubled to $7 billion, though the SolarCity deal drew intense scrutiny over potential conflicts of interest.	["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigafactory_Nevada", "https://thetechportal.com/2016/07/27/tesla-finally-opens-gigafactory/", "https://cleantechnica.com/2021/07/21/teslas-2016-solarcity-acquisition-was-approved-by-85-of-teslas-shareholders/", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
199	TSLA	five_year	2015-2019	Between 2015 and 2019, Tesla expanded from a single-vehicle luxury automaker into a multi-product, globally manufacturing company — launching the Model X, Model 3, and Model Y, opening its Shanghai Gigafactory, and surviving both the SolarCity controversy and the epic 'production hell' of the Model 3 ramp.\n\n2015: Model X and Tesla Energy Tesla launched the Model X SUV in September 2015, featuring distinctive falcon-wing rear doors and seating for seven adults. Manufacturing complexities slowed the initial ramp, but combined deliveries of Model S and Model X reached approximately 50,580 vehicles for the year. Revenue grew to approximately $4.05 billion. In April 2015, Elon Musk unveiled the Powerwall and Powerpack energy storage products, formally launching the Tesla Energy division and signaling that Tesla's long-term ambitions extended well beyond automobiles. The Nevada Gigafactory continued its rapid vertical construction throughout the year.\n\n2016: Model 3 Unveiled, Gigafactory Opens, SolarCity Acquired In March 2016, the mass-market Model 3 was revealed to extraordinary demand — approximately 400,000 reservations in the first week, at $1,000 deposits each, representing the largest advance order in automotive history. The Nevada Gigafactory celebrated its grand opening on July 29, 2016. Most controversially, Tesla acquired solar company SolarCity for approximately $2.6 billion in November 2016, creating the world's first vertically integrated sustainable energy company but drawing criticism over potential conflicts of interest. Deliveries reached approximately 76,230 vehicles, and revenue approximately doubled to $7 billion.\n\n2017: Model 3 Launch and 'Production Hell' The Model 3 began customer deliveries in July 2017, but the subsequent production ramp proved far more difficult than Tesla had projected. Elon Musk called it "production hell" as an over-reliance on automation created bottlenecks; by Q4 2017, Tesla had produced only about 2,425 Model 3 vehicles versus a target of 5,000/week. Despite the struggle, Model S and Model X together delivered over 101,000 units — the first time Tesla crossed 100,000 annual deliveries. Revenue grew 68% to approximately $11.76 billion, but net losses widened to approximately $1.96 billion. Tesla also revealed the Semi and a new Roadster in November 2017.\n\n2018: The Turnaround and the Tweet 2018 saw Tesla navigate its most turbulent stretch as a public company. Model 3 production finally hit 5,000/week in Q3 after Musk famously dismantled much of the automation and rebuilt with more human workers. Q3 delivered Tesla's first profit in two years ($311 million). Elon Musk's "funding secured" tweet in August triggered an SEC securities fraud action; he settled for $20 million, stepped down as chairman, and agreed to pre-approval of certain communications. Despite the turmoil, full-year revenue nearly doubled to $21.46 billion and deliveries reached approximately 245,240 vehicles.\n\n2019: Gigafactory Shanghai, Cybertruck Reveal Tesla delivered approximately 367,500 vehicles in 2019 — 50% more than 2018 — and generated revenue of approximately $24.5 billion. The Model 3 became the best-selling premium sedan in the US. Most remarkably, Gigafactory Shanghai went from groundbreaking in January to producing Made-in-China Model 3s by October, with first customer deliveries on December 30 — just 357 days from dirt to delivery, a global automotive record. Tesla also achieved four consecutive profitable quarters for the first time, qualifying for potential S&P 500 inclusion. In November, the Cybertruck was unveiled to polarizing reactions but collected 250,000+ reservations.\n\n| Year | Revenue | Deliveries | Key Event |\n|------|---------|------------|-----------|\n| 2015 | $4.05B | ~50,580 | Model X launch; Tesla Energy unveiled |\n| 2016 | $7.0B | ~76,230 | Model 3 revealed; Gigafactory Nevada opens; SolarCity acquired |\n| 2017 | $11.76B | ~101,312 | Model 3 launch; 'production hell' |\n| 2018 | $21.46B | ~245,240 | Model 3 ramp achieved; SEC settlement |\n| 2019 | $24.5B | ~367,500 | Gigafactory Shanghai opens; Cybertruck revealed |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
200	TSLA	yearly	2012	2012 was one of the most important years in Tesla's history: the year the company launched the Model S and proved it could actually build and sell a world-class automobile at scale. After years of promises and delays, Tesla began customer deliveries of the Model S in June 2012, an event that transformed Tesla from a scrappy startup into a credible automaker. The Model S was immediately recognized as an exceptional vehicle, combining a 265-mile range (on the top 85 kWh variant), blistering acceleration, and an interior centered around a 17-inch touchscreen — unlike anything else on the road.\n\nBy year's end, Tesla had manufactured approximately 3,100 Model S vehicles and delivered around 2,650 of them to customers. While these numbers were modest, the execution of the launch was a critical proof of concept. The Model S received near-universal critical acclaim. Motor Trend awarded it Car of the Year 2013, only the second time in the magazine's history that the award went to a car without a traditional internal combustion engine. Consumer Reports gave it a near-perfect score and called it one of the best cars they had ever tested.\n\nFinancially, 2012 was a heavy spending year. Total revenues rose to approximately $413 million, more than doubling from 2011's $204 million, with automotive revenues reaching $386 million as Model S deliveries ramped. However, net losses also increased sharply to approximately $396 million as Tesla invested in manufacturing infrastructure, the Supercharger network, and Model S production tooling. Tesla began building out its proprietary Supercharger fast-charging network in 2012, initially in California, offering free rapid charging to Model S owners — a major competitive advantage and a signal of Tesla's commitment to removing range anxiety as a barrier to adoption.\n\nKey milestones in 2012 included:\n* **Model S Deliveries Begin**: June 2012 — the first production Model S vehicles delivered to customers in the US.\n* **Motor Trend Car of the Year**: Model S wins the prestigious award, generating enormous press coverage.\n* **Supercharger Network Launch**: Tesla begins installing free fast-charging stations along major US corridors.\n* **Revenue Doubles**: Total revenue of $413M, up from $204M in 2011, driven by Model S sales.	Tesla launched the Model S in June 2012, delivering approximately 2,650 vehicles by year-end and posting $413 million in total revenue — more than double the prior year. The Model S earned Motor Trend's Car of the Year award and near-perfect Consumer Reports scores, establishing Tesla as a world-class automaker. The Supercharger fast-charging network also launched in 2012, though net losses widened to $396 million as the company invested heavily in production and infrastructure.	["https://www.torquenews.com/1075/tesla-motors-reports-massive-losses-2012-profitability-1st-quarter-2013", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://medium.com/@arindamb/economic-and-financial-dynamics-of-tesla-2003-present-with-comparison-to-byd-deaca73c3660", "https://www.thestreet.com/technology/history-of-tesla-15088992"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
201	TSLA	five_year	2010-2014	Between 2010 and 2014, Tesla transformed from a niche electric sports car startup into a credible, internationally recognized automaker with a flagship luxury sedan and the beginnings of a global manufacturing empire.\n\n2010: The IPO Year Tesla went public on June 29, 2010, raising $226 million on the NASDAQ at $17 per share — the first American automaker IPO since Ford in 1956. At the time, Tesla's sole product was the hand-built Roadster, a high-performance electric sports car. Revenue for 2010 was approximately $117 million, and the company was deeply unprofitable. However, the year also brought a $465 million Department of Energy loan and a $50 million strategic investment from Toyota, along with the acquisition of the giant Fremont, California factory — formerly a Toyota-GM joint venture — which would become Tesla's primary manufacturing plant.\n\n2011: Winding Down the Roadster, Preparing the Model S With Roadster deliveries nearing their end (total lifetime deliveries exceeded 2,150 units), Tesla focused almost entirely on preparing for the Model S. Revenue grew to approximately $204 million, up 75% from 2010, but net losses deepened to around $254 million as the company invested heavily in the Fremont factory retrofit, Model S development, and its emerging direct-sales retail model. Tesla's workforce expanded substantially as it brought in the engineering and manufacturing talent necessary for its next chapter.\n\n2012: Model S Launches and Changes Everything Tesla began delivering the Model S in June 2012, and the sedan immediately received universal acclaim — Motor Trend's Car of the Year, a near-perfect Consumer Reports score, and strong customer satisfaction ratings. By year-end, approximately 2,650 Model S vehicles had been delivered. Revenue more than doubled to approximately $413 million, though net losses widened to $396 million as production costs were high and scale was still limited. The Supercharger fast-charging network also launched in 2012, offering free rapid charging to Model S owners along major US corridors.\n\n2013: First Profit, DOE Loan Repaid, Stock Surge 2013 was Tesla's breakthrough year. The company reported its first profitable quarter (Q1 2013) and delivered over 22,000 Model S vehicles for the year, beating its guidance of 20,000 units. Revenue rocketed nearly fivefold to approximately $2.01 billion. The company repaid its entire $465 million DOE loan in May — nine years ahead of schedule — and TSLA stock surged approximately 344% for the year. The Model S received a record 99/100 Consumer Reports rating and became the best-selling large luxury sedan in the US by Q4.\n\n2014: Model X Announced, Gigafactory Planned Tesla delivered approximately 31,655 vehicles in 2014, growing revenue to $3.2 billion. The defining strategic move was the announcement of the Nevada Gigafactory in partnership with Panasonic — an audacious plan to build the world's largest building by footprint and produce more lithium-ion batteries than the entire global industry had in 2013. Elon Musk opened Tesla's patent portfolio to the public to accelerate EV adoption industry-wide, and the Model D (dual-motor AWD variant) launched with Autopilot hardware.\n\n| Year | Revenue | Key Event |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| 2010 | $117M | NASDAQ IPO at $17/share |\n| 2011 | $204M | Roadster wind-down; Model S preparation |\n| 2012 | $413M | Model S launch; Supercharger network begins |\n| 2013 | $2.01B | First profitable quarter; DOE loan repaid |\n| 2014 | $3.2B | Gigafactory announced; Model D/Autopilot |\n	\N	\N	2026-03-26 22:49:07
202	TSLA	yearly	2014	2014 was a year of ambitious expansion for Tesla, highlighted by the announcement of the Gigafactory in Nevada, strong Model S delivery growth, and a stock valuation that left some investors questioning whether the hype had outrun the fundamentals. Despite these concerns, Tesla executed on many of its operational goals and continued to disrupt the automotive industry.\n\nModel S deliveries grew substantially in 2014, reaching approximately 31,655 vehicles for the year — a more than 40% increase over 2013. By year-end, Tesla had delivered a cumulative total of nearly 57,000 Model S vehicles worldwide. Revenue rose to approximately $3.2 billion, up from $2.01 billion in 2013, continuing Tesla's extraordinary growth trajectory. However, the company remained unprofitable on a GAAP basis, posting a net loss of around $294 million as it invested aggressively in manufacturing expansion, the Supercharger network, and R&D.\n\nThe most consequential announcement of 2014 was the Gigafactory. In February 2014, Tesla announced plans to build a massive lithium-ion battery manufacturing facility that would be the largest building in the world by footprint. The site in Sparks, Nevada was officially announced in September 2014, and construction began immediately. Tesla's stated goal was to produce enough batteries by 2020 to match the entire global production of lithium-ion batteries in 2013 — a staggering ambition. The Gigafactory partnership with Panasonic was central to this plan, with Panasonic investing in battery cell manufacturing equipment within the facility.\n\nIn June 2014, Elon Musk made a surprise move by opening Tesla's entire patent portfolio to the public, pledging not to sue competitors who used Tesla's technology in good faith. This 'open source' approach was intended to accelerate EV adoption industry-wide. Tesla also unveiled the Model D in October 2014, a dual-motor all-wheel-drive version of the Model S with significantly improved performance and range, and introduced Autopilot hardware in all new Model S vehicles.\n\nKey milestones in 2014 included:\n* **Gigafactory Announcement**: Nevada Gigafactory announced in February, groundbreaking begins mid-year — targeting 35 GWh of battery cell production annually.\n* **31,655 Model S Deliveries**: 40%+ year-over-year growth; cumulative deliveries reach ~57,000.\n* **Revenue of $3.2 Billion**: Continued strong growth trajectory.\n* **Open-Source Patents**: Elon Musk opens Tesla's patent portfolio to accelerate EV adoption.\n* **Model D and Autopilot**: Dual-motor AWD Model S unveiled; Autopilot hardware included in all new cars.	Tesla delivered 31,655 Model S vehicles in 2014, growing revenue to $3.2 billion, as the company announced its landmark Gigafactory in Nevada with Panasonic as manufacturing partner. Elon Musk opened Tesla's entire patent portfolio to the public in June, and the Model D dual-motor variant launched with Autopilot hardware. Despite strong growth, Tesla posted a net loss of around $294 million as investments in manufacturing and infrastructure intensified.	["https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000156459015001031/tsla-10k_20141231.htm", "https://www.fool.com/investing/general/2015/02/22/tesla-motors-inc-in-2014-3-soaring-charts-and-1-fl.aspx", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigafactory_Nevada", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc."]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
203	TSLA	yearly	2015	2015 was a significant year for Tesla marked by the highly anticipated launch of the Model X SUV, continued Model S growth, and meaningful progress at the Nevada Gigafactory. The year demonstrated both Tesla's product creativity and its ongoing challenge of scaling manufacturing reliably.\n\nThe Model X officially launched with customer deliveries beginning in late September 2015, initially to reservation holders who had waited years. The Model X was a distinctive vehicle: a full-size electric SUV with falcon-wing rear doors, seating for up to seven adults, and some of the most advanced safety features available in any production car. However, the Model X's complexity — particularly the falcon-wing doors and second-row seats — created significant manufacturing challenges that would persist into 2016. Initial production was slow, and Tesla delivered only a few hundred Model X vehicles in 2015, far below demand.\n\nModel S deliveries continued to grow, and total 2015 vehicle deliveries reached approximately 50,580 units across both models, up from about 31,655 in 2014. Annual revenue grew to approximately $4.05 billion, another year of substantial growth. Tesla expanded aggressively internationally, establishing its presence in China, Hong Kong, and other Asian markets, though China would prove challenging due to local regulatory hurdles and consumer concerns about the Supercharger network's coverage.\n\nA major strategic development in 2015 was the launch of the Tesla Energy division. In April 2015, Elon Musk unveiled the Powerwall (home battery) and Powerpack (commercial battery) products at a dramatic event in Hawthorne, California. The Powerwall was a 7–10 kWh wall-mounted battery designed to store solar energy or grid electricity, priced at $3,500–$3,500. The announcement generated massive media coverage and pre-orders, signaling Tesla's ambition to become an energy company rather than just an automaker. Gigafactory construction in Nevada was ongoing throughout 2015, with vertical construction beginning in early 2015 and the building taking shape rapidly.\n\nKey milestones in 2015 included:\n* **Model X Deliveries Begin**: September 2015 — falcon-wing door SUV begins reaching customers after years of delay.\n* **~50,580 Total Deliveries**: First year of meaningful dual-model sales volume.\n* **Revenue of ~$4.05 Billion**: Continued strong growth, though net losses remained substantial.\n* **Tesla Energy Launch**: Powerwall and Powerpack home/commercial battery products unveiled in April.\n* **Gigafactory Progress**: Vertical construction underway in Nevada; Panasonic partnership deepens.	Tesla launched the Model X SUV in September 2015, featuring distinctive falcon-wing doors, and delivered a combined 50,580 vehicles across Model S and Model X. Revenue grew to approximately $4.05 billion. Elon Musk also unveiled the Powerwall and Powerpack energy storage products in April, signaling Tesla's expansion beyond automobiles into the broader energy storage market.	["https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000156459016013195/tsla-10k_20151231.htm", "https://www.macrotrends.net/stocks/charts/TSLA/tesla/revenue", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Tesla,_Inc.", "https://tridenstechnology.com/tesla-sales-statistics/", "https://www.thestreet.com/technology/history-of-tesla-15088992"]	2026-03-26 22:49:07
